INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1401,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9291393','To sim or not to sim-choosing wisely for procedural skills training in paediatrics.','Al Maawali A, Amin H, Baerg K, Feldman M, Gorodzinksy F, Puran A, Dubrowski A, Bismilla Z.','2022','bmv intraosseous maltreatment dictum otr otr otr subcomponents bmv bmv suprapubic suprapubic phlebotomy bmv procuring intraosseous maltreatment oro bmv intraosseous phlebotomy phlebotomy bmv otr otr otr otr intraosseous maltreatment pxac010_suppl_supplementary_material maawali muscat harish krista baerg saskatoon fabian gorodzinksy schulich puran dubrowski maxsimhealth oshawa zia bismilla legg e265 e271 tallentire wylde grzybowski thommasen tenore lipsky wigton vozenilek reznek ziv wolpe gaies hafler bismilla breakey swales nadel lavelle fein giardino escobedo baillargeon trainor krug toy rsf curric pusic e1313 petrusa gaies hafler hatala brydges kissoon keloth ubedulla sniffing ramped laroia progr eppich nadel lavelle fein giardino bismilla dubrowski simera hoey e49 sitzia kea duffett kho dubrowski bismilla butzin wigton lipsky ericsson s70 kurahashi moulton dubrowski pxac010_suppl_supplementary_material','Abstract Objectives Simulation is a commonly used modality to teach paediatric procedural skills, however, it is resource intensive. Which paediatric skills are best taught using simulation is not known. This study aims to examine what skills to simulate, allowing for the best use of resources in ever expanding curricula. Method We administered a questionnaire to community and hospitalist general paediatricians in Canada asking them to rate the importance of maintaining competency in each paediatric procedural skill and the frequency with which they perform the skill in their practice. Results Skills that were rated as highly important and also high frequency were: bag-mask ventilation (BMV), lumbar puncture, neonatal cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR), specimen procurement for infectious diseases, immunization, and ear curettage. Skills that were rated as highly important but low frequency were: paediatric CPR, intraosseous needle insertion, neonatal intubation, defibrillation, gathering specimens for evidence of child maltreatment, paediatric intubation, cervical spine immobilization, and oral/nasogastric tube placement. Conclusion Paediatric procedural skills are rated variably in terms of importance and frequency of use in general paediatric practice. Eight skills of high importance are infrequently performed and should be targeted for teaching via simulation. Keywords: Medical education, Procedural skills, Simulation Procedural skills have been identified as a training weakness for many medical specialties, and it is no longer acceptable to learn skills using the traditional dictum “see one, do one, teach one” ( 1–8 ). In paediatrics, many procedures are now less frequently encountered due to the decreased incidence of preventable health conditions in children and advances in medical care. This has resulted in fewer opportunities for teaching and learning paediatric procedural skills ( 9–13 ). Decreased clinical exposure and increasing concerns for patient safety have resulted in an explosion of the use of simulation for teaching skills ( 14 , 15 ). Simulation can be a constructive teaching modality when provided under the right circumstances, with careful attention to features that make it most effective ( 3 , 4 , 15–19 ). It has many advantages over traditional training methods ( 16 , 17 ). It carries no risk of medical error or harm to patients and has been shown to be superior to traditional medical education in achieving specific clinical skill acquisition goals ( 20 , 21 ). Evidence of effectiveness coupled with the current patient safety culture makes simulation the preferred method of teaching procedures in many instances. However, simulation is resource-intensive, requiring significant time, expertise, infrastructure, expensive equipment, and it is often limited in availability ( 18 ). These challenges require the use of simulation to be thoughtfully considered and implemented. In the field of paediatrics, simulation has focused primarily on resuscitation and acute care skills ( 13 , 22 , 23 ). However, the Royal College of Physicians and Surgeons of Canada (RCPSC) Paediatrics Objectives of Training (OTR) includes many other procedural skills in which residents must become competent by the completion of training. The skills included in the OTR and modalities used to teach these skills vary widely across programs ( 24 , 25 ). Little is known about which procedures are most important in practice and which should be prioritized for simulation training during paediatric residency. To address this gap, this study aims to identify skills that practicing paediatricians feel are important for practice but may not perform frequently. These important but infrequently performed skills could be ideal targets for simulation training ( 1 , 26 ). METHODS This study was conducted from 2015 to 2016 and reported using guidelines for survey research outlined in EQUATOR ( 27–30 ). Participants A descriptive cross-sectional survey was administered to 417 members of the Canadian Paediatric Society (CPS). The survey link was distributed to community and hospital paediatric section members of the CPS as a primary sampling frame to ensure the inclusion of the maximum number of paediatricians in general paediatric practice (hospital, office, or community-based) and to minimize the number of paediatricians practicing in a subspecialty area (those requiring training beyond the core 4 years of paediatric residency in Canada). No incentives were offered for survey completion. Questionnaire A two-part questionnaire modelled after a published survey on paediatric procedures was modified for Canadian context and target population based on feedback from previous studies conducted by our study group ( 9 , 26 , 31 ). Content that was specific to the USA was edited to reflect the Canadian equivalent (e.g. Pediatric Residency Review Committee’s Guidelines for procedural training were replaced with RCPSC Pediatric Specialty Committee Guidelines; questions regarding curriculum and assessment were removed; the original 10-point Likert scale was adjusted to a 100-point visual analogue scale (questionnaires can be found in Supplementary Appendix A ). The questionnaire was piloted on a group of paediatricians for clarity and modified prior to administration. The first part of the questionnaire captured demographic information of respondents, such as (i) number of years in general paediatric practice and (ii) percentage of time spent in various general paediatric practice types (general paediatrics in a tertiary care hospital, general paediatrics in a community hospital, primary care paediatric office, or consulting general paediatric office). Exclusion criteria included lack of practice in any of these general paediatric areas. The second part of the questionnaire addressed 26 procedural skills in the RCPSC Paediatric OTR. Several skills were broken down into subcomponents: ‘cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR) (neonatal and paediatric)’ was broken down into neonatal CPR, paediatric CPR and defibrillation; ‘bag-mask ventilation (BMV) and ‘tracheal intubation (neonatal and paediatric)’ was broken down into (1) BMV, (2) neonatal tracheal intubation, and (3) paediatric tracheal intubation; and ‘bladder catheterization and/or suprapubic aspiration’ was broken down into (1) bladder catheterization and (2) suprapubic aspiration. We sent four reminder emails to participants at intervals of 3 months each and collected data over 12 months. Research Ethics Board Approval was obtained from The Hospital for Sick Children. Responses Respondents were asked the following questions regarding each of the skills: How IMPORTANT is it for you to maintain competency in this skill for your practice? How FREQUENTLY do you perform this skill? Importance was defined as the value of maintaining competency in the skill for their practice. This includes a judgment of the consequences of being incompetent at the skill. Competency was defined as the ability to perform a procedure or skill independently, without supervision, and with a high likelihood of successful completion. This includes an understanding of the indications, contraindications, and risks of the procedure or skill. Respondents rated each item on a visual analogue scale from 1 to 100. We provided anchors at the beginning, middle, and end of each scale. Importance was anchored with ‘not important’, ‘important’, and ‘extremely important’; and frequency was anchored with ‘once a year’, ‘once a month’, and ‘many times a week’. Statistical analysis Incomplete surveys were removed per-protocol analysis. Descriptive analyses of questionnaire responses were performed to characterize respondent demographics, importance, and frequency of each procedure. Mean importance and frequency were calculated for each skill. RESULTS A total of 257 individuals responded to the survey (61% response rate). Representation was included from all Canadian provinces except Prince Edward Island ( Table 1 ). Table 1. Participant characteristics Characteristic Total number of respondents 257 Mean year of birth 1965 Mean years of practice (median±SD) 19 ± 12.7 Males (N, %) 69 (39%) Practice time in each setting (mean % ± SD) Tertiary care 25 ± 47 Community hospital 27 ± 30 Consulting practice 34 ± 30 Primary care practice 21 ± 31 Phlebotomy team availability (N, %) 134 (82%) Intravenous team availability (N, %) 72 (43%) Open in a new tab BMV was considered the most important procedure, while arterial/central venous catheter placement was considered the least important ( Figure 1 ). The most frequently performed procedure was procuring specimens for diagnosis of infectious diseases, and the least frequently performed procedures were defibrillation, followed closely by arterial or central venous line placement. Figure 1. Open in a new tab Importance and frequency of procedures. The scatter plot is divided into four quadrants: (i) High importance and high frequency—procedures perceived as important and frequently performed. (ii) Low importance and low frequency—procedures perceived as less important and not frequently completed. (iii) Low importance and high frequency—procedures perceived as less important but frequently completed. (iv) High importance and low frequency—procedures perceived as important but not frequently completed. DISCUSSION Results from this study indicate that the 30 skills required by the RCPSC for specialization in paediatrics are not consistently perceived as important to practice and some are used quite infrequently. Paediatricians, internists, and family physicians in the USA have also found differing degrees of perceived importance within procedural requirements between governing bodies and practicing physicians ( 4 , 32–35 ). Procedures of high frequency are likely to be encountered in clinical training. These are common procedures that paediatricians perform often during their careers; trainees should gain sufficient experience in these procedures through clinical rotations ( 1 , 26 ). These frequently performed procedures may not justify additional education resource investment in further simulation education. However, procedures of high importance but low frequency are less likely to be encountered routinely in clinical training, justifying the need to focus additional education resources on these particular experiences ( 1 ). Box 1 outlines the 8 procedural skills of high importance but low frequency that we suggest educators consider for inclusion in paediatric simulation curricula. Box 1. Recommended Skills to be Included in Paediatric Simulation Curricula (Important and Infrequent). Procedures of High Importance and Low Frequency Cardiopulmonary resuscitation (paediatric) Intraosseous insertion Tracheal intubation (neonatal) Defibrillation Gather child maltreatment evidence appropriately including documentation and specimen collection Tracheal intubation (paediatric) Cervical spine immobilization Gastric tube placement (oro or nasogastric) This includes some high acuity procedures, but also many that are less acute. Formal curricula are long-standing and ubiquitous for acute care skills, such as CPR, BMV, intubation, and intraosseous needle insertion ( 13 , 31 , 35 , 36 ). Most Canadian residency programs endorse a formal curriculum for these skills ( 26 ). Conversely, curricula for less acute or ambulatory skills are far less common and are not traditionally included in simulation curricula ( 37 ). Consideration should be given to including these lower acuity procedures in simulation curricula to ensure adequate deliberate practice to allow for the development of expertise. It is worth noting that intravenous (IV) starts and phlebotomy were not frequently performed skills; however, this may be due to the presence of dedicated IV (43%) and phlebotomy (82%) teams in many centres. Attention should be paid to the presence or absence of these teams when planning simulation training at specific institutions; those that have specialized teams will need a dedicated plan to teach residents these skills. Despite ample opportunities for clinical exposure, several procedures (e.g. BMV, neonatal CPR) are commonly included in simulation curricula ( 26 ). Simulation has many advantages over more traditional methods, such as increased repetitive practice, reflective learning, standardized exposure, tailored difficulty, and an increased range of cases ( 16 , 17 ). Simulating procedures where error carries a significant risk of harm to patients (i.e. to ensure a minimal level of safety prior to performing them on a patient) is reasonable. However, these high-frequency procedures may be integrated into and augmented with clinical opportunities when simulation resources are limited, with a higher reliance on clinical exposure to establish expertise. Limitations and future directions This study had several limitations. Skills evaluated were derived from the RCPSC Paediatric OTR, however, other skills may be important and may have been missed. Second, responses were perceptions rather than objective measures of frequency or importance. Third, responses represent a snapshot of practicing paediatricians across practice types; training occurs primarily in tertiary care centres, however, meaning that clinical exposure in our study population may not be representative of what is encountered in training. The best skills to teach may also differ from program to program depending on the nature of the clinical environment and available simulation expertise. Fourth, questions regarding specific areas of practice (e.g. neonatal intensive care units) were not asked, limiting interpretation of practice-specific procedures (e.g. ventral venous catheter placement in neonatal intensive care units). Fifth, there were limited early career paediatrician in our study, which may affect the evolving frequency and perceived importance of procedures practiced. Finally, the Competency By Design (CBD) framework has been introduced in the years since this questionnaire was distributed. Despite having competencies and entrustable professional activities (EPAs), the Pediatric OTR still exists in the CBD framework. The importance and frequency of the procedures outlined the OTR translate into the current EPAs and are still relevant. Experts have strongly encouraged simulation in paediatric training, while recognizing that the best approach to integrate simulation-based learning activities into existing training curricula requires further study ( 36 ). Our work can be used by educators to integrate simulation into their curricula in a thoughtful way, examining their own environment, clinical opportunities, and resources. Simulation has become so ubiquitous, that one author wrote “see one, do one, teach one” will soon become “see one, simulate many, do one competently, and teach everyone” ( 6 ). We suggest rather “see one, simulate wisely, and teach everyone appropriately”. CONCLUSION Procedural skills in the RCPSC Paediatrics OTR are rated variably by paediatricians in terms of importance and frequency of use in general paediatric practice. Eight skills are rated as high importance but infrequently performed: paediatric CPR, intraosseous needle insertion, neonatal intubation, defibrillation, gathering child maltreatment evidence, paediatric intubation, cervical spine immobilization, and gastric tube placement. These skills should be targeted for teaching via simulation. Supplementary Material pxac010_suppl_Supplementary_Material Click here for additional data file. (194.3KB, pdf) Funding: This work was supported by the Pediatric Consultants Education Research Grant, Hospital for Sick Children. Potential Conflicts of Interest: K.B. reports that she received royalties from Brush Education and she served on the executive committee for the Community Paediatrics Section of the Canadian Paediatric Society. All other authors declare no conflict of interest. Contributor Information Ali Al Maawali, Department of Paediatrics, The Hospital for Sick Children, University of Toronto, Toronto, Ontario, Canada; The Royal Hospital, Child Health Department, Muscat, Oman. Harish Amin, Department of Pediatrics, Alberta Health Services, University of Calgary, Calgary, Alberta, Canada. Krista Baerg, Department of Paediatrics, University of Saskatchewan, Saskatoon, Saskatchewan, Canada. Mark Feldman, Department of Paediatrics, The Hospital for Sick Children, University of Toronto, Toronto, Ontario, Canada. Fabian Gorodzinksy, Department of Pediatrics, Schulich School of Medicine and Dentistry, Western University, London, Ontario, Canada. Allan Puran, Department of Paediatrics, The Hospital for Sick Children, University of Toronto, Toronto, Ontario, Canada. Adam Dubrowski, maxSIMhealth, Faculty of Health Sciences, Ontario Tech University, Oshawa, Ontario, Canada. Zia Bismilla, Department of Paediatrics, The Hospital for Sick Children, University of Toronto, Toronto, Ontario, Canada. REFERENCES 1. White J, Rowan-Legg A, Writer H, et al. Procedural skill need for Canadian paediatricians: A national profile. Paediatric Child Health 2021;26(6):e265–e271. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Tallentire VR, Smith SE, Wylde K, Cameron HS. Are medical graduates ready to face the challenges of Foundation training? Postgrad Med J 2011;87:590–5. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. van der Goes T, Grzybowski SC, Thommasen H. Procedural skills training. Canadian family practice residency programs. Can Fam Physician 1999;45:78–85. [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Tenore JL, Sharp LK, Lipsky MS. A national survey of procedural skill requirements in family practice residency programs. Fam Med 2001;33:28–38. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Wigton RS, Blank LL, Nicolas JA, Tape TG. Procedural skills training in internal medicine residencies. A survey of program directors. Ann Intern Med 1989;111:932–8. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Vozenilek J, Huff JS, Reznek M, Gordon JA. See one, do one, teach one: Advanced technology in medical education. Acad Emerg Med 2004;11:1149–54. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Mason WT, Strike PW. See one, do one, teach one—Is this still how it works? A comparison of the medical and nursing professions in the teaching of practical procedures. Med Teach 2003;25:664–6. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Ziv A, Wolpe PR, Small SD, et al. Simulation-based medical education: An ethical imperative. Acad Med 2003;78:783–8. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Gaies MG, Landrigan CP, Hafler JP, et al. Assessing procedural skills training in pediatric residency programs. Pediatrics 2007;120:715–22. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar