INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1435,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8491075','The current state of general paediatric fellowships in Canada.','Al Maawali A, Puran A, Schwartz S, Johnstone J, Bismilla Z.','2021','gpfs nominate gpfs maltreatment perinatology gpfs realms gpfs predecessors gpfs gpfs allotments vetere gpfs vetere nonreporting pxaa136_suppl_supplementary_appendix_1 pxaa136_suppl_supplementary_appendix_2 pxaa136_suppl_supplementary_appendix_3 rhim maniscalco rassbach quotas hatala sawatsky dudek iter jerardi rassbach maniscalco blankenburg e20170698 percelay phmfellows gidwani harary muret wagstaff ambul librizzi winer banach elizov kassam vetere ottolini stucky pris 360a 360a oshimura maniscalco pxaa136_suppl_supplementary_appendix_1 pxaa136_suppl_supplementary_appendix_2 pxaa136_suppl_supplementary_appendix_3','Abstract Introduction The field of Paediatric Medicine has grown tremendously over the last two decades. Several niche areas of practice have emerged, and opportunities for focused training in these areas have grown in parallel. The landscape of ‘General Paediatric Fellowship’ (GPF) Programs in Canada is not well described; this knowledge is needed to promote standardization and high-quality training across Canada. This study explores the structure and components of existing GPFs in Canada and identifies the interest and barriers to providing such programs. Methods A questionnaire was created to explore the landscape of GPF Programs in Canada. Invitations to participate were sent to leaders of General Paediatric Divisions across Canada, with a request to forward the survey to the most appropriate individual to respond within their local context. Results A total of 19 responses (95%) representing 17 different Canadian universities were obtained. Eight universities offered a total of 13 GPF Programs in 2019, with one additional university planning to start a program in the coming year. Existing programs were variable in size, structure and curriculum. Most programs identified as Academic Paediatric Programs, with an overlap in content and structure between Academic Paediatrics and Paediatric Hospital Medicine programs. The majority of respondents felt there was a need for GPF Programs in Canada but cited funding as the most common perceived barrier. Conclusion A growing number of GPF Programs exist in Canada. Current fellowship programs are variable in structure and content. Collaboration between programs is required to advance GPF training in Canada. Keywords: Education, Fellowships and Scholarships, Hospital Medicine, Hospitals, Hospitalists/education, Medical, Paediatric Career paths for those trained as general paediatricians have evolved over the last decade. Upon completion of the Royal College of Physicians and Surgeons of Canada (RCPSC) paediatric certifying exam, a paediatrician may begin practicing in a variety of settings including primary care paediatrics, community paediatrics, hospital paediatrics, academic paediatrics, and others. Traditionally, paediatricians practicing in these areas have the equivalent of 4 years of RCPSC-accredited paediatric residency training. However, as niche practices within general paediatrics have developed, opportunities for specialized training in these areas have appeared. Traditionally, ‘Generalist’ Paediatric Sections or Divisions may offer fellowships to advance expertise in more focused areas within General Paediatrics. The first such ‘generalist’ paediatric fellowship in North America was established at the University of Toronto in 1992; since then, there has been a significant increase in the number of available ‘General Paediatric Fellowship’ (GPF) Programs ( 1 ). To our knowledge, the current landscape of GPF Programs in Canada has not been explored. This knowledge is essential for collaboration among centres, standardization of training, and to guide discussion around the development of formal ‘Areas of Focused Competence’ within niche areas of General Paediatrics in Canada. The objective of this study was to describe the current opportunities for GPF training in Canada. Our secondary objective was to identify current interest in and barriers to establishing additional GPF programs in Canada. METHODS Participants A descriptive cross-sectional study of the 17 Departments of Paediatrics within Canadian medical schools, as identified by the Pediatrics Chairs of Canada, was conducted. Leaders (Chairs, Chiefs, or Heads) of Generalist Paediatric Departments, Divisions, Sections, or equivalent from each centre were recruited for the study. Questionnaire A questionnaire modeled after similar studies of Paediatric Hospital Medicine (PHM) programs from the USA was created ( 2 ). The application of questions to the Canadian landscape of GPF Programs was established. Questions relating specifically to the US context were removed or edited to reflect differences in duration of training, training frameworks, and health care systems. Additional questions were generated from the authors’ knowledge of existing Canadian GPF Programs, local experience in fellowship training, and a review of the literature. The questionnaire underwent cognitive review and pilot testing by three members of the Division of Pediatric Medicine at the University of Toronto and was refined through an iterative process, resulting in a total of 27 open- and closed-ended questions. REDCap software was used to create, distribute, and collect responses to the questionnaire. Data collection An invitation to participate in the study was sent via e-mail in December 2019 to 20 General Paediatric Department/Division/Section Leaders in each of the Departments of Paediatrics within the 17 Canadian medical schools ( 3 ). Participants were asked to complete the questionnaire or nominate the most appropriate individual in their setting to complete it. An electronic consent form was provided, e-mail reminders were sent at the 2, 4, and 6-week marks and follow-up phone calls were conducted to clarify ambiguous data. This study was approved by the Research Ethics Board at the Hospital for Sick Children. RESULTS We received a total of 19 responses (response rate 95%) representing all 17 universities (13 Department/Division/Section leaders and 6 nominated Fellowship Program Directors). General paediatric departments, divisions, and sections Respondent demographics are shown in Supplementary Appendix 2 . In total, 512 paediatricians worked in these centres and more than half (67%) held a full-time position within the institution. The number of paediatricians in each Division or equivalent varied considerably and ranged from 8 to 80. The number of fellowship-trained paediatricians varied at each centre from 0% to 40%; 119 paediatricians (23%) working at these centres had completed fellowship training. Interest in general paediatric fellowship programs in Canada Eighty-four per cent of respondents felt a need for GPF Programs in Canada. The most common reason cited by 50% of respondents was the opportunity for enhanced training in nonclinical and included academic skills, such as medical education, leadership, research, and quality improvement. Other reasons reported include the increased complexity and acuity of patients in hospital settings (31%), the recognition of PHM as a distinct sub-specialty (31%), the need for a fellowship program as a job requirement in academic institutions (31%), and the need to strengthen or consolidate clinical knowledge (11%). Barriers to general paediatric fellowship programs in Canada Many barriers toward establishing GPFs were identified. The most common barrier reported was lack of funding (74%). Other barriers included inadequate number of staff/educators for fellowship programs (47%), no perceived need for a fellowship program (42%), lack of faculty interest (26%), lack of candidate interest (11%), limited clinical experience with limited clinical teams (11%), and concerns for job opportunities following graduation (11%). General paediatric fellowship programs in Canada A total of 8 Canadian universities offered one or more GPF, and one additional university was planning to start a fellowship program in the next year. A total of 13 GPF Programs are currently available in Canada ( Supplementary Appendix 3 ): Paediatrics (1), Academic Paediatrics (6), PHM (2), Complex Care (2), Social Paediatrics (1), and Community Paediatrics (1). The oldest program started at the University of Toronto in 1992, while the most recent programs were introduced at the University of Calgary and the University of Alberta in 2018. Two PHM programs are soon expected to be implemented—one at The University of British Columbia, and one at McGill University. Three universities do not offer fellowship programs regularly, rather on an on-demand basis. Multiple universities offered one type of fellowship, while three offered multiple different programs. Of the 12 programs with available data, 4 programs are 1 year in duration, 5 are 2 years, and 3 are variable (1 to 2 years). There are currently 10 to 26 fellowship positions available per year across all programs. Funding Funding sources for fellowship programs differed across institutions and were typically drawn from more than one source. The most common sources of funding include the fellow’s home government (50%), followed by the fellow’s home institution, division or department (37.5%). Ministry of Health and grant funds (each 25%), fellowship awards, hospital charity fellowships, and fellow self-funding (each 12.5%) were other common sources. Component and structures of fellowships Major components of fellowships include inpatient ward, outpatient clinic, academic, and elective time; the amount of time dedicated to each varies ( Figure 1 ). Rotations on general paediatric inpatient wards are mandatory in most fellowships. Other common mandatory rotations include continuity or longitudinal clinics, night float, complex care, emergency medicine, infectious diseases, intensive care, palliative care, and child maltreatment. The single community paediatric fellowship requires clinical rotations distinct from all other fellowships (perinatology, transport team, child development, and mental health). Figure 1. Open in a new tab Components of Canadian General Pediatric Fellowship Programs, 2019. Formal academic and training opportunities Existing fellowships included opportunities for formal academic education and training ( Supplementary Appendix 1 ). 62.5% of respondents indicated that their institution’s program offered opportunities to pursue graduate-level training in Public Health, Education, Epidemiology, and Bioethics or their equivalents. Fifty per cent offered graduate-level training in Leadership programs or equivalent, and 37.5% offered graduate studies in Quality Improvement (QI) or equivalents. Other options reported by 25% of respondents included Certificates or other similar programs in Teaching, Research, and QI. Current methods of assessment All programs reported using In-Training Assessment/Evaluation Reports as the main method of assessment. Other methods include assessment of Entrustable Professional Activities (50%), encounter cards (25%), multisource/360° feedback (25%), Objective Structured Clinical Exams (12.5%), and Portfolios (25%). Seventy-five per cent of respondents indicated that they plan to transition their method of assessment to a competency-based medical education model in the next few years. Employment after fellowship Respondents from universities with GPFs (N=8) indicated that their fellows found employment in a variety of areas after graduation. Most respondents (6 of 8) indicated that their fellows find employment in academic institution in Canada; 5 of 8 indicated their fellows find employment in their home institutions; 4 of 8 in academic institution outside of Canada; 4 of 8 in community hospitals in Canada; and 1 of 8 in community hospitals outside Canada. To our knowledge, this is the first study that has explored the landscape of GPF Programs in Canada. Our study suggests there is considerable interest in GPF Programs in Canada, with 84% of those surveyed believing these programs are needed. This is a notable change from 2001 where only 30% of paediatric chairs in North America believed that additional training was required beyond what was provided in residency ( 4 ). This change may reflect the evolution of career paths and further sub-specialization of general paediatricians over the last two decades as well as their changing role in both clinical and scholarly realms ( 5 ). Academic Paediatrics was the most common GPF offered, with PHM fellowships being the fastest growing in number. There has been tremendous growth in PHM over the past two decades resulting in an evolution of the hospitalist model of care in paediatrics ( 5 , 6 ). In the USA, PHM training programs have increased exponentially, from three programs in 2003 to over 59 in 2020, with significant growth expected to continue ( 2 , 7 ). Programs in the USA may now be accredited and most follow a standard curriculum and structure. The recognition of PHM as a distinct board-certified subspecialty in the USA by the American Board of Pediatrics supports the notion that the skills required to practice in this area go beyond those acquired in core paediatric residency training. Enhanced training in the scholarly areas of medical education, research, QI, and leadership were listed as the main reasons for GPF Programs. This additional training aligns with the growing skills required of paediatric hospitalists, including coordination of care, leadership, research, education, QI, and patient safety ( 8 ). GPFs offer generalists an opportunity to gain additional scholarly training in these areas within a tailored clinical environment while providing career coaching and mentorship from those already practicing in the field; those emerging from these fellowships may feel better prepared for the work they will encounter than their predecessors ( 9 ). While most respondents felt GPFs were worthwhile, many did not have one at their institution. One notable barrier was the perceived lack of need for a fellowship program. Many graduates, however, obtained positions at academic centres, suggesting that academic centres are seeking paediatricians with fellowship training, making applicants with this training more desirable. An overwhelming barrier toward establishing a fellowship program was funding; existing programs secure funding from several different sources, usually relying on multiple sources. The Ministry of Health funds positions in training programs accredited by the RCPSC; however, there are no accredited GPF Programs in Canada, and so no guaranteed funding. The Canadian General Internal Medicine (GIM) subspecialty journey exemplifies the benefits of recognizing a field as a distinct discipline with dedicated fellowship training ( 10 ). GIM was recognized by the RCPSC as a distinct subspecialty in 2010, resulting in tremendous growth in interest, skill, and scholarship within the field ( 10 ). It is now the most popular choice among all internal medicine subspecialty applicants, with current interest far exceeding capacity ( 10 ). Currently, 16 GIM training programs in Canada exist, with 97 funded positions in 2019. General paediatrics (particularly PHM) may progress similarly. As the field grows, the potential for recognition of distinct ‘Areas of Focused Competence’ becomes possible; accreditation of programs by the RCPSC could follow, with subsequent funding for fellowship positions moving forward. Among the 8 Canadian universities offering GPFs, a total of 13 Programs were identified, with some similarities and variations among each program related to the required rotations, inpatient and outpatient components, and academic time allotments. Similarly named programs were not necessarily similar in structure and content; Academic Paediatric Fellowships were no more likely to be similar to each other than they were to be similar to PHM Fellowship Programs. This variability can be attributed to the flexible nature of these fellowships, with many programs tailoring training experiences to the specific learning goals of each trainee and the needs of the institution. While this has many advantages, it makes defining each training path more difficult and creates ambiguity regarding the specific skills that define the ‘Academic Paediatrician’, ‘Paediatric Hospitalist’ or other types of General Paediatricians. Defining the required components of training is essential in the formulation of distinct career paths defined by specific competencies and expertise. Inpatient general paediatrics was a mandatory rotation across all programs, with most also encouraging rotations in complex care, continuity or longitudinal clinics. A study by Vetere and Cooke (2019) showed that a majority of graduating paediatric residents felt unprepared to manage care related to the complex care population, supporting the need for additional training in complex care ( 11 ). Most programs also offered formal teaching in research, QI, leadership, and education, with up to 50% offering the chance to pursue graduate degrees in these fields in which emerging trainees have historically felt unprepared ( 12–14 ). Our study found that fellows who finished GPFs were generally employed by academic centres (78%); similarly, others have found that graduates of generalist fellowships pursued careers with diverse leadership and professional responsibilities, most often in academic environments (96%). Vetere and Cooke showed that a majority of graduating paediatric residents as well as staff who had recently entered PHM felt ill-prepared to pursue key academic initiatives including quality improvement and research in the paediatric population; this speaks to the value of the additional academic training provided by GPF Programs ( 11 ). Future directions The landscape of GPF training in Canada is diverse resulting in disparate training across the country. Prospective GPF trainees should ensure the program they select aligns with their career goals. While many institutions are interested in developing fellowship programs that meet the needs of training paediatricians in niche areas of generalist practice, increased collaboration, and standardization across programs will benefit this evolving field. Programs must work together to define training requirements and core competencies in these focused areas and ensure a workforce that is well prepared for the future. Lessons learned from the successful standardization of training and recognition of PHM as a distinct sub-speciality in the USA may be of benefit as we strive to achieve similar goals in Canada. Limitations This study included feedback from leaders in general paediatrics, however, all were situated at large tertiary care hospitals. The opinions of paediatricians practicing at smaller hospitals or outside the academic system may be underrepresented. Despite an extremely high response rate, the survey methodology used in this study is prone to content error, response bias, and nonreporting bias; it is possible that programs were missed or that details about existing programs are inaccurate. There are likely nuances to each institution that are not captured in our responses. CONCLUSION A growing number of GPF Programs exist in Canada. Current fellowship programs are variable in structure and content; collaboration between programs is required to advance GPF training in Canada. Additional ‘Areas of Focused Competence’ within paediatrics should be considered to improve the training of future paediatricians in Canada. Supplementary Material pxaa136_suppl_Supplementary_Appendix_1 Click here for additional data file. (55.3KB, docx) pxaa136_suppl_Supplementary_Appendix_2 Click here for additional data file. (47.1KB, docx) pxaa136_suppl_Supplementary_Appendix_3 Click here for additional data file. (47.2KB, docx) DISCUSSION Funding: There are no funders to report for this submission. Potential Conflicts of Interest: All authors: No reported conflicts of interest. All authors have submitted the ICMJE Form for Disclosure of Potential Conflicts of Interest. Conflicts that the editors consider relevant to the content of the manuscript have been disclosed. REFERENCES 1. Freed GL, Dunham KM; Research Advisory Committee of the American Board of Pediatrics . Characteristics of pediatric hospital medicine fellowships and training programs. J Hosp Med 2009;4(3):157–63. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Shah NH, Rhim HJ, Maniscalco J, Wilson K, Rassbach C. The current state of pediatric hospital medicine fellowships: A survey of program directors. J Hosp Med 2016;11(5):324–8. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Canadian Resident Matching Services (CaRMS). 2018 medicine subspecialty match-first iteration. Table 2: Number of matched applications by first choice discipline and quotas. < https:\\www.carms.ca/data-reports/msm-data-reports/ >. (2018) (Accessed 23 September 2020). 4. Hatala R, Sawatsky AP, Dudek N, Ginsburg S, Cook DA. Using in-training evaluation report (ITER) qualitative comments to assess medical students and residents: A systematic review. Acad Med 2017;92(6):868–79. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Jerardi KE, Fisher E, Rassbach C, Maniscalco J, Blankenburg R, Chase L, Shah N. Development of a curricular framework for pediatric hospital medicine fellowships. Pediatrics 2017;140(1):e20170698. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Percelay JM, Strong GB; American Academy of Pediatrics Section on Hospital Medicine . Guiding principles for pediatric hospitalist programs. Pediatrics 2005;115(4):1101–2. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Pediatric Fellowship Directors. PHM fellowship programs. Pediatric hospital medicine fellows. < https:\\www.phmfellows.org/phm-programs/ >. [n.d] (Accessed 23 September 2020). 8. Srivastava R, Landrigan C, Gidwani P, Harary OH, Muret-Wagstaff S, Homer CJ. Pediatric hospitalists in Canada and the United States: A survey of pediatric academic department chairs. Ambul Pediatr 2001;1(6):338–9. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Librizzi J, Winer JC, Banach L, Davis A. Perceived core competency achievements of fellowship and non-fellowship-trained early career pediatric hospitalists. J Hosp Med 2015;10(6):373–9. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Card SE, Clark HD, Elizov M, Kassam N. The evolution of General Internal Medicine (GIM)in Canada: International implications. J Gen Intern Med 2017;32(5):576–81. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Vetere P, Cooke S. Preparedness to practice paediatric hospital medicine. Paediatr Child Health 2020;25(7):447–54. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. Ottolini MC, Landrigan C, Chiang VW, Stucky E. PRIS survey: Pediatric hospitalist (PH) roles and training needs. Pediatr Res 2004;55(4):360A–360A. [ Google Scholar ] 13. Oshimura JM, Bauer BD, Shah N, Nguyen E, Maniscalco J. Current roles and perceived needs of pediatric hospital medicine fellowship graduates. Hosp Pediatr 2016;6(10):633–7. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 14. Freed GL, Dunham KM; Research Advisory Committee of the American Board of Pediatrics . Pediatric hospitalists: Training, current practice, and career goals. J Hosp Med 2009;4(3):179–86. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials pxaa136_suppl_Supplementary_Appendix_1 Click here for additional data file. (55.3KB, docx) pxaa136_suppl_Supplementary_Appendix_2 Click here for additional data file. (47.1KB, docx) pxaa136_suppl_Supplementary_Appendix_3 Click here for additional data file. (47.2KB, docx)',3,'2025-10-10 08:28:29','2025-10-10 0