INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1438,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9191918','Assessing nephrology competence in general paediatrics-A survey of general paediatricians, paediatric nephrologists, residents, and program directors.','Kirpalani A, Prasad C, Jawa NA, Atkinson AR, Feldman M, Jeffers JM, Noone DG.','2021','sidak respirology enuresis neph enuresis neph sidak unimportance enuresis underdiagnosis pxab089_suppl_supplementary_figure_s1 pxab089_suppl_supplementary_table_s1 pxab089_suppl_supplementary_table_s2 pxab089_suppl_supplementary_table_s3 pxab089_suppl_supplementary_table_s4 mehp thankful amrit kirpalani schulich charushree natasha jawa adelle justin jeffers damien noone reidy norwood mahan iglesia doja e131 rcsite pediatrics_otr_e benisty hessey coca hessey perreault dorais zappitelli hessey dorais mammen skippen hessey s41390 bortolussi reaffirmation e20163026 e20163026 bortolussi enuresis kaelber nasir vandermeer kaelber underdiagnosis underappreciated blufpand kaspers bokenkamp pxab089_suppl_supplementary_figure_s1 pxab089_suppl_supplementary_table_s1 pxab089_suppl_supplementary_table_s2 pxab089_suppl_supplementary_table_s3 pxab089_suppl_supplementary_table_s4','Abstract Objective The objective of this study was to identify nephrology topics of lowest perceived competency and importance for general paediatricians. Methods Surveys were distributed to general paediatricians, paediatric residents, paediatric residency program directors, and paediatric nephrologists. Perceived importance and competence were rated on a 5-point Likert scale. Means and 95% confidence intervals were calculated. Results Mean perceived competency from general paediatricians across all nephrology domains was 3.0, 95%CI (2.9 to 3.1) and mean importance was 3.2, 95%CI (3.1 to 3.3). Domains scoring below the means for competence and importance, respectively were kidney stones (2.5, 95%CI [2.2 to 2.7]) and 2.6, 95%CI [2.3 to 2.8]), acute kidney injury (2.5, 95%CI [2.2 to 2.8] and 2.4, 95%CI [2.1 to 2.8]), chronic kidney disease (1.9, 95%CI [1.7 to 2.2] and 2.1, 95%CI [1.8 to 2.4]), tubular disorders (1.8, 95%CI [1.6 to 2.0] and 2.0, 95%CI [1.8 to 2.3]), and kidney transplant (1.6, 95%CI [1.4 to 1.8] and 1.7, 95%CI [1.4 to 1.9]). Residents, program directors, and paediatric nephrologists agreed that stones, chronic kidney disease, tubular disorders, and transplant were of lower importance. However, acute kidney injury was the domain with the largest discrepancy in perceived importance between residents (4.4, 95%CI [4.2 to 4.6]), nephrologists (4.2, 95%CI [3.8 to 4.6]), and program directors (4.2, 95%CI [3.7 to 4.7]) compared to general paediatricians ([2.4, 95%CI [2.1 to 2.8]; P<0.05). Conclusion Paediatricians did not believe acute kidney injury was important to their practice, despite expert opinion and evidence of long-term consequences. Educational interventions must address deficits in crucial domains of renal health in paediatrics. Keywords: Acute kidney injury, Continuing medical education, Medical education, Paediatric nephrology, Postgraduate medical education, Survey research General paediatricians and paediatric trainees are often the first physicians to diagnose and manage children presenting with kidney disease ( 1 ). They are expected to complete initial investigations, provide immediate management, and determine the urgency of referral for specialist follow-up, if required. It is therefore critical to ensure that these practitioners are competent in the core areas of nephrology. Despite this understanding, no study to date has explored paediatricians’ perceived competence within these domains in order to identify knowledge gaps that should be addressed in postgraduate (PG) and Continuing Medical Education curriculum development. Paediatric studies have revealed that while trainees find nephrology to be challenging ( 2 ), they also cite that it is one of the most important subject areas within their training ( 3 ). The difficulty of understanding complex renal physiology is reported as a barrier to undertaking further advanced training in nephrology ( 1 ). A 2011 survey of recent graduates from Canadian paediatric residency programs identified ‘hypertension’ and ‘acute and chronic renal failure’ as areas of low perceived competence ( 4 ). However, topics were grouped into higher order categories and not explored independently, most nephrology domains were not assessed, and no other stakeholders were included ( 4 ). In order to improve training in this area, it is critical to understand the perceptions of individual nephrology topics among a diverse group of practitioners. This study was the first of a multi-phase initiative with the primary objective of identifying areas of lowest perceived competency within nephrology for general paediatricians. Our secondary objectives were to identify domains of lowest perceived competency among paediatric residents, and to determine perceived importance of nephrology topics for general paediatrics practice according to multiple key stakeholder groups: paediatricians, paediatric residents, residency program directors (PDs), and paediatric nephrologists. METHODS We followed an established survey design described by Amin et al. in their national survey of recent paediatric training graduates ( 4 ). This tool had previously been developed through a modified Delphi method ( 4 ), and assessed for face validity with community and subspecialty paediatricians ( 4 ). While those authors based the data collection tool on 150 commonly encountered Royal College of Physicians and Surgeons of Canada (RCPSC) objectives of training across all of paediatrics, we adapted the survey to exclusively include those objectives related to nephrology as seen in Supplementary Table S1 . These were obtained from most recent RCPSC published objectives, which were created in 2008 ( 5 ). Each RCPSC objective consisted of a specific clinical problem, population, or disease category. The list of selected objectives was reviewed for content validity by nephrologists and paediatric residents at the lead study site. The final survey asked participants to rate self-reported competence and perceived importance of each objective on a 5-point Likert scale, consistent with prior literature ( 4 ). There was also an open-ended portion of the survey to allow participants to identify additional areas of priority within nephrology. General paediatricians, paediatric residents, paediatric residency program directors, and paediatric nephrologists were included in the study. Paediatricians and paediatric residents were asked to rate the nephrology objectives referenced above for self-reported competence and perceived importance. Paediatric PDs and nephrologists were asked to rate the above objectives only for perceived importance to general paediatricians. Demographics were collected in all groups. Distribution All surveys were distributed through an anonymous web-based Research Electronic Data Capture (REDCap) ( 6 , 7 ) survey. Surveys to general paediatricians were distributed to the Paediatricians of Ontario email list to be inclusive of urban- and rural-based practitioners in both academic and community practice, with a reminder email sent 6 months later. Residents from postgraduate years 1 to 4 at three universities (University of Toronto, Western University, and McMaster University) received the survey via email, with a reminder email sent 30 to 45 days later. Program directors across Canada received the survey via a single e-mail to the Paediatric Program Directors of Canada e-mail list. Paediatric Nephrologists across Canada received the survey via a single e-mail from the Canadian Association of Paediatric Nephrologists email list. Statistical analysis Likert scores were analyzed using Student’s t - test. Mean scores and 95% CIs for each objective, and overall means for perceived importance and competence, respectively, were calculated. The Šídák test was used to assess for significance in comparisons between multiple groups, under the assumption that each group’s response was independent of the others’ responses. The primary outcome was identification of domains in which the mean score for perceived competence reported by general paediatricians was below the overall mean competence score for all domains. Knowledge gap scores were used by Amin et al. ( 4 ) and we adapted this to the present study where knowledge gap scores for paediatricians and residents were measured as the difference between perceived importance and competence ( 4 ). The gap score (perceived importance – competence) was considered to be significant if it was significantly greater than 0 ( 4 ). A high gap score indicates that an individual’s competence with a topic is inadequate based on how important that topic is felt to be for their practice. We also calculated the differences between the mean scores in perceived importance of each nephrology topic across all of the groups. This study received approval from the Research Ethics Boards of the Hospital for Sick Children (Toronto), Western University, and McMaster University. RESULTS Survey of general paediatricians The survey was sent to 300 general paediatricians, of which 60 responded (20%). Characteristics of the respondents are shown in Table 1 . Table 1. Characteristics of respondents from general paediatrician survey Respondent characteristics Total n (%) of 60 Practice Setting - Academic Center 7 (12%) - Community Practice 53 (88%) Care Type - Inpatient 9 (15%) - Outpatient 36 (60%) - Both 15 (25%) Residency Training Location - Canada 38 (63%) - International 14 (23%) - Both 8 (13%) Subspecialty Training After Residency - Yes 31 (52%) Academic Paediatrics 2 Clinical Nutrition 2 Community Paediatrics 3 Emergency Medicine 3 Endocrinology 2 Hematology-Oncology 3 Neonatology 9 Nephrology 1 Respirology 1 Rheumatology 2 - No 29 (48%) Years in Practice (independent) - <5 16 (27%) - 5–10 5 (8%) - >10 39 (65%) Completed Nephrology Rotation in Residency? - Yes 46 (77%) - No 13 (27%) - Don’t Recall 1 (2%) Open in a new tab The mean self-reported competency score on the 5-point Likert scale across all objectives was 3.0, 95%CI (2.9 to 3.1) ( Table 2 ). The five objectives with mean scores significantly below this were the following: kidney transplantation (1.6, 95%CI [1.4 to 1.8]), tubular disorders (1.8, 95%CI [1.6 to 2.0]), chronic kidney disease (CKD) (1.9, 95%CI [1.7 to 2.2]), kidney stones (2.5, 95%CI [2.2 to 2.7]), and acute kidney injury (AKI) (2.5, 95%CI [2.2 to 2.8]; Figure 1 ). Table 2. Perceived competence in nephrology objectives of training Perceived Competence Score [Mean (95%CI)] Nephrology objective of training General paediatricians P (compared to mean) Program residents P (compared to mean) Overall Mean 3.0 (2.9–3.1) 2.6 (2.5–2.7) Hematuria 3.1 (2.9–3.4) 0.37 2.7 (2.5–2.9) 0.60 Proteinuria 2.1 (2.9–3.4) 0.37 3.1 (2.8–3.3) 0.002 AKI 2.5 (2.2–2.8) 0.006* 3.0 (2.8–3.3) 0.003 Fluids and Electrolytes 3.0 (2.7–3.3) 0.79 2.8 (2.6–3.1) 0.14 CAKUT 3.3 (3.0–3.5) 0.05 2.1 (1.9–2.4) <0.001* Hydronephrosis 3.7 (3.4–4.0) <0.001 2.8 (2.6–3.0) 0.23 Hypertension 2.9 (2.6–3.1) 0.57 3.0 (2.7–3.2) 0.02 Stones 2.5 (2.2–2.7) 0.002* 2.7 (2.5–2.9) 0.60 Tubular Disorders 1.8 (1.6–2.0) <0.001* 1.7 (1.5–1.9) <0.001* Enuresis 4.2 (3.9–4.5) <0.001 3.1(2.9–3.4) <0.001 UTI 4.3 (4.1–4.5) <0.001 3.8 (3.5–4.0) <0.001 Renal Imaging 3.5 (3.3–3.8) <0.001 2.7 (2.4–3.0) 0.69 Renal Masses 3.2 (2.9–3.4) 0.27 2.4 (2.1–3.0) 0.10 Transplant 1.6 (1.4–1.8) <0.001* 1.4 (1.2–1.6) <0.001* CKD 1.9 (1.7–2.2) <0.001* 2.0 (1.8–2.3) <0.001* Open in a new tab AKI Acute kidney injury; CAKUT Congenital anomalies of the kidney and urinary tract; CKD Chronic kidney disease; UTI Urinary tract infection. *Denotes objectives significantly below the overall mean perceived competence score. Figure 1. Open in a new tab General paediatricians’ self-reported competence in relation to overall mean competence. AKI Acute kidney injury; CAKUT Congenital anomalies of the kidney and urinary tract; CKD Chronic kidney disease; UTI Urinary tract infections . Dotted red line indicates mean self-reported competency score across all objectives. The mean perceived importance score across all objectives on the paediatrician survey was 3.2, 95%CI (3.1 to 3.3). The five objectives with mean scores significantly below this were: kidney transplantation (1.7, 95%CI [1.4 to 1.9]), tubular disorders (2.0, 95%CI [1.8 to 2.3]), CKD (2.1, 95%CI [1.8 to 2.4]), AKI (2.4, 95%CI [2.1 to 2.8]), and kidney stones (2.6, 95%CI [2.3 to 2.8]; Figure 2 ). Figure 2. Open in a new tab General paediatricians’ perceived importance in relation to overall mean competence. AKI Acute kidney injury; CAKUT Congenital anomalies of the kidney and urinary tract; CKD Chronic kidney disease; UTI Urinary tract infections . Dotted red line indicates mean perceived importance score across all objectives. The domains with a gap score significantly greater than 0 were hypertension, renal imaging, hematuria, hydronephrosis, and congenital anomalies of the kidneys and urinary tract as shown in Supplementary Figure S1 . Surveys of paediatric residents There were 59 responses to the resident survey from 159 recipients (37%). Characteristics of resident respondents are shown in Supplementary Table S2 , and mean scores of self-reported competency and perceived importance are shown in Tables 2 and 3 , respectively. Table 3. Perceived importance in nephrology objectives of training and gaps in importance between groups Perceived Importance Score [Mean (95%CI)] Gap in Importance Nephrology objective of training General paediatricians Paediatric nephrologists Program directors Paediatric residents Neph-GP P PD-GP P Res-GP P Hematuria 3.4 (3.2–3.8) 4.4 (4.0–4.7) 4.4 (4.0–4.8) 4.2 (3.9–4.4) 0.93 0.01 0.99 0.18 0.7 0.001 Proteinuria 3.6 (3.3–3.9) 4.7 (4.5–5.0) 4.4 (4.0–4.8) 4.2 (3.9–4.5) 1.14 <0.001 0.88 0.41 0.60 0.004 AKI 2.4 (2.1–2.8) 4.2 (3.8–4.6) 4.2 (3.7–4.7) 4.4 (4.2–4.6) 1.75 <0.001 1.78 <0.001 2.00 <0.001 Fluids and Electrolytes 3.4 (2.9–3.8) 4.0 (3.5–4.4) 4.8 (4.4–5.1) 4.6 (4.4–4.8) 0.59 0.49 1.42 0.003 1.2 <0.001 CAKUT 3.7 (3.4–3.9) 3.9 (3.4–4.3) 3.8 (3.0–4.5) 3.4 (3.1–3.7) 0.2 0.99 0.12 0.99 −0.3 0.20 Hydronephrosis 4.1 (3.9–4.4) 3.8 (3.4–4.2) 4.2 (3.6–4.9) 3.7 (3.3–4.0) −0.37 1.00 0.10 0.99 − 0.4 0.02 Hypertension 3.6 (3.4–3.9) 4.1 (3.7–4.5) 4.6 (4.2–5.0) 4.2 (3.9–4.4) 0.47 0.91 0.92 0.30 0.6 0.004 Stones 2.6 (2.3–2.8) 3.0 (2.6–3.4) 3.3 (2.7–4.0) 3.2 (2.9–3.5) 0.39 0.99 0.77 0.66 0.6 0.001 Tubular Disorders 2.0 (1.8–2.3) 2.5 (2.1–2.9) 3.1 (2.6–3.6) 3.0 (2.7–3.3) 0.49 0.84 1.08 0.10 1.0 <0.001 Enuresis 4.2 (3.9–4.5) 4.7 (4.5–5.0) 4.9 (4.6–5.2) 4.0 (3.7–4.4) 0.53 0.72 0.71 0.81 −0.2 0.49 UTI 4.7 (4.6–4.9) 4.4 (4.0–5.0) 4.9 (4.6–5.2) 4.4 (4.1–4.7) −0.29 0.99 0.15 0.99 −0.3 0.05 Renal Imaging 4.1 (3.8–4.3) 3.1 (2.8–3.5) 3.9 (3.3–4.5) 3.5 (3.2–3.8) − 0.93 0.01 −0.18 0.99 − 0.5 0.005 Renal Masses 3.1(2.8–3.4) 3.2 (2.8–3.6) 3.6 (2.9–4.2) 3.6 (3.3–3.9) 0.12 0.99 0.49 0.99 0.5 0.01 Transplant 1.7 (1.4–1.9) 2.3 (1.9–2.8) 2.1 (1.6–2.6) 2.3 (2.0–2.6) 0.64 0.34 0.42 0.99 0.6 0.04 CKD 2.1 (1.8–2.4) 2.7 (2.3–3.1) 3.0 (2.2–3.8) 3.1 (2.8–3.4) 0.61 0.43 0.90 0.35 1.0 <0.001 Open in a new tab AKI Acute kidney injury; CAKUT Congenital anomalies of the kidney and urinary tract; CKD Chronic kidney disease; Neph-GP Nephrologist perceived importance score minus general paediatrician score; PD-GP Program director perceived importance score minus general paediatrician score; Res-GP Resident perceived importance score minus general paediatrician score; UTI Urinary tract infection. The mean competency score was 2.6, 95%CI (2.5 to 2.7) across all nephrology domains in the resident survey. The domains scoring below the mean were congenital anomalies of the kidneys and urinary tract, kidney transplantation, tubular disorders, and CKD ( Table 2 ). The mean perceived importance score was 3.7, 95%CI (3.6 to 3.8) across all objectives in the resident survey. The domains scoring significantly below the mean were kidney transplantation and tubular disorders. Each objective had a gap score greater than 0. The highest gap score in the resident survey was in the domain of fluids and electrolytes (gap score 1.8, 95%CI [1.5 to 2.0]). Surveys of paediatric nephrologists and paediatric residency program directors For the nephrologist survey, a total of 20 responses out of 80 recipients were received (25%). From the PD survey, there were a total of 9 responses out of 17 recipients (53%) received. Characteristics from paediatric nephrologist and residency program directors are shown in Supplementary Tables S3 and S4 , respectively. The mean perceived importance score across all objectives was 3.7, 95%CI (3.5 to 3.8) in the nephrologist survey and 3.9, 95%CI (3.8 to 4.1) in the program director survey. In the nephrologist survey, the domains scoring significantly below the overall mean in importance were kidney transplantation, tubular disorders, CKD, and kidney stones ( Table 3 ). In the program director survey, kidney transplantation, CKD, and tubular disorders scored below the overall mean importance score ( Table 3 ). Paediatric residents, PDs, and nephrologists agreed that stones, CKD, tubular disorders, and transplant were of lower importance, as these objectives consistently scored below the overall mean perceived importance score across all domains. Table 3 shows the mean perceived importance in all domains for the three groups. AKI was the domain with the largest discrepancy in perceived importance rated between general paediatricians and other practitioners. Following the Šídák multiple comparisons test, the importance of AKI was scored significantly lower by general paediatricians (2.4, 95%CI [2.1 to 2.8]) than paediatric residents (4.4, 95%CI [4.2 to 4.6], P<0.0001), paediatric nephrologists (4.2, 95%CI [3.8 to 4.6], P<0.0001), and PDs (4.2, 95%CI [3.7 to 4.7], P=0.0005). DISCUSSION In this needs assessment of nephrology training for general paediatrics, we developed important insight into perceptions about nephrology training and competency in general paediatrics, as seen through the lenses of different key stakeholders. We found that general paediatricians assign low importance and report low perceived competency in the domains of kidney transplantation, CKD, tubular disorders, kidney stones, and AKI. There was general agreement from respondents that most of these domains are of relatively lower importance within the nephrology objectives of training. For topics such as transplantation, CKD, and tubular disorders, this may be reflective of less exposure in clinical practice and in residency, as many of these patients are encountered primarily in subspecialty clinics. However, AKI was rated as more important to stakeholders other than general paediatricians in independent practice. These findings are consistent with findings from the 2011 survey of recent RCPSC graduates which identified ‘acute and chronic renal failure’ as a domain with inadequate competency ( 4 ). Notably, our study adds the perceptions of other stakeholders, and suggests that a perceived lack of importance may be a contributing factor to the low competency in this area. In addition, our study also explored all of the RCPSC nephrology objectives of training, compared to the previous study, which included only four nephrology domains amongst 50 common general paediatrics problems ( 4 ). Moreover, we have shown that the unimportance assigned to AKI seems to be limited to general paediatricians in independent practice: trainees did not report the same perceptions. This may be related to the perceived lack of exposure to AKI outside of the academic setting, as respondents to the general paediatrician survey were predominantly community-based practitioners. However, there has been overwhelming literature over the past decade suggesting that AKI is not necessarily a time-limited disease, and carries significant long-term consequences, including a higher risk of chronic kidney disease and hypertension ( 8–12 ). Furthermore, the majority of patients with AKI have post-discharge follow-up with non-nephrologists ( 13 ), suggesting a need to ensure adequate competence in this area. Under appreciation of AKI is not limited to community practitioners. Data from Canadian registries also show that recognition and follow-up of in-hospital AKI in paediatrics is suboptimal ( 11 ), despite its long-term implications. These data suggest that general paediatricians may underestimate the incidence, severity, and prognosis of AKI and that educational interventions are needed in this area. Paediatric residents in our study identified AKI among the more important objectives of training. This may suggest that initiatives aimed at improving competency in general paediatrics in AKI may be particularly beneficial at the continuing medical education level. Given the low perceived importance of AKI suggested by our practicing paediatrician respondents, there is a need to highlight the long-term disease burden to the general paediatrician in the community. Position statements and/or practice points on the topic of AKI are noticeably lacking from national paediatric guidelines in North America, with neither the Canadian Paediatric Society (CPS) nor the American Academy of Pediatrics (AAP) having a clinical practice guideline for general paediatricians on the topic of AKI. Nephrology domains featured in CPS and/or AAP guidelines include UTIs (including hydronephrosis and renal imaging) ( 14–16 ), enuresis ( 17 ), and hypertension ( 18 ), which mirrors the domains perceived as most important by paediatricians in the present study. This relationship may be bidirectional: topics deemed more important are more likely to be featured in clinical practice guidelines, and topics in these guidelines may be assigned higher importance. It has been shown that the majority of practicing paediatricians and paediatric residents in Canada do use CPS and/or AAP guidelines ( 19 ) and the paediatric nephrology community should consider developing an AKI guideline for general paediatricians. Another important aspect of the present study was the use of gap scores to identify domains with the largest discrepancies between perceived importance and competence, as had been done previously by Amin et al. ( 4 ). In that study ‘acute and chronic renal failure’ and ‘hypertension’ were noted to be domains with relatively large gap scores, and in the present study we note that hypertension was the domain with the single largest gap score in the general paediatrician survey. This may suggest that there is inadequate competence with childhood hypertension, despite an appreciation for its importance. The clinical implications of this knowledge gap are reflected in the literature, where despite improved recognition of hypertension as a disease entity in children over the past decade, there are still many cases that have had a delay in diagnosis, leading to prolonged time to initiation of therapy and development of preventable complications ( 20 , 21 ). Underdiagnosis of hypertension has also been attributed to inadequate screening and infrequent reference of normative blood pressure values in children ( 22 ). Our response rate (25 to 53%) was consistent with the rates reported in other web-based surveys of physicians ( 23 ). This may have introduced a selection bias in the results; however, given that our results are in line with the 2011 recent graduate survey ( 4 ), it is conceivable that knowledge gaps in AKI and hypertension are indeed domains for further study. Moreover, while the response rate in each group was suboptimal, there was agreement between groups in the majority of the low-importance classifications. The paediatrician survey was sent to practitioners in Ontario and may not necessarily reflect the entire Canadian paediatrician population. We also had relatively few academic paediatricians respond to the survey, so these results may be more applicable to community practitioners. Finally, surveys are limited in their ability to extract root causes and complex perceptions, and there is a need for further understanding of why these responses may have been generated. Future phases of this study will collect qualitative focus group data from practitioners to gain a more holistic view of the identified barriers. In the first published needs assessment of nephrology for general paediatrics, we found that general paediatricians do not feel competent with AKI and do not find this topic important to their practice, contrary to other key stakeholders’ opinions, and despite growing evidence in recent years of the under-recognition, poor follow-up, and significant long-term implications of AKI. Hypertension is the area with the largest knowledge gap, which also raises concerns due to its rising prevalence in paediatrics. Qualitative studies should also be conducted to better understand the experiences of practitioners and their perspectives toward these core nephrology topics. Finally, educational interventions are needed to address deficits in these crucial domains of kidney health in general paediatrics. Supplementary Material pxab089_suppl_Supplementary_Figure_S1 Click here for additional data file. (35.7KB, docx) pxab089_suppl_Supplementary_Table_S1 Click here for additional data file. (19.4KB, docx) pxab089_suppl_Supplementary_Table_S2 Click here for additional data file. (21.6KB, docx) pxab089_suppl_Supplementary_Table_S3 Click here for additional data file. (21.1KB, docx) pxab089_suppl_Supplementary_Table_S4 Click here for additional data file. (20.9KB, docx) ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS This study was done as part of a capstone research project in the Johns Hopkins University Master of Education in Health Professions (MEHP) Program. We are thankful to faculty and peers who provided guidance and feedback. Funding : There are no funders to report for this submission. Potential Conflicts of Interest : All authors: No reported conflicts of interest. All authors have submitted the ICMJE Form for Disclosure of Potential Conflicts of Interest. Conflicts that the editors consider relevant to the content of the manuscript have been disclosed. Contributor Information Amrit Kirpalani, Division of Nephrology, Department of Paediatrics, Schulich School of Medicine and Dentistry, Western University, London, Ontario, Canada; Lawson Health Research Institute, London, Ontario, Canada. Charushree Prasad, Division of Nephrology, Department of Paediatrics, McMaster University, Hamilton, Ontario, Canada. Natasha A Jawa, Division of Nephrology, Department of Paediatrics, The Hospital for Sick Children, Toronto, Ontario, Canada. Adelle R Atkinson, Department of Paediatrics, Faculty of Medicine, University of Toronto, Toronto, Ontario, Canada. Mark Feldman, Department of Paediatrics, Faculty of Medicine, University of Toronto, Toronto, Ontario, Canada. Justin M Jeffers, Department of Pediatrics, Johns Hopkins University, Baltimore, Maryland, USA. Damien G Noone, Division of Nephrology, Department of Paediatrics, The Hospital for Sick Children, Toronto, Ontario, Canada; Department of Paediatrics, Faculty of Medicine, University of Toronto, Toronto, Ontario, Canada. References 1. Weinstein AR, Reidy K, Norwood VF, Mahan JD. Factors influencing pediatric nephrology trainee entry into the workforce. Clin J Am Soc Nephrol 2010;5(10):1770–4. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Ferris M, Iglesia E, Ko Z, et al. Wanted: Pediatric nephrologists! - why trainees are not choosing pediatric nephrology. Ren Fail 2014;36(8):1340–4. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Kumar G, Ni A, Lawrence SE, Doja A. Incorporating CanMEDS and subspecialty training into paediatric residency programs: Why are we still deficient? Paediatr Child Health 2012;17(1):e7–e11. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMe