graduate Curriculum in Paediatrics. [cited November 19, 2020]. < https://www.pupdoc.ca/en/canuc-paeds/ >. 16. Dyrbye LN, Thomas MR, Shanafelt TD. Medical student distress: causes, consequences, and proposed solutions. Mayo Clin Proc 2005;80(12):1613–22. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 17. Dyrbye LN, West CP, Satele D, et al. Burnout among U.S. medical students, residents, and early career physicians relative to the general U.S. population. Acad Med 2014;89(3):443–51. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 18. The future of medical education in Canada (FMEC): A collective vision for MD education. The Association of Faculties of Medicine of Canada; [cited November 19, 2020]. < https://afmc.ca/sites/default/files/pdf/2010-FMEC-MD_EN.pdf >. [ Google Scholar ] 19. Supporting learning and continuous practice improvement for physicians in Canada: A new way forward. [cited June 30, 2021]. < https://www.afmc.ca/sites/default/files/pdf/2019-FMEC-MD_EN.pdf >. 20. Keegan DA, Scott I, Sylvester M, Tan A, Horrey K, Weston WW. Shared Canadian Curriculum in Family Medicine (SHARC-FM): Creating a national consensus on relevant and practical training for medical students. Can Fam Physician 2017;63(4):e223–31. [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials pxab066_suppl_Supplementary_Appendix_1 Click here for additional data file. (26.1KB, docx) pxab066_suppl_Supplementary_Appendix_2 Click here for additional data file. (36.1KB, docx)',46,'2025-10-10 08:30:33','2025-10-10 08:30:33');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1446,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11848105','Status of cerebrovascular autoregulation relates to outcome in severe paediatric head injury: STARSHIP, a prospective multicentre validation study.','Agrawal S, Smith C, Bogli SY, Placek MM, Cabeleira M, White D, Daubney E, Young A, Beqiri E, Kayani R, O''Donnell D, Pathan N, Watson S, Maw A, Ganrett M, Kanthimathinathan HK, Bangalore H, Sundararajan S, Subramanian G, Raffaj D, Sarfatti A, Lampareillo S, Mayer A, Ross O, Czosnyka M, Hutchinson PJ, Smielewski P; STARSHIP study team.','2025','ptbi starship nct0688462 ictus starship derangements ptbi individualizing ptbi addenbrookes gn2609 a094693 ellerbeck ellerbeck ptbi individualise ptbi ptbi ptbi personalising ptbi starship ptbi ptbi btf anatomico vasodilatation hyperaemia vasomotor deranged cppopt cppopt ptbi ptbi starship ptbi ptbi starship ptbi btf normocarbia normoxia hyperosmolar hyperventilation barbiturate decompressive craniectomy 100hz woke cppopt parabolic cppopt δcppopt cppopt cppopt gosep gosep gosep gosep ictus rstatix tidyr dplyr gtsummary lme4 matchit ictus variate variates confines gosep gosep gosep gosep neighbour calibre veto ptbi kgs polytrauma polytrauma bicycle ictus intraparenchymal evds cppopt cppopt gosep ictus etco cppopt δcpp δcpp δcpp δcpp cppopt etco kilo pascals millimeters dichotomised ictus dichotomised ictus gosep ictus starship ptbi ptbi ptbi starship btf cppopt btf cppopt δcpp cppopt starship ptbi nai ptbi ptbi ptbi unwillingness ictus ictus cppopt ptbi dichotomised ptbi czosnyka smielewski ganrett maw cabeleira placek daubney kayani pathan bangalore sundararajan raffaj sarfatti lampareillo bogli beqiri smielewski bogli smielewski bogli smielewski czosnyka smielewski smielewski bogli smielewski nihr203312 marek czosnyka smielewski bogli skye erta beqiri n013433 brc hrc indebted ricardo branco bogli shruti sa882 starship carly tooke belfield amisha mistry collette bunni beckley tahnima khatun hafiza khatun olivia nugent aldridge morcombe quinton postlethwaite jenny caitlin mmc2 mmc3 dewan wellons bonfield kochanek tasker s82 udomphorn armstead vavilala pediatrneurol armstead fisk vavilala 0b013e31816725d7 muizelaar marmarou newlon wachi s00381 czosnyka laing pickard vasomotor czosnyka piechnik beqiri kaam cogitate czosnyka jalloh easley e1205 e1212 czosnyka rohlwink mauff diedler gerbig heimberg schuhmann hockel 3_48 hockel diedler neunhoeffer heimberg schuhmann s00701 abecasis zakrzewska czosnyka s00381 appavu temkit foldes s12028 velle lewen howells hanell enblad peds22352 placek starship smielewski beqiri mataczynski neuromonitoring beqiri ercole aries cppopt s10877 sorrentino ksprowicz budohoski s12028 roozenbeek lingsma perel cc10240 steyerberg marmarou nurt alali vavrek dikmen nathens temkin kanthimathinathan scholefield e270 e2274 kantawala inhospital bhargava lifshitz kuhia agoston kolias timofeev decompressive craniectomy rescueicp mmc2 mmc3','Summary Background Continuous assessment of cerebral autoregulation (CA) using pressure reactivity index (PRx), is a promising tool for individualized management to improve outcome after traumatic brain injury (TBI). However, experience with CA in paediatric TBI (pTBI) is limited to retrospective or single-centre studies. Methods Studying Trends of AutoRegulation in Severe Head Injury in Paediatrics (STARSHIP) ( clinicalTrials.gov identifier-NCT0688462), was a prospective, multicentre, observational, research database study conducted across 10 identified UK Paediatric Intensive Care Units from 01.07.2018 till 31.04.2024. The main objective was to validate and identify optimal thresholds of PRx associated with outcome (as assessed with Glasgow outcome scale extended for Pediatrics at 12 months) in children (<16 years) requiring invasive arterial blood pressure and intracranial pressure monitoring for TBI and establish a comprehensive research database. Apart from high-resolution data, clinical and outcome data up to 12 months post-ictus were collected. Univariable and multivariable analyses including propensity score matching, were employed to determine the effect of PRx on outcome whilst considering covariates, centre-specific differences and other multimodal metrics. Findings Out of 153 recruited, 135 children (median age 96 months) with consent and adequate data were included. Overall median PRx of the cohort was −0.09 (IQR −0.19 to 0.08). Both ICP and PRx were elevated in non-survivors and children with unfavourable outcome. PRx retained a significant effect on outcome after adjusting for various clinical and monitoring variables. The critical PRx threshold identified were 0.5 for mortality and 0.0 for favourable outcome. Interpretation With STARSHIP, we validate the outcome association of CA derangements as assessed by PRx in pTBI in the first prospective observational multicentre study. This provides additional evidence for the potential use of PRx for individualizing prognosis and treatment and pave way for further research in pTBI with the created database. Funding This study was funded by Action Medical Research for Children''s Charity and Addenbrookes Charitable Trust, UK (Grant number-GN2609). Cambridge University Hospitals is the study sponsor (Reference: A094693, contact person: Michelle Ellerbeck- michelle.ellerbeck@nhs.net ). Keywords: Paediatric traumatic brain injury, Cerebral autoregulation, Pressure reactivity index (PRx), Cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP) Research in context. Evidence before this study The current recommended treatment targets for cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP) in paediatric traumatic brain injury (pTBI) may be inadequate across the paediatric age range. Cerebral autoregulation (CA) as assessed by pressure reactivity index (PRx), can help individualise CPP. Medline search for studies published in English till 18th August 2024 identified 30 studies assessing CA in pTBI; continuous CA monitoring was used in 18 studies, out of which 14 used PRx. All these studies identified PRx association with outcome after pTBI. However, all these are retrospective, single centre studies which limit the use of PRx in pTBI. Evidence from multicentre studies is required before it can be used towards personalising treatment targets. Added value of this study In the first prospective, multicentre study powered to identify PRx thresholds associated with outcome in pTBI (STARSHIP), we confirm PRx association with outcome, describe its temporal trends, and identify thresholds related to outcome in a cohort of 135 pTBI patients. We have also created a unique, multicentre, research database of time-synchronized, high-resolution physiological data paired with comprehensive clinical and outcome information. Implications of all the available evidence Our results support continuous PRx monitoring in pTBI. Evidence generated in our study will help with further external validation and phase II trials in children. Introduction Traumatic brain injury (TBI) affects between 47 and 280 per 100,000 children worldwide and is the leading cause of morbidity and mortality in children. 1 Despite reduction in mortality, nearly 61% children suffer significant morbidity after moderate to severe TBI. 1 , 2 To reduce neuro-morbidity, current management strategies include minimising secondary brain injury by maintaining intracranial pressure (ICP) below 20 mmHg and cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP, defined as arterial blood pressure, ABP, minus ICP) targets between 40 and 50 mmHg as per Brain Trauma Foundation (BTF) guidelines, based mainly on class III evidence. 3 These thresholds may be inadequate across paediatric age range due to changing anatomico-physiological features, heterogenous disease presentation, and the dynamic disease process. 4 , 5 Cerebral autoregulation (CA) helps maintain adequate cerebral blood flow (CBF) by cerebrovascular vasoconstriction or vasodilatation with rise or fall in ABP, respectively. After TBI, impairments in CA expose the brain to secondary injury from ischemia, hypoxia, hyperaemia and raised ICP. 6 Impairment in CA has shown association with neurological outcome in TBI. 7 Cerebral vasomotor pressure reactivity, a proxy measure of cerebrovascular component of CA, can be assessed clinically by studying the transmission of slow wave fluctuations of ABP to slow changes in ICP, where ICP is treated as a surrogate of cerebral blood volume, thereby CBF. A correlation coefficient, termed PRx (pressure reactivity index), can express this transmission with negative values representing robust CA (ABP changes not reflected in ICP) and positive values representing deranged CA (ABP fluctuations transmitted into ICP). 8 PRx has been validated in adult TBI prognostication. 8 Plotting PRx against measured CPP over a certain time period can help estimate an optimum CPP where the vasculature is most reactive (CPPopt) for individual patient at a particular time point. 9 A recent Phase II trial has proven feasibility and safety of CPPopt as a potential treatment target in adult TBI. 10 Given the physiological changes in ICP and CBF in children, monitoring CA is of particular interest, 7 and PRx has been suggested as an advanced neuro-monitoring tool for paediatric TBI (pTBI). 3 To date, paediatric evidence is limited to single centre, small or retrospective studies, which limits PRx''s generalisability. 11 , 12 , 13 , 14 , 15 , 16 , 17 , 18 , 19 With increasing use of artificial intelligence, unique physiological information coded in the pulse level waveform profiles can be extracted and help with the management of pTBI. There is no multicentre paediatric dataset with waveform resolution data which creates a serious data gap. To fill these gaps, we have conducted the first prospective multicentre cohort study in children, Studying Trends of AutoRegulation in Severe Head Injury in Paediatrics (STARSHIP), to investigate PRx and detect optimal thresholds associated with outcome in pTBI. In addition, we establish a multicentre high-resolution neuro-monitoring database in pTBI. Methods The study protocol (including a description of the methodology) is previously published, 20 and available in Supplement 1 . Study design STARSHIP was a prospective, multicentre, observational cohort study conducted in the United Kingdom (UK) with approval from Southwest-Central Bristol Research Ethics Committee (Ref: 18/SW/0053, 23/SW/0011) as a research database study. 20 Study aim To identify critical thresholds of PRx in relation to outcome in a multicentre pTBI cohort, and to create a research database of high-resolution physiological monitoring data with demographics, clinical details, as well as neuropsychological outcome up to 12 months in this cohort. Sample size A power analysis estimated a necessary sample size of 120. Based on a prior investigation, we expected a mean (±SD) PRx of 0.03 ± 0.13 for favourable and 0.10 ± 0.17 for unfavourable outcome, and a favourable vs unfavourable ratio of 0.77 (i.e. 23% will suffer unfavourable outcome). 11 For the estimation, a one-sided analysis (based on the assumption that higher PRx could only be associated with worse outcome) was done and a power of 0.8 and alpha of 0.05 were used. A final inclusion target was set at 135 patients to allow for protocol violations, consent withdrawal, or lost follow-up. Ethics The ethics approval came from the Southwest-Central Bristol Research Ethics Committee as a research database study, in the 1st instance for five years (Ref: 18/SW/0053), which was subsequently renewed in March 2023 (Ref: 23/SW/0011) for five more years in view of COVID-19 related delays in study completion. All included patients had valid consent from the legal guardian/parent except for those who died before the consent could be obtained; for these patients, the clinician responsible for patient''s clinical care only (not study team) accessed and anonymised data before sharing it with the study team. Sites and participants Children (≤16 years of age) admitted to Paediatric intensive care unit (PICU) with TBI [Glasgow Coma Score (GCS) ≤8 and/or TBI related pathology confirmed on neuroimaging] requiring invasive ICP and ABP monitoring for clinical indications were recruited from 10 identified PICUs. There were no exclusion criteria for the study. Patient recruitment and consent High-resolution data was collected as soon as possible after commencement of invasive monitoring and stabilization, without disturbing the routine clinical care as per individual PICUs'' protocol. The identified guardian/parent was consented for data sharing and follow-up at a convenient time before the patient was discharged from the hospital; if refused, collected data was destroyed. For patients who died before obtaining consent, the clinician responsible for patient''s clinical care only (not study team) accessed and anonymised data before sharing it with the study team. Data collection Apart from general demographics and clinical data, we also collected mechanism of injury, relevant past history, post resuscitation GCS, initial pupillary size and reactivity, pre-hospital events, disease severity scores and admission neuroimaging findings. Daily laboratory and ventilation parameters, medical and surgical interventions, and any complications were also collected. As the study involved critically unwell children, only biological sex was collected, no information was collected on the patient''s gender. The study was entirely observational, treatment centres were allowed to carry out their regular practices and the CA information was not used to change patient management (briefly, all sites used BTF guidelines adapted for local needs with tiered management including airway protection, mechanical ventilation to achieve normocarbia and normoxia, sedation, analgesia with head end elevation and invasive ABP and ICP monitoring. A target ICP of below 20 mmHg was management with a combination of hematoma evacuation deep sedation, muscle relaxants, hyperosmolar treatment, CSF diversion, mild hyperventilation and hypothermia with barbiturate coma and decompressive craniectomy reserved for refractory ICP. Targets for CPP were maintained with intravenous fluids and inotropes). The routinely monitored physiological data including ICP, ABP and CPP were collected continuously from the bedside monitors via study laptop equipped with ICM+ (Cambridge Enterprise Ltd, Cambridge, UK). Pulsatile signals [ICP, ABP and ECG] were recorded using relevant, data export protocols implemented in the software with a sampling frequency of at least 100Hz. 21 The continuous data collection stopped when the patient either woke up or died and invasive monitoring was stopped. Data processing Artefact cleaning and data pre-processing was performed as previously described. 20 In short, high-resolution (i.e. waveforms, Figures S1 and S2 in Supplement 2 ) monitoring data acquired using ICM+ was subsequently processed using the same software. Blood pressure waveforms were manually cleaned to remove sections with arterial line failure (continuous reduction of the arterial blood pressure amplitude followed by flushing). Additionally, we automatically removed sections with ABP below 0 or above 300 mmHg and sections with a pulse amplitude below 15 mmHg and sections with ICP below −30 or above 200 mmHg or with low amplitude (<0.04 mmHg), or with a 95% Spectral edge frequency above 10 Hz (high-frequency noise). The resulting artifact free data was then processed to acquire 10 s averages of ABP, ICP, CPP (difference between ABP and ICP), thus removing the higher frequency (cardiac and respiratory) components. Metrics describing cerebrovascular reactivity were calculated as previously described: PRx—spearman correlation coefficient between 10 s averages of ABP and ICP calculated over 5 min; optimal CPP (CPPopt)—CPP at the lowest PRx value identified by a parabolic curve fitted to 5 min median CPP and PRx values, with bins of CPP of 2.5 mmHg, with additional heuristic algorithmic details previously described. 22 The bin-width was chosen considering the manually meticulously cleaned data and through visual inspection of the CPPopt curves. Additionally, delta CPP (ΔCPPopt = CPPopt-CPP) was calculated to assess deviation of actual CPP from CPPopt. Study outcomes The primary objective was to identify PRx thresholds associated with outcome at 12 months as assessed with Glasgow Outcome Scale Extended Pediatric version (GOSEP) with the hypothesis that these would be different in children compared to adults (based upon previous work), 13 and to create a research database. Outcome was dichotomized to favourable (GOSEP 1–4) or unfavourable (GOSEP 5–8). COVID-19 pandemic impact We required two-year extension to finish target recruitment. With approval from the study sponsor and ethics committee, to capture missed follow-ups’, retrospective GOSEP at one-year post-ictus was collected after telephone interviews with parents/guardian or clinic appointments, whichever was feasible. Statistics Statistical analysis and figure preparation was performed in R Studio (R version 4.4.1– https://www.r-project.org/ –packages used: rstatix, tidyr, dplyr, gtsummary, MASS, lme4, MatchIt, ggplot2). Briefly, continuous variables are expressed as median (interquartile range–IQR) and categorical variables as counts and proportions respectively. All tests were two-tailed and unadjusted for multiple comparisons. Analysis was performed primarily using full and repeated with the first 24 h monitoring duration after ictus. Clinical and monitoring metrics were assessed with the Mann–Whitney U -test, student t -test, Fisher exact or chi-square test, as appropriate. Logistic regression was performed to assess the variable association with outcome. Univariable analyses were performed to compare mortality (survivors vs non-survivors) and 12-month dichotomized outcome (favourable vs unfavourable) and assess difference in physiological variables across age stratified groups (0–2, 2–8, ≥8 years). Critical thresholds of PRx for predicting outcome were identified using iterative chi-square tests, as per previously defined methods. 23 In short, 2 × 2 contingency tables were created by grouping patients according to, 1) mortality (survivors, and non-survivors) or dichotomized outcome (favourable, and unfavourable), and 2) above or below a PRx threshold (in 0.05 steps, from −1 to 1). Thresholds where there was at least one case in each contingency group were used and Pearson''s chi-square or Fisher''s exact test was calculated for each table. Resulting chi squared values and the PRx threshold were plotted. The highest chi-square value was assumed to have the best discriminative value for death and 12 months dichotomized outcome. This process was repeated in sub-cohorts to identify critical thresholds based on three age groups (0–2-year-olds, 2–8-year-olds, and ≥8-year-olds). To investigate the prognostic effect of the acute period post injury, mean PRx value for the first 24 h, first 3 days, and first week post injury was used in additional sub-analyses. Propensity scores were calculated for each patient which is a single “z-score” that takes multiple variables into consideration, that are known to affect outcome. Using an overall propensity score per patient instead of adding each variable as independent co-variate allows the model to be adjusted for multiple co-variates within the confines of a limited cohort size. The following parameters were chosen based on the currently available literature describing clinical outcome predictors and largely follow the metrics included in the IMPACT score: Age, motor GCS, pupillary reactivity, injury severity score (ISS), Rotterdam Score, pre-hospital hypoxia, hypotension, cardiac arrest. Multivariable analyses As the non-survivor cohort had only 10 patients, multivariable analyses were performed comparing the 12-months dichotomized outcome only. To maximize the statistical power and account for heterogeneity, three multivariable analysis methods were employed. 1. Sliding dichotomy: This approach creates a relative outcome scale for each patient propensity score. 24 The definition of favourable and unfavourable outcome is then adjusted depending on the propensity score for each patient. In this way, for a patient with very poor prognosis based on the clinical parameters added to the propensity score, survival would be regarded as a favourable outcome. In a patient with excellent prognosis on admission, only a GOSEP of 1 or 2 would be considered a favourable outcome, any other outcome would be considered unfavourable. This method, in effect, mimics the clinical decision process in which the outcome of each patient is put into perspective considering the initial severity. Other metrics which remain relevant when assessed against this adjusted outcome definition are consequently relevant irrespective of the initial severity. Based on the propensity scores, the patients were divided into three groups of roughly equal size with low, intermediate, and high likelihood of unfavourable outcome. The definition of favourable and unfavourable outcome was then adjusted depending on the propensity score for each patient. In patients with low likelihood only GOSEP of 1–2 was considered favourable, while for the patients with intermediate likelihood GOSEP of 1–4 and for the patients with high likelihood GOSEP of 1–6 was considered favourable. The patients were then assessed using logistic regression based on this adjusted outcome definition. 2. Propensity Score Matching (PSM) was performed using the nearest-neighbour method with a calibre of 0.2 and 1:1 matching to ensure that matches were within a reasonable distance in terms of their propensity scores. The propensity scores were built using logistic regression comparing favourable vs unfavourable outcome. The results of the matching are shown in Supplement 2 ( Figure S3 & Table S1 ). 3. Mixed Effects Models: A mixed effects model is particularly suited for handling the hierarchical structure of the data, with observations nested within the different treatment centers. This approach allowed us to adjust for variations across centers while assessing the overall treatment effects and was used for two purposes: - To assess differences in daily PRx trajectories depending on the day post injury, a mixed effects model including day post injury and outcome as fixed effects, as well as the patient ID as a random effect was applied. - To account for centre-specific differences in ICP, and CPP management, mixed effect model was applied which included fixed effects for PRx, ICP and CPP, with random intercepts and slopes for ICP and CPP for each treatment centre (no random effect was added to PRx since it was not targeted). 25 Role of the funding source The funders or the sponsor did not have any role in the collection, management, analysis, and interpretation of the data; preparation, review, or approval of the manuscript; or decision to submit the manuscript for publication, the right to veto publication or control the decision regarding which journal the manuscript was submitted. Results Between 1st July 2018 and 31st March 2023, 165 pTBI patients fulfilling the eligibility criteria were recruited; consent could be obtained in 153 (92.7%) patients. The final analysis was performed on 135 patients with adequate (minimum ABP and ICP recording for at least 6 h) high resolution physiological data alongside clinical details; 12 months follow-up was completed in 124 patients ( Fig. 1 ). Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Inclusion/exclusion flow chart. Demographics and clinical presentation The median age at presentation was 96 months (IQR 26–152 months) with a male preponderance (n = 105, 78%). Most patients suffered a blunt head injury (130/135, 96%) with road traffic collisions being the commonest injury mechanism (47%). As per the study inclusion criteria, patients were recruited to the study if they had GCS ≤8 and/or CT scan confirming TBI. The overall outcome was good with 93% survival (125/135) and 70% (80/114) favourable outcome amongst the survivors who completed 12 months follow-up. Table 1 summarizes the demographic and clinical details of the entire cohort. The pre-hospital events, total and motor GCS, pupillary reactivity, admission CT scores and blood glucose, were all significantly different between outcome groups. Interestingly for dichotomized outcome, the significance was reduced for most parameters except for ISS which was significantly associated with dichotomized outcome but not mortality. There were 32 patients who were recruited despite a GCS above 8 as they had CT scan positive for TBI, some of these patients also dropped GCS with disease progression after the initial recording and hence qualified for the study. For patients with GCS >8, 10/32 had unfavourable outcome compared to 35/99 for patients with GCS ≤8 and overall, there was no difference in the patient outcomes in relation to GCS distribution. None of the surgical interventions had any association with the outcome and are not described further. Table 1. Demographic & clinical presentation of the cohort. Characteristic Entire cohort Discharge mortality 12-month dichotomized outcome N = 135 Survivor, N = 125 Non-survivor, N = 10 p-value Favourable, N = 80 Unfavourable, N = 44 p-value Sex >0.9 0.5 Female 30 (22%) 28 (22%) 2 (20%) 19 (24%) 8 (18%) Male 105 (78%) 97 (78%) 8 (80%) 61 (76%) 36 (82%) Age (months) 96 (26, 152) 95 (27, 151) 130 (18, 157) 0.8 95 (28, 149) 132 (26, 155) 0.7 Weight (kgs) 12 (6, 34) 12 (6, 35) 8 (5, 17) 0.3 12 (5, 37) 9 (6, 29) 0.6 Type of injury 0.3 0.7 Blunt 130 (96%) 121 (97%) 9 (90%) 76 (95%) 43 (98%) Penetrating 5 (3.7%) 4 (3.2%) 1 (10%) 4 (5.0%) 1 (2.3%) Isolated/polytrauma 0.10 0.2 Isolated 63 (47%) 61 (49%) 2 (20%) 42 (53%) 18 (41%) Polytrauma 72 (53%) 64 (51%) 8 (80%) 38 (48%) 26 (59%) Mechanism of injury 0.4 0