gical data was available for analysis with median monitoring length of 109 h per patient (IQR 67–170, range 6–678 h). The median time from ictus to monitoring was 33 h (IQR 24–36, range 12–138 h). All centres used intraparenchymal ICP monitors; in patients with EVDs’, only the periods of monitoring with closed drainage were used for analysis. The median amount of available data within the first 24 h was 13 h (IQR 8–18). The median percentage of good monitoring time (i.e. after artefact removal) was 91% (IQR 84–96%) for ABP, and 88% (IQR 75–93%) for ICP respectively. Both PRx and CPPopt could be calculated for 73% (IQR 62–91%) and 82% (IQR 81–92%) of the length of monitoring period/patient respectively. Figures S1 and S2 show examples of the physiological data collected. Median PRx for the entire cohort was −0.09 (IQR −0.19, 0.08), suggesting a relatively preserved CA averaged over the monitoring period. Higher PRx values were noted in the first 48 h in non-survivors. Plotting the median PRx value per patient per day against outcome for the first 8 days ( Fig. 2 ) showed a trend towards decreasing PRx over the first 4 days and worsening beyond day 4 in children with unfavourable outcomes. Average CPP increased with age, with ICP remaining similar between age groups ( Table 2 ). Similarly, CPPopt increased with age (61 vs 65 vs 67 mmHg for 0–2, 2–8 and above 8 years respectively, p = 0.005). Examples of the physiological data collected ( Figures S1, S3 ), PRx and ICP relative to each GOSEP outcome category ( Figure S4 ) can be found in the Supplement 2 . Fig. 2. Open in a new tab Mean PRx of the entire cohort per day from ictus (day 0) for mortality at discharge (A) and dichotomized outcome (B) as favourable (Glasgow Outcome Scale Extended Pediatrics 1–4) and unfavourable (Glasgow Outcome Scale Extended Pediatrics 5–8). Only the first 8 days shown. PRx, pressure reactivity index. Table 2. Physiological data of the entire cohort by outcome and age groups. Characteristi