Significant association depicted with p-values in bold (p < 0.05). Critical thresholds of PRx The critical PRx threshold yielding highest value with sequential chi-square testing was 0.5 for mortality, and 0.0 for favourable outcome at 12 months respectively ( Fig. 3 ). We also calculated PRx thresholds at various time points (first 24 h, first 3 days and first week post injury) which remained between 0.40–0.50 for mortality and 0.0–0.1 for favourable outcome ( Supplement 2 , Table S2 ). Similar calculations for each age category did not yield results likely due to small number of patients per category. Fig. 3. Open in a new tab Critical Thresholds of PRx for mortality and dichotomized outcome. Multiple chi squared tests were used to identify critical thresholds of PRx for the entire cohort and stratified by age group- for mortality (A) and dichotomised outcome (C) as well as for different time periods post ictus- for mortality (B) and dichotomised outcome (D), respectively. The PRx value that corresponded to the highest Chi squared values are the critical thresholds for that cohort or time period. PRx, pressure reactivity index. ∗No thresholds could be calculated for 0–2 years age group due to insufficient number of patients. Regression analyses Univariable analyses showed a significant association of higher PRx and ICP (overall and first 24 h) with mortality and unfavourable outcome while lower CPP was only associated with mortality but not unfavourable outcome ( Table 3 ). The multimodal metrics remained significant irrespective of time to monitoring post-ictus and duration of monitoring (PRx: OR 1.29, CI 1.10–1.53, p = 0.003; ICP: OR 1.13, CI 1.04–1.27, p = 0.027; CPP: OR 0.95, CI 0.90–0.99, p = 0.031). When assessing PRx, ICP and CPP trajectories within linear mixed effects models (dependent on day post injury and outcome with patient added as a random effect), there was no significant association between day and multimodal metric. The overall and first 24 h’ average PRx and ICP for each GOSEP category is shown in Supplement 2 ( Figure S4 ). Table 3. Overall physiological variables and outcome explored with regression analyses. Variable Discharge mortality 12-month dichotomized outcome Odds ratio (95% confidence interval) p-value Odds ratio (95% confidence interval) p-value Overall PRx 1.59 (1.29–2.06) <0.001 1.26 (1.09–1.49) 0.003 Overall ICP 1.40 (1.18–1.76) <0.001 1.12 (1.04–1.24) 0.009 Overall CPP 0.92 (0.86–0.97) 0.004 0.97 (0.93–1.00) 0.094 First 24-h PRx 1.62 (1.30–2.13) <0.001 1.24 (1.06–1.46) 0.008 First 24-h ICP 1.24 (1.09–1.50) 0.013 1.14 (1.04–1.28) 0.016 First 24-h CPP 0.88 (0.80–0.95) 0.005 0.95 (0.90–1.00) 0.053 Open in a new tab n , number of patients; PRx, pressure reactivity index; ICP, intracranial pressure; CPP, cerebral perfusion pressure. Overall variables investigated in 135 patients; first 24 h included 107 patients. Significant association depicted with p-values in bold (p < 0.05). A sliding dichotomy approach was used to assess the added value of PRx with the outcome definition corrected for initial severity of disease. Within the overall monitoring period, there was a significant impact of PRx on the likelihood of unfavourable outcome (OR 1.28, CI 1.04–1.61, p = 0.024) together with ICP (OR 1.15, CI 1.04–1.30, p = 0.018). When evaluating the initial 24 h after ictus, there was only a trend towards significance with PRx (OR 1.21, CI 1.00–1.49, p = 0.055) and ICP considered together (OR 1.19, CI 1.06–1.38, p = 0.010). With PSM approach, a total of 60 patients were matched after confirming no relevant difference in confounders distribution post correction ( Figure S3 in Supplement 2 ); for patients with favourable and unfavourable outcome, no difference was seen in ICP [median 13.8 (12.7, 17.1) vs 15.4 (13.3, 17.0) mmHg, p = 0.3], but the difference in PRx remained significant [median −0.14 (−0.22, 0.00) vs 0.03 (−0.14, 0.13), p = 0.022]. The difference in ICP, CPP, and PRx depending on the centre is shown in Supplement 2 ( Figure S5 ). When added to a mixed effects model, PRx was the only variable that remained relevant to outcome (OR 1.30, CI 1.01–1.69, p = 0.046) unlike CPP (OR 1.06, CI 0.91–1.23, p = 0.4) or ICP (OR 1.26, CI 0.92–1.71, p = 0.15). Discussion STARSHIP study marks the first, prospective, multicentre validation of PRx in pTBI. The study was powered to identify critical PRx thresholds for outcome in this cohort and was performed in accordance with the prior published protocol and analysis plan. 20 A total of 135 children enrolled from 10 centres across the UK has also established a unique research database which contains time-synchronized, high-resolution physiological data paired with comprehensive clinical and outcome information. This invaluable resource will, in due course, be opened to third-party investigators, fostering continued research and collaboration to help improve understanding and outcome from pTBI. In this paper, we present the per protocol analysis with three main findings in relation to PRx: 1) Independent association of PRx with outcome, even when the outcome was adjusted for initial disease severity, propensity score matching, and centre specific effects, 2) Worse PRx in the first 48 h in non-survivors which progressively worsened beyond day 4 in children with unfavourable outcome, and, 3) Critical PRx threshold of 0.5 for mortality and 0.0 for favourable outcome respectively. To date, only a handful of single-centre studies have shown PRx association with outcome in pTBI, with most study cohort sizes between 10 and 60, and a single study with 196 patients. 11 , 12 , 13 , 14 , 15 , 16 , 17 , 18 , 19 For the first time, with STARSHIP, we investigate and characterize this association in a multicentre prospective cohort. We used robust statistical methods to strengthen our results as per recommendations and confirmed the outcome association of PRx in multivariate statistical models accounting for disease heterogeneity, severity and between centre variations. 25 Sliding dichotomy effectively mimics intuitive clinical prognostication, where outcome is assessed relative to the initial injury. 26 Despite this adjustment, PRx remained associated with outcome, regardless of the initial injury severity. We used PSM to select a homogenous sub-cohort of patients without significant difference in disease characteristics but differing outcomes which removed patients with “predetermined” prognosis (i.e. highest or lowest disease severity), allowing to focus on patients with uncertain outcome who could be potentially susceptible to monitoring based treatment. Again, in this sub-cohort, only PRx remained a significant outcome predictor suggesting that PRx carries additional information when the outcome is not “predetermined” by severity of the initial injury, while ICP does not. Lastly, our data revealed centre-dependent variations in ICP, ABP and CPP reflecting possible differences in management protocols. This in itself is not surprising given the uncertainty in thresholds recommended by BTF and was highlighted by a recent survey of practices in the UK. 3 , 27 After accounting for centre differences with mixed effects model, only PRx remained a significant predictor of outcome. As PRx is dependent on the dynamics of ICP and CPP rather than individual values, the centre differences in treatment targets did not affect outcome association. With improved understanding, this relevant finding could offer means for choosing individual treatment targets to fine tune PICU management. Higher PRx thresholds for mortality discrimination in children than adults in our study may reflect a better tolerance for impaired CA in children. 20 The lower threshold of PRx for favourable outcome than mortality is reported in both adult and paediatric patients and indicates the importance of intact CA for improved neurological outcome. 13 , 23 This offers a potential focus to investigate the impact of ‘insult dose’ of PRx above this threshold on outcome as well as informing treatment target selection. In contrast to the many studies conducted in much larger adult TBI cohorts, we did not observe outcome difference with deviations from CPPopt in our cohort. However, we noted higher median CPP values across all age groups than the current BTF recommendations, likely a result of higher managed targets as nearly all the patients in the study were on vasopressors for some duration and a significant difference in CPP, CPPopt, and ΔCPP between age stratified groups, similar to Lewis et al. 13 This likely reflects some form of age-based individualization of the CPP targets by clinical teams in line with physiological understanding and in keeping with other paediatric studies which have identified higher thresholds of CPP for in-hospital mortality across age groups. 28 , 29 Hence, our results do not exclude the possibility of CPPopt playing a role in individualized management in children given that our study has limited number of patients and observed between centre differences. It would be important to elucidate if higher individualized targets based upon real-time information about CA could improve the neuro-outcomes in the survivors. Hence, larger prospective studies, powered to define age and CA related, optimal CPP targets in children are warranted. Given that there is evidence of sex and gender on outcome after TBI in adults, that would be an important area to explore in the paediatric population in future studies as well. 30 Our study has many strengths, the most important being the prospective multicentre design with a predefined sample size and protocol. Given the paucity of such evidence and data, STARSHIP database offers a unique opportunity of ongoing research and data collection in pTBI. The sample size calculation could have been skewed due to higher mortality in the pilot study used for power calculation. 11 Ideally, we would have liked to capture consecutive patients fulfilling the inclusion criteria; however given the difficulties with consent in children with suspected NAI, we may have missed recruiting some of these patients. The cohort of patients in our study would be a representative sample of pTBI in the UK as explained in the protocol. Each of the recruiting sites was chosen as the regional paediatric neurosurgical referral centres with a PICU in the UK (8–20 beds per center) with an expected recruitment of 7–10 pTBI requiring invasive ICP monitoring (national audit data for PICU and Trauma as well as from the experience of recruiting to another pTBI study) per site per year; all except one site are also the regional Major Trauma Centre receiving both primary and secondary candidate major trauma patients serving a population between 3 and 7 million (one site only receives secondary trauma transfers). It''s difficult to fully assess the impact of the COVID-19 pandemic due to reduced research staff and the pandemic-related restrictions on visiting PICUs, along with the reduced incidence of trauma in children for the majority of the study recruitment period, the recruited sample may not be fully representative. This prolonged the study duration and required modifications to capture lost follow-ups. 31 , 32 Of the missed 12-month follow-ups, most were related to pandemic impact (6), parents'' unwillingness for telephone calls (4), and family migration (1). We chose 12 months to assess the impact as some studies suggest that most patients show improvement or deterioration up to that time point post-ictus and six months may not completely capture the outcome from TBI. 33 Additional components can affect the recovery process in the first-year post-ictus and although we cannot account for differences in family and social construct, all patients would have equal access to neuro-rehabilitation services which are available at all the recruiting sites. Despite this, we managed to fulfil pre-defined recruitment target and create a rich research database. Though treatment heterogeneity between centres did not affect the PRx interpretation, it could have affected the results seen with CPP, CPPopt and ICP. Most of the analyses in this paper were performed with one value of PRx per patient representing the overall state of CA considering either the first 24 h or full monitoring period. Future explorations should evaluate whether there is a dose response of impaired CA or ICP with outcome. Due to the sample size, we did not adjust for multiple comparisons within the univariable analysis but explored the association between PRx and outcome using different multivariable methods. In this first multicentre, prospective study in pTBI, our analyses confirm the independent association of PRx with outcome irrespective of the initial disease severity, CPP and ICP, and differences in treatment between centres. We demonstrate a dynamic temporal trend of PRx and have identified optimal PRx thresholds for mortality and dichotomised outcome. Our results support PRx monitoring with a step towards precision medicine and a potential for fine tuning treatment targets to improve neuro-outcome in pTBI. Evidence generated in our study will help with further external validation and phase II trials in children. Contributors Concept and design: Agrawal, Czosnyka, Smielewski, Hutchinson, Ganrett, Young, Maw, Watson. Acquisition of data: Agrawal, Cabeleira, Placek, White, Daubney, Kayani, O''Donnell, Pathan, Krishnan, Bangalore, Sundararajan, Subramanian, Raffaj, Sarfatti, Lampareillo, Mayer, Ross. Analysis or interpretation: Bogli, Smith, Beqiri, Smielewski, Agrawal. Drafting of the manuscript: Agrawal, Bogli, Smith, Smielewski. Critical revision of the manuscript for important intellectual content: All authors. Statistical analysis: Bogli, Smith. Obtained funding: Agrawal, Smielewski, Czosnyka, Hutchinson. Supervision: Smielewski, Hutchinson. Verification of the underlying data : Agrawal, Smielewski, Bogli, Smith. All authors read and approved the final version of the manuscript. Agrawal and Smielewski have full access to all the data in the study, have independently verified the underlying data and take responsibility for the integrity of the data and accuracy of the data analysis. Data sharing statement As the project is limited to a rare disease in children with specific physiological data collection, we would welcome applications from the researchers contributing to the database. The applications would be considered on individual basis and assessed by the steering group of the project based upon their scientific merits. For the duration of the project, the data will be available to all the researchers contributing to the database. Afterwards, applications will be considered on individual basis and assessed by the steering group of the project based upon their scientific merits from the researchers outside the project. Declaration of interests This research was supported by the National Institute of Health Research (NIHR) Cambridge Biomedical Research Centre (NIHR203312∗) and received support from the NIHR Clinical Research Network. The views expressed are those of the authors and not necessarily those of the NIHR or the Department of Health and Social Care. ICM+ is a software licensed by Cambridge Enterprise Ltd. Marek Czosnyka and Peter Smielewski have a financial interest in a part of licensing fee; the licensing fee was waived for this study. Stefan Y. Bogli is supported by the Swiss National Science Foundation (grant number: 210839/225270). Claudia Smith is supported by the Patrick & Margaret Flanagan Skye Cambridge Trust Scholarship. Erta Beqiri is supported by the Medical Research Council [grant number MR N013433-1] and by the Gates Cambridge Scholarship. Peter J. Hutchinson is supported by the National Institute for Health Research (NIHR) (Senior Investigator Award, Cambridge BRC, NIHR HRC for Brain Injury) and the Royal College of Surgeons of England. None of the other authors have any further declaration of interests. Acknowledgements We are indebted to all the patients and their families for participation in this study. We are grateful to the staff in all the participating PICUs for their valuable contribution to the study. We would also like to acknowledge the valuable contribution from Dr Ricardo G Branco towards the study protocol. We would also like to acknowledge Swiss National Science Foundation funded the postdoctoral research period for Stefan Y. Bogli, within which period the analysis was performed. Footnotes Appendix A Supplementary data related to this article can be found at https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eclinm.2025.103077 . Contributor Information Shruti Agrawal, Email: sa882@cam.ac.uk. the STARSHIP study team: Carly Tooke , Caroline Payne , Holly Belfield , Amisha Mistry , Collette Spencer , Claire Jennings , Lara Bunni , Laura Anderson , Emily Morgan , Melanie James , Rebecca Beckley , Tahnima Khatun , Hafiza Khatun , Olivia Nugent , Richard Aldridge , Ruth Morgan , Julie Morcombe , Martin Quinton , Catherine Postlethwaite , Jenny Pond , Jessica Cutler , and Caitlin Oxford Appendix A. Supplementary data Study Group mmc1.docx (18.2KB, docx) Supplement 1 mmc2.pdf (737.5KB, pdf) Supplement 2 mmc3.docx (2.8MB, docx) References 1. Dewan M.C., Wellons C., III, Bonfield C.M. Epidemiology of global pediatric traumatic brain injury: qualitative review. World Neurosurg. 2016;91:497–509. doi: 10.1016/j.wneu.2016.03.045. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. The L. The burden of traumatic brain injury in children. Lancet. 2018;391(10123):813. doi: 10.1016/S0140-6736(18)30547-6. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Kochanek P.M., Tasker R.C., Carney N., et al. Guidelines for the management of pediatric severe traumatic brain injury, third edition: update of the brain trauma foundation guidelines. Pediatr Crit Care Med. 2019;20(3S Suppl 1):S1–S82. doi: 10.1097/PCC.0000000000001735. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Udomphorn Y., 