INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1457,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5073653','Order effects in high stakes undergraduate examinations: an analysis of 5 years of administrative data in one UK medical school.','Burt J, Abel G, Barclay M, Evans R, Benson J, Gurnell M.','2016','quarantining advantaging disadvantaging watches collusion leaking quarantining scee pseudonymous scee scee scee gynae paeds scee gynae paeds scee gynae paeds gynae paeds scee scee scee scee gynae paeds scee gynae paeds scee scee scee scee gynae paeds gynae paeds scee contextualised scee scee scee scee scee scee gynae paeds gynae paeds scee scee scee gynae paeds gynae paeds scee scee cancel uncontrollable fetched moods quarantining jenni jenniaburt jbu jbe jbu jbe organises subdean jbu jab35 medschl newble gleeson gormley ulster colliver barrows rutala witzke leko clauser equating collusion champlain battles mcintire schoonheim muijtjens habets gispert rue doig harasym fick s96 kilminster ahse bassi colacito fulghieri financ forgas unkelbach unobtrusive howarth adbulghani ponnamperuma','Abstract Objective To investigate the association between student performance in undergraduate objective structured clinical examinations (OSCEs) and the examination schedule to which they were assigned to undertake these examinations. Design Analysis of routinely collected data. Setting One UK medical school. Participants 2331 OSCEs of 3 different types (obstetrics OSCE, paediatrics OSCE and simulated clinical encounter examination OSCE) between 2009 and 2013. Students were not quarantined between examinations. Outcomes (1) Pass rates by day examination started, (2) pass rates by day station undertaken and (3) mean scores by day examination started. Results We found no evidence that pass rates differed according to the day on which the examination was started by a candidate in any of the examinations considered (p>0.1 for all). There was evidence (p=0.013) that students were more likely to pass individual stations on the second day of the paediatrics OSCE (OR 1.27, 95% CI 1.05 to 1.54). In the cases of the simulated clinical encounter examination and the obstetrics and gynaecology OSCEs, there was no (p=0.42) or very weak evidence (p=0.099), respectively, of any such variation in the probability of passing individual stations according to the day they were attempted. There was no evidence that mean scores varied by day apart from the paediatric OSCE, where slightly higher scores were achieved on the second day of the examination. Conclusions There is little evidence that different examination schedules have a consistent effect on pass rates or mean scores: students starting the examinations later were not consistently more or less likely to pass or score more highly than those starting earlier. The practice of quarantining students to prevent communication with (and subsequent unfair advantage for) subsequent examination cohorts is unlikely to be required. Keywords: MEDICAL EDUCATION & TRAINING, Educational Measurement, Clinical Competence Strengths and limitations of this study. The study data set, containing 5 years of data from three separate objective structured clinical examinations (OSCE) in one medical school, is the largest to date to be analysed to investigate the impact of examination schedule on examination performance. The varying nature of examinations between medical schools makes it challenging to conduct cross-institutional analyses, but the inclusion of only one institution may limit the generalisability of our findings. Ideally, we would consider the impact of examiners on variations in examination performance according to day: however, this is not straightforward to satisfactorily accomplish as examiner effects may be confounded by subject and station difficulty, and could change across different years. Introduction High stakes undergraduate medical assessments determine whether a student may or may not progress to medical qualification. As such, it is essential that the examination processes are valid, reliable, transparent, and fair. Medical schools worldwide use objective structured clinical examinations (OSCEs) 1 2 in this context to assess students’ clinical and communication skills. Such examinations aspire to ensure robust procedures by requiring all candidates to undertake the same clinical stations, to be completed within predetermined time limits and assessed using the same marking scheme. 2 To accommodate all candidates for these examinations, many medical schools are compelled to run an OSCE repeatedly, with students scheduled to undertake the examination in sequential groups over a number of days. This leads to two concerns for students and medical schools. The first concern relates to security breaches: it is possible that students in earlier scheduled examination times might tell those in later times about the content of the examination, either advantaging (or potentially disadvantaging) those students who come later. To reduce the potential for this, some medical schools routinely quarantine earlier student examination groupings from later ones until an overall OSCE is complete. This involves challenges for the medical schools in accommodating quarantined students (especially if an OSCE runs over more than 1 day) and in ensuring effective quarantine, such as restricting the use of smart phones/watches and other mobile devices. In addition, it imposes a significant burden on quarantined students, which is not shared by their colleagues in later cohorts. With OSCEs’ focus on assessment of skills, rather than knowledge, some have argued that the short time lag between examination groupings is insufficient for any briefing about examination content to lead to an improvement in the performance of candidates in later examination times. 3 4 For example, a 1989 study reported no evidence of information sharing affecting the performance among US fourth year medical students in OSCEs (which, in this instance, took place over a period of several weeks). 5 Others remain concerned about the likely impact of security breaches. 6 Collusion between third year medical students on the content of OSCEs has previously been identified through monitoring of discussions between students on an electronic discussion board, in which concerns were expressed about this taking place. 7 An experimental study modelling the effect of a severe security breach (ie, the leaking of checklists or the provision of coaching for three of the six clinical stations) found that students who had received additional information outperformed the control group by around 7%. 8 The second concern relates to whether examination grouping, in itself and independent of possible security breaches, may influence a candidate''s performance. One explanation for this is that students in different examination groups may perform variably as a result of psychological pressures relating to the timing of their examination or changes in aspects of examination process between groups. An additional or alternative explanation is that examiners’ scoring may change over time, becoming either more or less generous across examination groupings. The core question here is whether there is something inherent about being in the first versus subsequent examination groups that places candidates at an advantage or disadvantage in comparison to their peers. Small but inconsistent effects have previously been found for the time of day (morning or afternoon) and day on which second-year medical students undertake OSCEs in a US medical school, assessed on a pass/fail basis. 9 However, other research has demonstrated little impact of the day of the examination (in dental OSCEs in the Netherlands, 10 in undergraduate medical OSCEs in Spain 11 ) or the timing of the examination (in undergraduate medical OSCEs in Canada 12 ). These studies were relatively small in scale (with 772, 9 463, 10 172 11 and 69 12 students, respectively) and examined a variety of OSCE set-ups, including the use of parallel streams 12 and non-consecutive days with no quarantining of students. 10 Given continuing concern about this issue, there is a need for larger-scale and up-to-date examination of the impact of examination order on performance in modern OSCE settings. The purpose of this study was, therefore, to investigate the association between the scores achieved by students in high-stakes OSCEs in one UK medical school, the University of Cambridge School of Clinical Medicine, and the examination grouping to which they were assigned. Methods We conducted a retrospective analysis of three high-stakes OSCEs conducted in the School of Clinical Medicine between 2009 and 2013. Students in sequential examination groupings were not quarantined in any of these examinations. Data were retrieved by a single member of the research team, with the assistance of the School examinations administration team. Student and examiner identities were removed on data retrieval and replaced by a unique, anonymised study identifier. Our data covered: Year 5 obstetrics and gynaecology OSCE: for this examination, individual candidates sat all stations in 1 day and in each year the OSCE was completed over 2 consecutive days. Year 5 paediatrics OSCE: for this examination, individual candidates sat all stations in 1 day and in each year the OSCE was completed over 2 consecutive days. Year 6 simulated clinical encounter examination (SCEE) OSCE: for this examination, individual candidates sat stations over 2 days and in each year the OSCE was completed in 3 days. Candidates were evenly split between sitting stations on days 1 and 2, days 1 and 3, and days 2 and 3, with the same number of candidates taking each station on each day. For all OSCEs, the content of each station, including the question wording, did not vary between circuits or between days. Data For each OSCE examination, data were obtained for individual candidates and individual stations, alongside the overall pass mark for each examination in each year. Data were obtained for each OSCE for 2009–2013. For each candidate, we knew whether they passed the examination, their mean score, whether they passed each individual station of the examination and their score for each individual station. We had further information on the subject of each station, a pseudonymous code for the examiner for each station and the day and time of each station. There were no missing data. In the case of all three examinations, in order to pass, students were required (1) to meet the overall examination pass mark, as defined by the borderline group method, 13 and (2) to additionally pass a minimum of 50% of individual stations: this ensures that poor performance in several stations cannot be compensated for by exceptionally high performance in one or two other stations. Statistical analysis We used a series of models to investigate various aspects of potential order effects in OSCEs, which we applied separately to each of the three examinations under consideration ((1) Year 5 obstetrics and gynaecology OSCE, (2) Year 5 paediatrics OSCE and (3) Year 6 simulated clinical encounter examination OSCE). Each model included data from all candidates and all years for that OSCE. Our primary question was whether the probability of passing each OSCE varied according to the day on which the examination was undertaken. We used a logistic regression model, adjusting for the year of examination, to investigate whether the probability of passing each OSCE varied according to the day on which each candidate started the examination (Model 1). We recognise, however, that the very high overall pass rate (>97%) limits the power of this approach. For example, if the true pass rate on day 1 was 96.5%, and the true pass rate on day 2 was 98.5%, the power to detect this difference, an OR of 2.4, would be <50%. Our second question (less limited by power than our primary question) was whether the probability of passing individual stations varied according to the day on which those stations were attempted. This differs from Model 1, which considered the first day on which candidates attempted any stations (remembering that, in some OSCEs, candidates were required to be examined over 2 days). We used a mixed-effects logistic regression model adjusting for year (fixed effect) and for clustering of individual station results within candidates using a random effect (Model 2). Our third question was whether any observed effects of the day on which stations were attempted were consistent from year to year: to consider this, we augmented Model 2 by including an interaction between day and the year-cohort (Model 3). The above analyses focus on the nature of these OSCEs as pass or fail exams; as long as candidates do well enough to pass, their actual overall score is not of great importance. However, in the University of Cambridge Medical School, as elsewhere, candidates may be awarded a Pass with Distinction if they score particularly highly, with the potential for subsequent impact on their career opportunities. We, therefore, used a linear regression model to investigate whether there were differences in the overall mean score between start days of the OSCE (as in Model 1), adjusting for year (Model 4). Results Between 2009 and 2013, 770 candidates sat a Year 5 obstetrics and gynaecology OSCE, 771 candidates sat a Year 5 paediatrics OSCE, and 790 candidates sat a Year 6 SCEE OSCE at Cambridge. Around 150–160 candidates sat each examination in each year. Overall pass rates were very high: of 2331 total examinations taken across all three OSCEs, 2273 (97%) were passed ( table 1 ). Pass rates appeared broadly similar across start days, as did mean scores. Pass rates for individual stations were lower, with passes given for 17 421 (87%) of the 20 078 stations taken. Table 1. Descriptive statistics for the SCEE, stage 2 obstetrics and gynaecology and stage 2 paediatrics OSCE results, 2009–2013 Start day 1 Start day 2 Total Candidates Failed (%) Passed (%) Failed (%) Passed (%) Failed (%) Passed (%) SCEE 14 (2.7) 514 (97.3) 4 (1.5) 258 (98.5) 18 (2.3) 772 (97.7) Obs and Gynae 14 (3.7) 369 (96.3) 17 (4.4) 370 (95.6) 31 (4.0) 739 (96.0) Paeds 7 (1.8) 383 (98.2) 2 (0.5) 379 (99.5) 9 (1.2) 762 (98.8) Start day 1 Start day 2 Total Mean score (%) Mean (SD) Mean (SD) Mean (SD) SCEE 70.8 (7.8) 70.7 (7.7) 70.7 (7.7) Obs and Gynae 69.5 (7.9) 69.3 (8.8) 69.4 (8.3) Paeds 68.1 (8.3) 70.0 (8.0) 69.0 (8.2) Day 1 Day 2 Day 3 Total Stations Failed (%) Passed (%) Failed (%) Passed (%) Failed (%) Passed (%) Failed (%) Passed (%) SCEE 363 (13.8) 2277 (86.3) 353 (13.4) 2277 (86.6) 323 (12.3) 2307 (87.7) 1039 (13.1) 6867 (86.9) Obs and Gynae 404 (14.2) 2433 (85.8) 464 (16.2) 2401 (83.8) 868 (15.2) 4834 (84.8) Paeds 418 (12.8) 2852 (87.2) 332 (10.4) 2868 (89.6) 750 (11.6) 5720 (88.4) Open in a new tab Gynae, gynaecology; Obs, obstetrics; Paeds, paediatrics; SCEE, simulated clinical encounter examination. Probability of passing each examination according to start day (Model 1) We found no evidence that pass rates differed according to the day on which the examination was started by a candidate (p>0.1, table 2 ). However, as noted above, the high pass rates limit the power of this analysis to detect any notable difference between days (reflected in the wide CIs, particularly for the SCEE and paediatrics OSCEs). Table 2. Probability of passing the OSCE for the SCEE, stage 2 obstetrics and gynaecology and stage 2 paediatrics OSCE results, 2009–2013 (model 1) SCEE Obs and Gynae Paeds Factor OR (95% CI) p Value OR (95% CI) p Value OR (95% CI) p Value First day Day 1 Reference 0.327 Reference 0.575 Reference 0.121 Day 2 1.76 (0.57 to 5.42) 0.81 (0.39 to 1.68) 3.49 (0.72 to 16.94) Year 2009 * 0.254 Reference 0.002 Reference 0.121 2010 1.41 (0.23 to 8.57) 1.52 (0.62 to 3.72) 1.12 (0.15 to 8.06) 2011 0.37 (0.09 to 1.40) 4.77 (1.32 to 17.26) 2.26 (0.20 to 25.26) 2012 0.61 (0.14 to 2.61) 2.76 (0.95 to 8.05) 2.19 (0.20 to 24.45) 2013 Reference 7.13 (1.57 to 32.42) 0.73 (0.12 to 4.44) Open in a new tab *All candidates in 2009 passed the SCEE OSCE, so this year was omitted from the model. Gynae, gynaecology; Obs, obstetrics; OSCE, objective structured clinical examinations; Paeds, paediatrics; SCEE, simulated clinical encounter examination. Probability of passing individual stations according to the day on which those stations were attempted (Model 2) We found no evidence of a difference in the probability of passing an individual station in the SCEE OSCE according to the day on which that station was attempted (p=0.42, table 3 ). For the obstetrics and gynaecology OSCE, we found very weak evidence of a difference according to the day on which each station was attempted (p=0.1, table 3 ), with a lower probability of passing an individual station on the second day (OR 0.85, 95% CI 0.70 to 1.03). Finally, in the paediatrics OSCE, we found evidence of a difference according to the day on which each station was attempted (p=0.01, table 2 ), with a higher probability of passing an individual station on the second day (OR 1.27, 95% CI 1.05 to 1.54). Table 3. Probability of passing individual stations for the SCEE, stage 2 obstetrics and gynaecology and stage 2 paediatrics OSCE results, 2009–2013 (model 2) SCEE Obs and Gynae Paeds Factor OR (95% CI) p Value OR (95% CI) p Value OR (95% CI) p Value Day of station Day 1 Reference 0.424 Reference 0.099 Reference 0.013 Day 2 1.09 (0.92 to 1.30) 0.85 (0.70 to 1.03) 1.27 (1.05 to 1.54) Day 3 1.12 (0.94 to 1.34) Year 2009 Reference <0.001 Reference 0.038 Reference 0.524 2010 0.78 (0.58 to 1.04) 1.19 (0.88 to 1.62) 0.85 (0.63 to 1.16) 2011 0.63 (0.48 to 0.83) 1.31 (0.97 to 1.78) 1.01 (0.74 to 1.38) 2012 0.98 (0.84 to 1.51) 1.48 (1.09 to 2.01) 0.90 (0.67 to 1.23) 2013 1.13 (0.84 to 1.51) 1.55 (1.14 to 2.10) 0.81 (0.61 to 1.09) Candidate SD of random effect 0.80 (0.70 to 0.92) 0.80 (0.68 to 0.93) 0.71 (0.59 to 0.86) 95% reference range† 23.18 (15.54 to 36.66) 22.76 (14.36 to 39.06) 16.48 (10.22 to 29.33) Open in a new tab †Odds ratio comparing the probability of passing for a candidate better than 97.5% of other candidates to that of a candidate worse than 97.5% of other candidates, estimated using standard deviation of the random effect (95% reference range = e 2×1.96×SD RE ). Gynae, gynaecology; Obs, obstetrics; Paeds, paediatrics; SCEE, simulated clinical encounter examination. The size of these differences can be contextualised by comparing them to the year-to year differences and to the variability between candidates. The differences between the day on which the stations were attempted were smaller than the differences between year-cohorts ( table 3 ), and much smaller than the variability between candidates (captured by the random effect). Using the SD of the random effect we estimate ORs comparing the ‘best’ candidates (defined as those who are better at passing OSCE stations than 97.5% of other candidates) to the ‘worst’ candidates (defined as those worse at passing OSCE stations than 97.5% of other candidates at between 16.48 (paediatrics OSCE) and 23.18 (SCEE OSCE). Probability of passing individual stations according to the day on which those stations were attempted, within each examination year (Model 3) All three OSCEs had at least 1 year in which we found a large, highly statistically significant difference in the odds of passing individual stations according to the day on which stations were attempted ( table 4 ). For the SCEE and the obstetrics and gynaecology OSCEs, the higher probability of passing on a particular day in 1 year were matched by a lower probability of passing on that day in a different year. For example, in the 2010 SCEE, candidates’ odds of passing stations on day 2 were twice those on day 1, but in the 2012 SCEE candidates’ odds of passing stations on day 2 were half those on day 1. For the paediatrics OSCE there was a significant increase in pass rate for day two (p<0.001) in 2010 only: for all other years there was no clear evidence of a difference in pass rates between days. Table 4. Probability of passing individual stations by day within individual years for the SCEE, stage 2 obstetrics and gynaecology and stage 2 paediatrics OSCE results, 2009–2013 (model 3) SCEE* Obs and Gynae* Paeds** Factor OR (95% CI) p Value OR (95% CI) p Value OR (95% CI) p Value 2009 Day of station Day 1 Reference 0.717 Reference 0.559 Reference 0.626 Day 2 1.05 (0.70 to 1.56) 0.88 (0.58 to 1.35) 1.11 (0.72 to 1.72) Day 3 1.18 (0.78 to 1.79) 2010 Day of station Day 1 Reference <0.001 Reference 0.539 Reference <0.001 Day 2 2.08 (1.38 to 3.15) 1.14 (0.75 to 1.73) 2.15 (1.39 to 3.31) Day 3 1.09 (0.75 to 1.59) 2011 Day of station Day 1 Reference 0.736 Reference 0.011 Reference 0.274 Day 2 1.05 (0.74 to 1.50) 1.74 (1.14 to 2.67) 1.28 (0.82, 1.98) Day 3 1.15 (0.68 to 1.62) 2012 Day of station Day 1 Reference <0.001 Reference <0.001 Reference 0.988 Day 2 0.53 (0.36 to 0.78) 0.48 (0.31 to 0.74) 1.00 (0.65 to 1.53) Day 3 1.05 (0.68 to 1.62) 2013 Day of station Day 1 Reference 0.243 Reference 0.003 Reference 0.615 Day 2 1.45 (0.94 to 2.23) 0.52 (0.34 to 0.80) 1.11 (0.74 to 1.65) Day 3 1.19 (0.78 to 1.80) Candidate SD of random effect 0.81 (0.70 to 0.93) 0.76 (0.64 to 0.90) 0.71 (0.58 to 0.85) 95% reference range† 23.71 (15.84 to 37.65) 19.75 (12.46 to 34.07) 15.86 (9.82 to 28.32) Open in a new tab *Combined p-value for day-year interaction: p<0.001. **Combined p-value for day-year interaction: p=0.103. †Odds ratio comparing the probability of passing for a candidate better than 97.5% of other candidates to that of a candidate worse than 97.5% of other candidates, estimated using the standard deviation of the random effect (95% reference range = e 2×1.96×SD RE ). Gynae, gynaecology; Obs, obstetrics; Paeds, paediatrics; SCEE, simulated clinical encounter examination. Differences in overall mean scores between start days of the OSCE, adjusting for year (model 4) Candidates who sat the paediatrics OSCE on the second day of the examination tended to have higher scores, with a mean score difference of 1.8 (95% CI 0.8 to 2.8) ( table 5 ). We note, however, that this is small compared to the SD of paediatrics OSCE scores (8.2). Table 5. Mean difference in overall score by day and year for the SCEE, stage 2 obstetrics and gynaecology and stage 2 paediatrics OSCE results, 2009–2013 (model 4) SCEE Obs and Gynae Paeds Factor Difference (95% CI) p Value Difference (95% CI) p Value Difference (95% CI) p Value First day Day 1 Reference 0.858 Reference 0.716 Reference <0.001 Day 2 −0.09 (−1.13 to 0.94) −0.21 (−1.33 to 0.91) 1.78 (0.79 to 2.76) Year 2009 Reference <0.001 Reference <0.001 Reference <0.001 2010 1.64 (0.008 to 3.20) 2.47 (0.67 to 4.27) −2.36 (−3.94 to −0.78) 2011 −1.56 (−3.09 to −0.03) −0.30 (−2.10 to 1.49) −10.35 (−11.93 to −8.77) 2012 5.44 (3.92 to 6.96) −0.05 (−1.85 to 1.76) −9.30 (−10.89 to −7.71) 2013 −4.56 (−6.09 to −3.02) −5.10 (−6.90 to −3.30) −9.09 (−10.67 to −7.51) Open in a new tab Gynae, gynaecology; Obs, obstetrics; Paeds, paediatrics; SCEE, simulated clinical encounter examination. There was no evidence that mean scores on either the SCEE or obstetrics and gynaecology OSCE varied between candidates starting on the first and second days of the examination ( table 5 ). Discussion While our analyses identified some potential order effects in the OSCEs under investigation, these were inconsistent in direction across the three examinations and relatively small. For all OSCEs, there was no evidence that overall pass rates varied according to the day on which the examination was started, although our certainty is limited by the very high overall pass rates. We did not seek to see if overall order of examinations taken made a difference; however, if this had been the case, we would expect it to have been highlighted in our analysis by the day the examination was started. There was some evidence for variations in pass rates for individual stations according to the day on which stations were attempted. In particular, we found that in a number of years, there were strong differences in the chance of passing individual stations according to when they were taken. However, these effects were highly inconsistent across years and tended to cancel each other out when considering the 5 years of data together. Only the paediatric OSCE provided any substantive evidence of an order effect over the 5-year period, and even then it was dominated by a single year. The inconsistencies between examinations and years suggest that differences were unlikely to represent a true order effect, but rather other factors that varied between years. These could potentially be attributable to different examiners, but could also be attributable to uncontrollable factors such as the weather or the traffic (see below). It is also noteworthy that the differences by day were smaller than the year-to-year differences and much smaller than the differences between candidates. A particular strength of this analysis is the inclusion of large numbers of candidates and stations, across 5 years of the conduct of three separate examinations with no differences in content between days for each examination within each year. The completeness and level of detail of data available on candidates, stations, and examinations enabled us to undertake a comprehensive study of the association between the time of examination and overall pass rates and mean scores. The varying nature of examinations between medical schools makes it challenging to conduct cross-institutional analyses, although the inclusion of only one institution may limit the generalisability of our findings. We acknowledge that differences between examiners may explain some of the observed differences between days, and ideally we would consider the impact of potential confounding by examiner. However, this is not straightforward to satisfactorily accomplish: examiner effects may be confounded by subject and station difficulty, and could change across different years. Additionally, the simulated patients and patients used within the examinations may vary between circuits and days; we were not able to investigate the potential impact of this, as we did not hold information about the simulated patients and patients involved in these examinations. However, we note that all simulated patients are trained to a high standard and discuss each station in detail in advance to minimise variation in performance. Our findings reflect those of the next largest analyses of OSCEs conducted on this issue to date, in which the effect of day and time of examination was examined over 4 years of second-year medical pass/fail examinations (1990–1993) in one US medical school. 9 Here, while differences in the pass rate were identified according to the day of examination and the time of examination (morning or afternoon), none of these were consistent between years. 9 The authors concluded that there was little evidence that test security between repeated examinations was a concern. 9 Two other studies examining the effect of day on OSCE pass rates found no strong evidence of variation: one, an examination of dental OSCEs in 463 students across 4 days of the week, 10 the second involving 172 final year medical students from three medical schools across 8 days. 11 We can only speculate for the causes of inconsistent within-year, by-day variations in examination performance in our analyses. However, we would suggest that issues such as variations in the weather according to examination days are not entirely far-fetched as potential explanations. For example, psychological experiments have demonstrated that weather may affect a wide range of moods and behaviours, including risk-aversion, 14 memory 15 and concentration, 16 all of which might affect the performance of either students or examiners. The variations in the odds of passing between years are more reasonably likely to be as a result of differences in cohort ability. These findings demonstrate clearly that there is little need to quarantine students across different cohorts of OSCE examinations. Such quarantining is recommended in some quarters, 17 yet it is costly in time and money. It is apparent that, even though students in later cohorts may have the opportunity to discuss and review examination content with those who have already undertaken the examination, such discussions—if they occur—do not significantly affect the performance in examinations. Acknowledgments The authors thank staff at the University of Cambridge Clinical School for all their assistance with data collection and entry. Footnotes Twitter: Follow Jenni Burt at @jenniaburt Contributors: JBu designed the study, oversaw data collection, contributed to the interpretation of the data as well as drafted and revised the paper. GA designed the study, wrote the statistical analysis plan, contributed to the interpretation of the data and revised the paper. MB analysed the data, and drafted and revised the paper. RE carried out data collection and data entry, and commented on draft versions of the paper. JBe designed the study, oversaw data collection, contributed to the interpretation of the data and revised the paper. MG contributed to the design of the study and interpretation of the data, and revised the paper. JBu is the guarantor. Funding: This work was funded by the University of Cambridge School of Clinical Medicine. The authors experienced no influence from the funding institution regarding the execution and analyses of this study, the interpretation of the data or the decision to submit the study findings. Competing interests: JBe is Director of the GP Education Group, which organises Primary Care teaching in the School of Clinical Medicine. From 2003 to 2013, he was an Associate Dean in the School of Clinical Medicine. MG is Clinical SubDean in the School of Clinical Medicine and has been Assessment Lead since 2011. Ethics approval: The study was approved in this form by the University of Cambridge Psychology Research Ethics Committee. Provenance and peer review: Not commissioned; externally peer reviewed. Data sharing statement: Statistical code and data set are available from the authors on request: contact JBu (jab35@medschl.cam.ac.uk). References 1. Newble D. Techniques for measuring clinical competence: objective structured clinical examinations. Med Educ 2004;38:199–203. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Harden RM, Gleeson FA. Assessment of clinical competence using an objective structured clinical examination (OSCE). Med Educ 1979;13:41–54. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Gormley G. Summative OSCEs in undergraduate medical education. Ulster Med J 2011;80:127–32. [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Colliver JA, Barrows HS, Vu NV et al. Test security in examinations that use standardized-patient cases at one medical school. Acad Med 1991;66:279–82. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Rutala PJ, Witzke DB, Leko EO et al. Sharing of information by students in an objective structured clinical examination. Arch Intern Med 1991;151:541–4. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Swanson DB, Clauser BE, Case SM. Clinical skills assessment with standardized patients in high-stakes tests: a framework for thinking about score precision, equating, and security. Adv Health Sci Educ Theory Pract 1999;4:67–106. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Parks R, Warren PM, Boyd KM et al. The Objective Structured Clinical Examination and student collusion: marks do not tell the whole truth. J Med Ethics 2003;32:734–8. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. De Champlain AF, MacMillan MK, Margolis MJ et al. Modeling the effects of security breaches on students’ performances on a large-scale standardized patient examination. Acad Med 1999;74(10 Suppl):S49–51. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Battles JB, Carpenter JL, McIntire DD et al. Analyzing and adjusting for variables in a large-scale standardized-patient examination. Acad Med 1994;69:370–6. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Schoonheim-Klein M, Muijtjens A, Habets L et al. On the reliability of a dental OSCE, using SEM: effect of different days. Eur J Dent Educ 2008;12:131–7. doi:10.1111/j.1600-0579.2008.00507.x [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Gispert R, Rué M, Roma J et al. Gender, sequence of cases and day effects on clinical skills assessment with standardized patients. Med Educ 1999;33:499–503. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. Doig CJ, Harasym PH, Fick GH et al. The effects of examiner background, station organization, and time of exam on OSCE scores assessing undergraduate medical students’ physical examination skills. Acad Med 2000;75(10 Suppl):S96–8. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. Kilminster S, Roberts T. Standard setting for OSCEs: trial of borderline approach. Adv Health Sci Educ Theory Pract 2004;9:201–9. doi:10.1023/B:AHSE.0000038208.06099.9a [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 14. Bassi A, Colacito R, Fulghieri P. ‘O Sole Mio: an experimental analysis of weather and risk attitudes in financial decisions. Rev Financ Stud 2013;26:1824–52. [ Google Scholar ] 15. Forgas JP, Goldenberg L, Unkelbach C. Can bad weather improve your memory? An unobtrusive field study of natural mood effects on real-life memory. J Exp Soc Psychol 2009;45:254–7. [ Google Scholar ] 16. Howarth E, Hoffman MS. A multidimensional approach to the relationship between mood and weather. Br J Psychol 1984;751):15–23. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 17. Adbulghani HM, Ponnamperuma G, Amin Z. An essential guide to developing, implementing, and evaluating objective structured clinical examination (OSCE). Singapore: World Scientific Publishing Ltd., 2015. [ Google Scholar ]',107,'2025-10-10 08:33:00','2025-10-10 08:33:00');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1458,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9121434','Paediatric neuroblastoma presenting as an asymptomatic abdominal mass: a report on the importance of a complete clinical examination with a view to a timely diagnosis and therapeutic guidance in paediatric oncology.','Granja C, Mota L.','2022','leukaemias toddler toddler rhinorrhoea capsulated hilum hyperreflective capsulated hilum ectasia excretory medially homolateral permeable scintigram exuberant i123 medially i123 vanylmandelic vma scintigram i123 permeable 1p36 ganglionic inrgss idrfs carbo cado idrfs cado i123 scintigram i123 connotation leukaemias unaffordable socialisation asymmetries illiteracy kindergarten haematuria fbc mesoblastic nephroma haematuria xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis fbc leucocytosis haemangiomas distention ecchymosis bream sweats fullness fbc leukaemias enigmatic regress ganglioneuroma metastasise vma homovanillic hva vma hva vma hva intrabdominal complain renovascular bloating inrgss unaffordable homogeneously brodeur enigma nrc1014 maris hogarty bagatell mcguffin mogul inconspicuous anbl0531 mlakar jurkovic mlakar 11q s12943 gatta botta eurocare gloria registo oncologico tumores populacao residente 0518_publicacao ron_2018 feusner s0031 infantil juvenil norma dgs dgs documentos publicacoes atuacao infantil juvenil wonca definicao europeia geral geral wonca woncaeurope 20definition 20in 20portuguese estatisticos pipop pipop amigos cancro pediatrico estatisticos brodeur marris pizzo heck whittle semmes thwe e713 monclair brodeur ambros inrg inrg nganga e000397 bmjpo referenciacao secundarios atraves processo informatizado portuguesa geral rpmgf v35i4','Abstract Neuroblastoma (NB) is the most common extracranial solid tumour in children after lymphoblastic leukaemias, brain tumours and lymphomas. We report a case of an NB in a previously healthy toddler. Though she had no abdominal complaints, on physical examination, she had a palpable mass identified over the right upper portion of the abdomen. Imaging studies demonstrated a heterogeneous tumour with an irregular capsule and visible calcifications. After initial diagnosis, the patient underwent chemotherapy and was subjected to a radical resection of the tumour 4 months later. One-year follow-up of the patient showed no recurrence. In children, oncological diseases are often asymptomatic or have non-specific symptoms, and in remote locations where specialised/paediatric services may not be available, the importance of medical training in paediatrics is highlighted, with emphasis on performing a complete clinical examination, namely abdominal palpation. The role of timely referral will be evident in such a situation. Keywords: Healthcare improvement and patient safety, Medical management, Medical education, Paediatric oncology Background Abdominal tumours can be detected as an asymptomatic abdominal mass during regular visits or by the parents themselves. Abdominal masses in children can have many causes, but they are usually benign conditions. Asymptomatic abdominal tumours, on the other hand, present more frequently in children. The child’s age, symptoms, location of the mass and test results provide vital clues to the underlying aetiology and can help guide an appropriate assessment and consultation. Neuroblastoma (NB) develops from sympathetic nervous system primordial cells produced from the neural crest. NB is the second most frequent solid tumour in children, accounting for 7%–10% of all malignancies in children. 1 2 Furthermore, during childhood, it is the most prevalent extracranial malignant solid tumour. 3 NB tumours are most typically found in the abdomen and are most often found in the adrenal gland. 4 5 While the 5 year survival percentage for low-risk and intermediate-risk NB patients approaches 100%, the 5 year survival rate for high-risk NB patients is less than 50%. 6 The clinical presentation can be fairly varied due to the large variety of primary locations and stages. The mass in abdominal tumours can be asymptomatic and is frequently discovered by chance during routine physical examination, as in this case, emphasising the significance of constantly performing a thorough patient check-up. Case presentation A previously healthy toddler was referred to our paediatric emergency department due to 8 days of upper respiratory infection, fever, cough and rhinorrhoea, without abdominal symptoms such as abdominal pain, abdominal swelling, vomiting, constipation or diarrhoea. There was no family history of related diseases, namely tumours or immunological diseases. On physical examination, a palpable mass about 5 cm in diameter was identified over the right upper portion of the abdomen. The mass was painless, solid, hard and seemed to be fixed in the peritoneal cavity. Ultrasonography disclosed a heterogeneous, capsulated retroperitoneal mass with calcifications, located at the level of the right renal hilum, measuring about 74 mm longest axis ( figure 1 ). Figure 1. Open in a new tab Ultrasonography demonstrating a heterogeneous retroperitoneal mass, predominantly hyperreflective, capsulated, with calcifications measuring about 74 mm longest axis, located at the level of the right renal hilum and causing slight ectasia of the respective excretory tree. Abdomen CT showed in the retroperitoneal topography, medially to the right kidney, a large oval solid mass, with regular well-defined contour and some calcifications. The mass shows relatively homogeneous enhancement measuring 77×49×67 mm. It induces lateral deviation of the right kidney and anterior deviation of the homolateral renal artery and vein, as well as the inferior vena cava, with no apparent invasion of these permeable structures. The scintigram shows an exuberant uptake of I123-MIBG by a mass located medially to the right kidney, congruent with the presence of neuroblastoma at this level. There are no other areas with abnormal uptake of I123-MIBG suggestive of secondary locations at other levels. The serum levels of lactic dehydrogenase (LDH) and serum neuron-specific enolase (NSE) were normal. The urine vanylmandelic acid (VMA) levels were elevated. In the scintigram, the iodine-123-metaiodobenzylguanidine (I123-MIBG) uptake was positive only in the paravertebral mass, not anywhere else, though about 10% of NB does not take up MIBG ( figure 1 ). Abdomen CT was also consistent with the diagnosis of NB, with additional evidence of lateral deviation of the right kidney and anterior deviation of the renal artery and vein on this side, as well as the inferior vena cava, with no apparent invasion of these permeable structures ( figure 1 ). Needle biopsy and pathological examination diagnosed the patient as having poorly differentiated NB, with NSE reactivity in the immunohistochemical study and without N-Myc gene amplification, 1p36 deletion or anaplastic lymphoma kinase (ALK) translocation. No metastasis to the bone marrow was detected. The final diagnosis was an NB of the right ganglionic chains, without identified metastases, stage 2 according to the International Neuroblastoma Staging System and stage L2 according to the International Neuroblastoma Risk Group Staging System (INRGSS), locoregional tumour with presence of one or more image-defined risk factors (IDRFs). The patient received four courses of chemotherapy, two cycles by combination of etoposide/carboplatin (VP/CARBO) and two cycles by combination of cyclophosphamide/doxorubicin/vincristine (CADO). Given the apparent lack of response with localised tumour with IDRFs, the patient received additional two courses of CADO followed by surgical resection. Surgical intervention with radical tumour resection was performed 5 months after diagnosis, without complications. Pathological identification of questionable surgical margins dictated the need for subsequent radiotherapy. The patient completed a total of 6 months of treatment, maintaining follow-up in paediatric oncology, with I123-MIBG, CT ( figure 2 ) and serial analytical evaluations. Figure 2. Open in a new tab On scintigram after chemotherapy and surgical recession, there is no evidence of abnormal I123-MIBG uptake areas suggestive of residual disease or secondary lesions. Chest, abdomen and pelvis CT without metastatic connotation alterations. Four-years follow-up of the patient showed no recurrence. Global health problem list The incidence of childhood cancer is about 400 000 diagnosed cases per year. The paediatric physical examination should be complete and not just based on specific complaints and/or guiding symptoms. The most common types of cancer diagnosed in children are leukaemias, brain tumours, lymphomas and solid tumours such as NB. Cancers in children are not always treated in the same manner as in adults, and paediatric oncology is a medical specialty focused on the care of children with cancer. In high-income countries, with organised health services and easier access to these specialists, the cure rate is around 80%. An important public health strategy is the diagnosis of cancer as early as possible, with a view to the best chance of successful treatment. When cancer treatment is delayed or unaffordable, there is a lower chance of survival, more difficult and time-consuming treatments, and higher overall care costs. Global health problem analysis The incidence of childhood cancer is about 400 000 diagnosed cases per year. The worldwide incidence of childhood cancer is about 400 000 diagnosed cases per year. 7 Paediatric oncology is, in numerical terms, a small portion of all oncology. In Portugal, around 400 new cases of paediatric cancer are diagnosed per year, with a survival rate of 80%. 8 The paediatric physical examination should be complete and not just based on specific complaints and/or guiding symptoms. The presence of palpable abdominal masses is one of the main forms of clinical presentation of solid tumours in children. 9 In Portugal, there is a national child surveillance programme carried out by primary care physicians, which aims to schedule appointments for key ages, corresponding to important events in the life of the baby, child or adolescent, such as the stages of physical, psychomotor and cognitive development and emotional, socialisation, food and schooling. The harmonisation of these consultations with the chronological scheme recommended in the National Vaccination Programme makes it possible to reduce the number of visits to health services and values anticipatory care as a factor for health promotion and disease prevention ( figure 3 ). 10 Figure 3. Open in a new tab Frequency of child surveillance consultations in Portugal. In Portugal, primary care physicians have a medical specialty dedicated to the provision of primary healthcare to the whole family from birth and to guide the patient to other specialists if their intervention is necessary. 11 The shortages and asymmetries in the distribution of professionals in primary healthcare are manifested in the diversity of human resources allocated to the performance of these activities, but the increase in the level of knowledge and motivation of families, together with the reduction of illiteracy and the improvement of living conditions, favour the development of the exercise of parenting and make it possible for parents and families to assume it, as a right and a duty; it is up to professionals to facilitate and promote it. Access to these health services, especially for rural communities, implies that teamwork needs to be increased also in the community and in the structures that support children and adolescents (kindergarten, school, leisure activities, sports organisations or associations, social security services and municipalities) as entities with competence in the field of childhood and, therefore, with responsibility for promoting health at these ages. 10 In view of this specific case, it is intended to highlight the importance of abdominal examination in children and the relevance in the early detection of abdominal tumours, often asymptomatic in children ( table 1 ). Table 1. Abdominal tumours by anatomical site—what to look for in children according to age Abdominal site Tumour name Typical presentation Typical age Helpful tests Renal masses Wilms tumour. Asymptomatic abdominal mass, haematuria and hypertension. Most common age 1–12 years, with a peak 2–5 years. Abdominal USS. Urine dipstick. Clotting. FBC. Urea and electrolytes. Congenital mesoblastic nephroma. Asymptomatic abdominal mass, antenatal diagnosis of abdominal mass. <3 months Abdominal USS. Renal cell carcinoma. Abdominal pain and haematuria. Exceptionally rare in childhood, more common than Wilms tumour (WT) in those >12 years. Abdominal USS. Xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis. Large abdominal mass. Any age. FBC— leucocytosis. Raised ESR Hepatic masses Hepatoblastoma. Right upper quadrant mass, failure to thrive and anaemia. Most common in children <5 years. Serum alfa-fetoprotein (AFP)—highly elevated. Liver USS. Hepatocellular carcinoma. Weight loss, anorexia and abdominal pain. Greatest incidence in children >10 years. AFP markedly elevated (sensitive, but non-specific marker). Benign liver masses: hepatic haemangiomas. Asymptomatic/abdominal mass. Age <6 months. Liver USS. Adrenal masses Adrenocortical carcinoma. Usually metastatic on presentation with symptoms of steroid hormone excess and abdominal distention. Rare malignancy, usually seen in those <5 years old. Abdominal USS. Neuroblastoma. Presentation depends on the primary disease site and degree of dissemination: weight loss, anorexia, unexplained fevers, bone pains, asymptomatic abdominal mass and periorbital ecchymosis. Greatest incidence in children <5 years old. Abdominal USS. Urinary catecholamines. Pelvic masses Germ cell tumour. Abdominal discomfort, mass palpable above the pelvic bream, palpable regional lymph nodes and precocious puberty. Older children and adolescents. Abdominal USS. Serum AFP and human chorionic gonadotropin. Lymph node masses Burkitt’s lymphoma (type of non-Hodgkin’s lymphoma). Usually short history of abdominal distension, abdominal mass, fever, weight loss and night sweats. Late childhood and adolescence (usually >6 years). Abdominal USS. LDH, bone profile, urea and electrolytes. Other rare tumours Gastrointestinal stromal tumour. Abdominal pain, fullness, dysphagia and weight loss. Rare in childhood and adolescence. Abdominal X-ray. Endoscopy. Colorectal cancer Per rectum bleeding, weight loss and change in bowel habit. Extremely rare in children and adolescence. Colonoscopy. Open in a new tab Note that the list is not exhaustive but provides some information on more commonly encountered malignancies. ESR, erythrocyte sedimentation rate; FBC, full blood count; LDH, lactate dehydrogenase; USS, ultrasound scan. The most common types of cancer diagnosed in children are leukaemias, brain tumours, lymphomas and solid tumours such as NB. NB is the fourth most frequent paediatric cancer in Portugal after leukaemia, central nervous system tumours and lymphoma, similar to international data. 12 13 Most of the diagnosis occur in the first 5 years of life, with a mean age at diagnosis of 19 months, and in this age group, NB is the most frequent cancer. In fact, the peak incidence occurs during the first year of life, falling to half after the second year and rarely affecting children after 10 years of age. 14 15 In children up to 15 years of age, the annual incidence, in Portugal and in Europe, is around 10–15 cases per million, with no variation by sex. 12 14 NB is found more frequently in the abdomen, located in the adrenal gland having an enigmatic behaviour, as it can spontaneously regress, differentiate into a benign ganglioneuroma or, in about half of the cases, metastasise with a worse prognosis. 4 16 The disparity of behaviour and prognosis correlates with age and stage at diagnosis, with tumour histopathology and with several genetic features of the tumour, of which N-Myc oncogene amplification is the best recognised. 16 The secretion of catecholamines and their metabolites, such as VMA and homovanillic acid (HVA), is related to endocrine tumours, such as pheochromocytoma and NB. Elevated levels of these are found in more than 85% of NB carriers, and their excretion remains increased as long as the active disease persists. Although the absolute values of VMA and HVA have no prognostic significance, the relationship between their levels is important as the higher the VMA:HVA ratio in patients with disseminated disease, the better the prognosis. In addition, NSE produced by NB cells is elevated in about 95% of patients with disseminated disease, as well as elevated blood levels of the enzyme LDH, although non-specific, is often a marker of advanced disease. 17 18 Intrabdominal NB can be detected incidentally, during consultations for other reasons, or even before birth, on a routine ultrasound. Children may complain of abdominal discomfort. If the mass is bulky, it can cause compressive symptoms such as urinary retention, constipation or intestinal occlusion, scrotal and lower limb oedema or renovascular hypertension. Bulky tumours can bleed, causing bloating, which can also be associated with secondary liver involvement. The follow-up of our patient 4 years after treatment, and according to literature data, we recognise the validity of the treatment performed based on the pretreatment risk according to the INRGSS, 79% event-free survival at 5 years and 89% survival global for L2. 19 Cancers in children are not always treated in the same manner as in adults, and paediatric oncology is a medical specialty focused on the care of children with cancer. In high-income countries, with organised health services and easier access to these specialists, the cure rate is around 80%. According to the data from the WHO, it is in low-income and middle-income countries that more cases of childhood cancer are registered, where they face inequalities in early detection, diagnosis and access to quality treatment and palliative care. In high-income countries 80% of children with cancer will survive but in some low-income and middle-income countries only about 20% will survive. 9 20 In many countries the distribution of child health specialists, their role in child care and the structure of health systems are unknown or insufficient. According to a recent study, the number of paediatricians per 100 000 children ranged from a median of 0.5 (IQR 0.3–1.4) in low-income countries to 72 (IQR 4–118) in high-income countries. 21 The WHO, with a view to meeting the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) by 2030, has set the goal of achieving a rate of 4.45 health professionals per 1000 inhabitants. 20 An important public health strategy is the diagnosis of cancer as early as possible, with a view to the best chance of successful treatment. When cancer treatment is delayed or unaffordable, there is a lower chance of survival, more difficult and time-consuming treatments, and higher overall care costs. In each country, there are different health systems projects suggesting great variability in the roles that paediatricians and other professionals play in supporting children’s health. Although most countries provide some form of preventive child care, this primary care role is often performed by non-physicians or general practitioners. It is extremely important to understand the functioning of child health around the world, so that adequate training skills can be developed more homogeneously for all child health professionals in order to provide equitable and quality healthcare for all. 22 In this case, the referral to the paediatric oncology specialty was not a problem; a telephone contact was made, and the patient was sent that same day for specialised medical observation. In general, in Portugal, with the exception of informal situations or urgent cases in which the request for a specialist consultation can be made directly orally or by telephone, the reference to a specific medical specialty is made by computer in a national platform or by letter with the sending of relevant and complete information in writing. Specialist physicians provide feedback on the observed case by letter and are often available for regular training sessions on various topics relevant for the sharing of clinical experiences and increasing healthcare improvement in both in primary healthcare and secondary/specialist healthcare. 23 In remote locations where specialised/paediatric services may not be available, the importance of medical training in paediatrics is highlighted, with emphasis on carrying out a careful physical examination every time we evaluate a child, namely abdominal palpation. The role of timely referral will be evident in such a situation. Learning points. Most countries provide some regular primary care for children, but across the world there are inequalities in the distribution of trained specialists/paediatricians, particularly in areas with high infant mortality. It is important to alert and invest in the training of health professionals who observe children, namely in the field of paediatric oncology. In children, oncological diseases are often asymptomatic and are not detected in a timely manner for timely referral. Neuroblastoma is the second most typical retroperitoneal tumour in children. In this case, the importance of a complete objective examination of the child is highlighted, in any situation or context of clinical observation, namely abdominal palpation, which allowed a timely specialised referral and a favourable outcome for this child. Footnotes Contributors: All authors contributed to the design of this protocol. CG observed the patient, collected data and constructed the article. LM acted as scientific advisor and critically reviewed the study proposal. Both authors guided the patient and followed the treatment and his clinical evolution. All authors contributed to the manuscript and read and approved the final manuscript. Funding: The authors have not declared a specific grant for this research from any funding agency in the public, commercial or not-for-profit sectors. Case reports provide a valuable learning resource for the scientific community and can indicate areas of interest for future research. They should not be used in isolation to guide treatment choices or public health policy. Competing interests: None declared. Provenance and peer review: Not commissioned; externally peer reviewed. Ethics statements Patient consent for publication Consent obtained from parent(s)/guardian(s). References 1. Brodeur GM. Neuroblastoma: biological insights into a clinical enigma. Nat Rev Cancer 2003;3:203–16. 10.1038/nrc1014 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Maris JM, Hogarty MD, Bagatell R, et al. Neuroblastoma. Lancet 2007;369:2106–20. 10.1016/S0140-6736(07)60983-0 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Hatten J, McGuffin A, Mogul M. Inconspicuous presentation of metastatic neuroblastoma. W V Med J 2016;112:38–41. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Twist CJ, Schmidt ML, Naranjo A, et al. Maintaining outstanding outcomes using response- and biology-based therapy for intermediate-risk neuroblastoma: a report from the children''s Oncology Group study ANBL0531. J Clin Oncol 2019;37:3243–55. 10.1200/JCO.19.00919 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Mlakar V, Jurkovic Mlakar S, Lopez G, et al. 11Q deletion in neuroblastoma: a review of biological and clinical implications. Mol Cancer 2017;16:114. 10.1186/s12943-017-0686-8 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Gatta G, Botta L, Rossi S, et al. Childhood cancer survival in Europe 1999-2007: results of EUROCARE-5--a population-based study. Lancet Oncol 2014;15:35–47. 10.1016/S1470-2045(13)70548-5 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. International childhood cancer day. Available: https://www.paho.org/en/campaigns/international-childhood-cancer-day 8. Miranda AC, Mayer-da-Silva A, Glória L, et al. Registo Oncológico Nacional de Todos os Tumores na População Residente em Portugal, em 2018. Janeiro, 2021. Available: https://ron.min-saude.pt/media/2196/2021-0518_publicação-ron_2018.pdf 9. Golden CB, Feusner JH. Malignant abdominal masses in children: quick guide to evaluation and diagnosis. Pediatr Clin North Am 2002;49:1369–92. 10.1016/S0031-3955(02)00098-6 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Programa Nacional de Saúde Infantil e Juvenil. Norma da DGS N° 010/2013 de, 2013. Available: https://www.dgs.pt/documentos-e-publicacoes/programa-tipo-de-atuacao-em-saude-infantil-e-juvenil-png.aspx 11. WONCA Europa . A definição europeia de medicina geral e familiar (Clínica geral/Medicina familiar). WONCA Europa, 2002. Available: http://www.woncaeurope.org/sites/default/files/documents/European%20Definition%20in%20Portuguese.pdf 12. Dados Estatísticos [Internet]. Pipop.info, 2018. Available: https://www.pipop.info/pais-e-amigos/cancro-pediatrico/dados-estatisticos/ [Accessed 06 Apr 2021]. 13. Brodeur GM, Marris J. Neuroblastoma. Pizzo DPP eds. Lippincott Williams & Wilkins, 2005: 933–70. [ Google Scholar ] 14. Heck JE, Ritz B, Hung RJ, et al. The epidemiology of neuroblastoma: a review. Paediatr Perinat Epidemiol 2009;23:125–43. 10.1111/j.1365-3016.2008.00983.x [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 15. Whittle SB, Smith V, Doherty E, et al. Overview and recent advances in the treatment of neuroblastoma. Expert Rev Anticancer Ther 2017;17:369–86. 10.1080/14737140.2017.1285230 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 16. Semmes EC, Shen E, Cohen JL, et al. Genetic variation associated with childhood and adult stature and risk of MYCN-amplified neuroblastoma. Cancer Med 2020;9:8216–25. 10.1002/cam4.3458 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 17. Small AG, Thwe LM, Byrne JA. Neuroblastoma, body mass index, and survival: a retrospective analysis: a retrospective analysis. Medicine 2015;94:e713. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 18. Fang X, Wang H, Ma X, et al. Clinical features of children with retinoblastoma and neuroblastoma. J Ophthalmol 2020;2020:1–8. 10.1155/2020/9315784 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 19. Monclair T, Brodeur GM, Ambros PF, et al. The International neuroblastoma risk group (INRG) staging system: an INRG Task force report. J Clin Oncol 2009;27:298–303. 10.1200/JCO.2008.16.6876 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 20. World Health Organization . Assessing national capacity for the prevention and control of noncommunicable diseases: report of the 2019 global survey. World Health organization, 2020. Available: https://apps.who.int/iris/handle/10665/331452 21. Harper BD, Nganga W, Armstrong R, et al. Where are the paediatricians? an international survey to understand the global paediatric workforce. BMJ Paediatr Open 2019;3:e000397 10.1136/bmjpo-2018-000397 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 22. WHO . Health workforce requirements for universal health coverage and the sustainable development goals: human resources for health observer series. 17, 2016. [ Google Scholar ] 23. Rocha H, Braga R, Silva C, et al. Referenciação aos Cuidados de Saúde Secundários Através de um Processo Informatizado. Revista Portuguesa de Clínica Geral 2019;35:285–98. 10.32385/rpmgf.v35i4.12321 [ DOI ] [ Google Scholar ]',952,'2025-10-10 08:33:12','2025-10-10 08:33:12');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1459,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11605843','Healthcare decision-makers'' perspectives on evaluating conflict management training in paediatric healthcare: a utilisation-focused qualitative study.','Phillipson J, Barclay S, Menson E, Lyons O.','2024','breakdowns cmf formalised cmf behavio repeatable undergirds summarising wonderful incredibly disappointed intangible juliette phillipson juliette phillipson thrumleadership barclay barclay medicalmediation menson menson medicalmediation pyne haraway haraway htps thistlethwaite pblh panahi tosanloo adham jehp jehp_54_19 forbat sayer mcnamee brinkert gerardi aacn sherlock saulo wagener forbat sayer forbat teuten barclay forbat barclay medicalmediation forbat menson e001088 bmjpo wmich evalctr asg 1478088706qp063oa edens evalprogplan yalcin saks hannum martineau reinelt yardley dornan hammick koppel brit hammick dornan 7is jeehp','Abstract Background Conflict is prevalent across healthcare settings but is especially common in paediatrics, where high emotional stakes and parental expectations often intensify disagreements. Conflict can lead to negative outcomes for hospitals, staff and patients. Effective conflict management training can mitigate these impacts, but evaluating such training programmes remains challenging due to a lack of standardised tools and best practices. Methods This qualitative study aimed to explore healthcare decision-makers'' perspectives on what key areas should be evaluated in conflict management training programmes in paediatric healthcare settings, employing Patton’s utilisation-focused evaluation approach. Semi-structured interviews were conducted with 13 healthcare decision-makers and key stakeholders from various healthcare and charitable organisations. Interviews were analysed using reflexive thematic analysis by Braun and Clarke to identify key themes for evaluation. Results Four primary themes were generated regarding the key areas that healthcare decision-makers believe should be evaluated in conflict management training. These were experience of training sessions, staff competency and well-being, patient/family experiences of conflict and impact on staff time and on clinical resources. Interviewees identified that the evaluation of training sessions should focus on participant engagement and satisfaction. Codes relating to staff competency and well-being included the acquisition and long-term retention of conflict management skills. Codes relating to patient/family experience focused on the quality of communication and support during conflicts. The theme of resource utilisation included codes relating to the time and cost implications of conflict. Conclusion The study identified essential evaluation areas that align with and expand on Kirkpatrick’s framework, suggesting the need for both qualitative and quantitative data and long-term follow-up. Tailoring evaluation frameworks to specific programme contexts can enhance their relevance and utility, contributing to improved conflict management in both paediatric and wider healthcare settings. Keywords: Qualitative research, Health services research WHAT IS ALREADY KNOWN ON THIS TOPIC Conflict in paediatric healthcare is prevalent and has detrimental effects on staff, patients and families. While conflict management training can help reduce these impacts, current methods for evaluating such training programmes are inconsistent and lack standardisation. WHAT THIS STUDY ADDS This study identifies four key areas for evaluating conflict management training: experience of training sessions, staff competency and well-being, patient/family experiences of conflict and impact on staff time and on clinical resources. It presents a framework that incorporates both qualitative and quantitative data, addressing a broader range of outcomes than traditional models like Kirkpatrick’s framework, particularly focusing on long-term impacts and well-being. HOW THIS STUDY MIGHT AFFECT RESEARCH, PRACTICE OR POLICY The study offers an evaluation framework that can enhance the effectiveness of conflict management training in a paediatric healthcare setting, leading to improved staff performance, better patient outcomes, and more efficient use of healthcare resources. Background Conflict is a multifaceted phenomenon characterised by negative emotional reactions stemming from perceived disagreements between two or more parties. 1 Conflict is widely viewed as inevitable within healthcare settings given the stressful context, real or perceived resource scarcity, communication issues and workload pressures. 1 , 5 Paediatric healthcare settings are prone to a high prevalence of conflict, primarily driven by emotional intensity, communication breakdowns, disagreements over treatment plans and the often unrealistic expectations of families navigating complex medical decisions. 6 If not managed appropriately and promptly, conflict can lead to detrimental outcomes for hospitals (including legal expenses, staff costs, higher staff turnover and reduced productivity), for staff (such as burnout, poor team cohesion and reduced well-being) and most critically for patients and families (resulting in medical errors, poor therapeutic relationships and worse prognoses). 17 , 12 Training in conflict management has proven effective in mitigating the negative impacts of conflict on hospitals, staff and patients/families. 1 , 37 10 12 13 To enhance the application of training in practice, there have been calls for the development and assessment of structured conflict management processes. 114 , 17 The Conflict Management Framework (CMF) developed by the Medical Mediation Foundation (MMF) 18 for use in paediatric settings is one of the few formalised approaches to managing healthcare conflict. The CMF has shown promise in reducing the frequency of conflicts and in improving staff self-reported scores of burnout. 17 19 The time healthcare staff spend managing conflicts represents a significant resource investment. Reducing time spent on conflicts has the potential to save considerable time and, consequently, reduce costs. 7 There is no established single best practice for evaluating the impact of a conflict management training intervention. Previous studies of conflict management training in healthcare settings have adopted several different approaches, including the use of surveys, interviews, observations and focus groups, and collecting data relating to a range hypothesised outcomes from conflict. 1 For instance, surveys and interviews have been used to gauge participants'' perceptions and self-reported changes in behavio u r, while observational studies and focus groups have provided deeper insights into the dynamics and real-world application of conflict management strategies. However, there is yet to be a consensus on the relative utility of each approach to studying conflict management training, and there is a paucity of validated or standardised tools for comparable and repeatable evaluation. This study aimed to answer the following research question: ‘What are the evaluation needs of healthcare decision-makers regarding conflict management training in paediatric healthcare settings?’ We conducted a qualitative study, rooted in the principles of utilisation-focused evaluation, 20 to answer this question. Our aim was to develop an evaluation of a complex multi-site, multi-year conflict management training programme and to increase the utility of data collected through that evaluation. By focusing on the specific needs and contexts of healthcare decision-makers, we aimed to maximise both practicality and relevance of the evaluation, ultimately contributing to a better understanding of the impact of conflict management training within healthcare settings. 21 Methods The purpose of evaluation as outlined by Patton is to provide information to support decision-making. Using Patton’s utilisation-focussed evaluation approach, ‘a focus on intended use by intended users undergirds and informs every design decision in the evaluation’. 21 With this in mind, we first identified, organised and engaged primary intended users of the evaluation of conflict management training; then identified primary intended uses of evaluation data by establishing the evaluation’s priority purposes. 20 , 22 Interviews were arranged with key stakeholders identified at each of the UK sites chosen for this study (the Great North Children’s Hospital in Newcastle, Leeds Children’s Hospital, Oxford Children’s Hospital and Oxford’s Helen and Douglas House Hospice), along with key stakeholders from the True Colours Charitable Trust, which had funded the training programme, the Together for Short Lives Charity and with parents who volunteered to contribute their insights. Interviews were conducted by a medical doctor who was independent of any of the training, healthcare or charitable organisation involved in the programmes or interviews and who has expertise in programme evaluation research. They were conducted over video-call or telephone, lasted for 30–60 minutes and were recorded and professionally transcribed for analysis. Informed consent was obtained from all participants prior to the commencement of each interview. Patients or members of the public were not involved in the design, conduct, reporting or dissemination of this research. Ethical approval This study was classified as a service evaluation as it sought to gather information on how an existing conflict management programme should be evaluated to meet the needs of healthcare decision-makers. In accordance with NIHR guidelines, service evaluation focuses on assessing the effectiveness and outcomes of a current service, which was the intent of this study. Data analysis Interviews were analysed by two authors (JP and OL) in duplicate through reflexive thematic analysis, as described by Braun and Clarke. 23 This involved (1) independently reading all the material to become familiar with the data; (2) systematically reviewing the transcription line by line to identify meaningful codes and (3) grouping and summarising groups of codes to themes. The authors then met to compare and discuss their initial themes. Themes were reviewed, refined and re-organised to ensure they accurately reflected the data and captured all relevant aspects. Any discrepancies were resolved through discussion and consensus. The final themes were then interpreted and analysed to formulate the results. Patient and public involvement No patients or members of the public were involved in the design, recruitment or conduct of this study. Although the study engaged parents as stakeholders to provide their perspectives during interviews, their involvement was limited to participation and did not extend to study design or recruitment. The results will not be directly disseminated to the study participants, but findings may be shared through academic publications and presentations. Results A total of 12 interviews were completed with 13 healthcare decision-makers. One interview was conducted with two True Colours Charitable Trust stakeholders simultaneously, at the request of the participants. The other interviewees consisted of four nurses, four doctors, two parents and the director of Programmes for the Together for Short Lives Charity. The nurses and doctors were all in decision-making positions including co-chair of the clinical ethics advisory group, medical director, lead nurse for education, associate director of nursing for children and young people, quality and safety lead, deputy head of nursing, and mental health and well-being lead for critical care. Four themes were generated from the interviews regarding the evaluation of conflict management training. These were experience of training sessions, staff competency and well-being, patient/family experiences of conflict, and impact on staff time and on clinical resources. Themes and relevant codes are included in table 1 . Table 1. Themes and relevant codes. Theme Relevant codes Staff competency and well-being Staff confidence Staff skills Staff well-being Staff stress Teamwork among healthcare staff Use of new skills Nursing experience Staff absence Patient/family experiences of conflict Staff-parent relationships and communication Parental experience of conflict Parental stress Parental well-being Support for families Impact of conflict on children Patient safety and care Impact on staff time and on clinical resources Time spent in conflict Incidence of conflict Costs and finances Prevention of conflict Healthcare system efficiency High-profile cases Complaints Litigation Experience of training sessions Engagement in training Longevity of intervention Experience of training Difficulties implementing training Open in a new tab Experience of training sessions Interviewees highlighted that the evaluation of training programmes should focus on staff members’ experience of the training. I think, whether the approach… [whether] it was easy, whether they found the information easy to absorb and use and put into practice, that kind of thing. Because you''re really trying to sell that as a model, aren''t you? - Charitable organisation Programme Director We''ll look at the numbers of staff we''ve got, the number of hours we think it''ll need, the number of sessions that we need to put on, how often do we need to get new key trainers, when have we reached a saturation point? - Nurse (Deputy head of nursing) Interviewees suggested relevant data might include the number of sessions, number of attendees from various demographics (particularly different disciplinary roles), engagement, satisfaction, perceived relevance to work, recommendations for improvement and financial cost of sessions. The importance of longevity of the training and retention of skills gained was frequently mentioned, with emphasis on culture change and sustainability. I think one of the things that I''d like to achieve with it is that it’s not standalone training. That it’s a real… for me that it’s part of our everyday… it’s part of our everyday training that it’s not just a standalone training that it’s really recognised in the Children’s Hospital. - Nurse (Lead nurse for education of the nursing team) Staff competency and well-being This theme included both skill development and the psychological impact of conflict and conflict management training on staff, as well as their collaborative experiences. Interviewees drew attention to the importance of assessing whether the programme successfully equipped healthcare staff with the necessary skills to both identify and manage conflict. They also highlighted utility in measuring (1) staff confidence in using conflict management skills, (2) the frequency of use of conflict management skills and (3) long-term retention of those skills. I hope that the people will come out with a range of skills and thoughts on how to communicate with difficult situations and have strategies to deal with difficult situations. And I guess we''re all individuals and we all will deal with a situation differently, but I guess it’s giving a consistent set of principles to the staff – Nurse (Associate director of nursing for children and young people) I think one of the issues that would be good and would be important, would be to show that people don''t just walk out of the training and say, Yes, yes. I know lots of things now. It’s wonderful. but that six months later, that they''re using those skills, they can recognise cases of conflict, that they have something to do when they encounter them. – Doctor (Consultant in newborn intensive care and co-chair of the clinical ethics advisory group) Interviewees frequently discussed the detrimental effects of conflict on staff. By providing better skills to manage conflict, they reasoned the training programme could lead to a reduction in stress and burnout and an improvement in general well-being. Interviewees suggested that such impacts on staff would be important outcomes from the training and therefore worth evaluating. Patient/family experiences of conflict Interviewees emphasised that the evaluation of training programmes should include the perspectives of patients and their families. Suggested outcomes with respect to patient and family experiences included the parental experience of healthcare conflict, whether families felt supported, and the impact of conflict-associated stress on well-being. Do they [parents] feel free, psychologically safe, to ask all those questions? Is there that safety that they can go into a dialogue and not be concerned whether that’s going to be detrimental to the care of the child, whether they''re going to be seen as difficult, and do that in a way that’s helpful to their understanding? They might be frustrated, stressed, all those things. – Doctor (Paediatric intensive care consultant) Interviewees highlighted that effective communication is essential for facilitating shared decision-making. They felt that evaluation outcomes relating to family experiences of their relationship with healthcare staff and the communication received would be valuable. That the family’s voices are heard. That would be the bottom line, that they are actually involved in the decision-making process. – Parent But in a lot of those cases, we''re aware that it’s a communication problem. It’s often communicating bad news with families, and if that’s not done well, families don''t quite understand what’s going on and all this disagreement and conflict occurs. – Charitable organisation Programme Director Interviewees identified that poorly managed conflict is likely to have a negative impact on patient care, particularly as paediatric patients are a vulnerable population. Including direct measurement of patient outcomes in the evaluation of training programmes was suggested. But in a way, the child is paramount. And we never quite measure it. We know that, through other stats, if there is a conflict there’s increased errors, increased safety [concerns], increased dissatisfaction in the care given. – Doctor (Paediatric nephrologist and quality and safety lead) Impact on staff time and on clinical resources Interviewees commonly discussed the potential for conflict to consume large amounts of healthcare workers’ time. Interviewees especially highlighted the value of quantifying this as a metric for securing funding for conflict management training long-term, as a reduction in time spent on conflict could result in a reduction in overall healthcare costs. I think one of the very significant issues about conflicts of this kind is the time that they take because clinicians'' time within a stretched NHS is an exceptionally precious resource. And these cases are incredibly time consuming, disproportionately so, and far out of proportion with the political complexity. And they''re all complex cases by their nature, but they take, often, five times more time than any other patient on the ward. – Doctor (Consultant in newborn intensive care and co-chair of the clinical ethics advisory group) I''d be disappointed if we didn''t see something on savings and clinical time hours and if we can quantify that in terms of the cost. - True Colours Charitable Trust stakeholder Interviewees suggested a range of outcome measures related to utilisation of resources. A direct measure of the incidence of conflict would provide valuable insight into the efficacy of the training framework. This could be a comparison of incidence before and after training or between groups who have had training versus those who have not. A gold standard way of evaluating something like this would be to measure the numbers of the different types of conflict as a function of the number of patients over a period of time, and to show that the high-level conflict was reduced, for example. – Doctor (Consultant in newborn intensive care and co-chair of the clinical ethics advisory group) Other quantifiable conflict outcomes suggested by participants included the number/seriousness of complaints, litigation and high-profile cases. Discussion In this study, we conducted a thematic analysis of interviews with healthcare decision-makers to guide the evaluation of a paediatric healthcare conflict management programme and to determine the types of data that would allow stakeholders to assess the programme’s success. Four key themes were generated as specific areas for evaluation: experience of training sessions, staff competency and well-being, patient/family experiences of conflict, and impact on staff time and on clinical resources. Our findings provide insights into the outcomes that healthcare decision-makers consider important when evaluating conflict management training. These areas align with existing frameworks but also highlight additional factors that are unique to the context of paediatric healthcare. Kirkpatrick’s framework, developed in 1960, remains the most popular approach to evaluating training programmes in organisations. 24 , 27 Kirkpatrick categorised training programme outcomes into a four-tier hierarchy, designed as a guide for evaluation rather than a fixed protocol. Many evaluation themes identified in this study align with each of the tiers of Kirkpatrick’s framework. For example, suggested measures of staff reaction to the training programme included engagement, satisfaction, perceived relevance to work and recommendations for improvement, all of which align with Kirkpatrick level 1: ‘Reaction’. Learning outcomes, such as skills gained in identifying and managing conflict and staff confidence, align with Kirkpatrick level 2: ‘Learning’. The frequency of use of newly learnt skills reflects Kirkpatrick level 3: ‘Behaviour’. Kirkpatrick level 4: ‘Results’ encompasses many suggestions such as the incidence of conflict, patient outcomes, reduction in time spent on conflict, reduction in conflict-related costs, complaints, litigation and high-profile cases. Some researchers have pointed out flaws in Kirkpatrick’s framework, discussing the possibility of neglecting important intangible and soft outcomes. 28 29 Our findings align with these identified shortfalls as certain themes for evaluation cannot be stratified into the framework including reduction in staff stress or burnout and the improvement in general well-being. Similarly, family experiences such as perceived parental support, experiences of communication with the medical team, parental stress and well-being require a broader evaluation framework. Several adaptations of Kirkpatrick’s framework have been developed for evaluation purposes. 29 , 32 Despite critiques over the years, Kirkpatrick’s framework offers a helpful starting point for establishing the scope of important evaluation outcomes, especially when combined with modern mixed-methods approaches. The current study has highlighted the utility of evaluation planning interviews in identifying important areas for evaluation that may not be captured within a traditional framework. Limitations of this study include the sample population of 13 individuals from selected children’s hospitals or services, which are context-specific and may limit the generalisability of our findings to all healthcare settings or other types of conflict management programmes. Strengths of the study include the range of stakeholders interviewed (nurses and doctors in decision-making roles, parents, charity representatives), capturing a wide array of perspectives on conflict management training. Using Patton’s utilisation-focused evaluation approach ensures that the evaluation framework is tailored to the specific needs and contexts of healthcare decision-makers, enhancing its practical relevance and utility. This study provides insights into evaluation measures to include in the design of conflict management training programmes for paediatric healthcare professionals. Our results suggest that an effective utilisation-focused evaluation of a conflict management programme will include qualitative and quantitative data, information from healthcare professionals and patients/families, and long-term follow-up. We recommend that future programmes incorporate a similar methodology in planning programme evaluations to ensure that the design is tailored to the specific aims of the programme and the relevant context. Acknowledgements Funding for this work was provided by the True Colours Charitable Trust. We would like to thank the participants who gave their time for these interviews. We would also like to thank the Together for Short Lives Charity for their assistance in recruiting parents for these interviews. Footnotes Funding: OL was commissioned and financially supported by the Medical Mediation Foundation to plan and conduct an evaluation of the Conflict Management Programme. Delivery of the programme was funded by the True Colours Charitable Trust (Award/Grant numbers N/A). Patient consent for publication: Consent obtained directly from patient(s). Ethics approval: This study involves human participants but was deemed unnecessary by the University of Oxford Clinical Trials Research Governance Committee. No reference number ID as approved for exemption. Participants gave informed consent to participate in the study before taking part. Provenance and peer review: Not commissioned; externally peer reviewed. Patient and public involvement: Patients and/or the public were not involved in the design, or conduct, or reporting, or dissemination plans of this research. Contributor Information Juliette Phillipson, Email: juliette.phillipson@thrumleadership.com. Sarah Barclay, Email: sarah.barclay@medicalmediation.org.uk. Esse Menson, Email: esse.menson@medicalmediation.org.uk. Oscar Lyons, Email: oscar.lyons@nds.ox.ac.uk. Data availability statement All data relevant to the study are included in the article or uploaded as supplementary information. References 1. Almost J, Wolff AC, Stewart-Pyne A, et al. Managing and mitigating conflict in healthcare teams: an integrative review. J Adv Nurs. 2016;72:1490–505. doi: 10.1111/jan.12903. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Haraway DL, Haraway WM. Analysis of the effect of conflict-management and resolution training on employee stress at a healthcare organization. Hosp Top. 2005;83:11–7. doi: 10.3200/HTPS.83.4.11-18. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Shin J-H. Developing constructive and proactive conflict management strategies in healthcare. Comm Health. 2009;2:78–94. doi: 10.1179/175380609790912841. [ DOI ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Thistlethwaite JE, Jackson A. Conflict in Practice-based Settings: Nature. Res Educ. 2014 doi: 10.11120/pblh.2014.00036. [ DOI ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Panahi Tosanloo M, Adham D, Ahmadi B, et al. Causes of conflict between clinical and administrative staff in hospitals. J Educ Health Promot. 2019;8:191. doi: 10.4103/jehp.jehp_54_19. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Forbat L, Sayer C, McNamee P, et al. Conflict in a paediatric hospital: a prospective mixed-method study. Arch Dis Child. 2016;101:23–7. doi: 10.1136/archdischild-2015-308814. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Brinkert R. A literature review of conflict communication causes, costs, benefits and interventions in nursing. J Nurs Manag. 2010;18:145–56. doi: 10.1111/j.1365-2834.2010.01061.x. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Gerardi D. Using mediation techniques to manage conflict and create healthy work environments. AACN Clin Issues. 2004;15:182–95. doi: 10.1097/00044067-200404000-00003. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Rowe MM, Sherlock H. Stress and verbal abuse in nursing: do burned out nurses eat their young? J Nurs Manag. 2005;13:242–8. doi: 10.1111/j.1365-2834.2004.00533.x. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Saulo M, Wagener RJ. Mediation training enhances conflict management by healthcare p