Gumpper KF, Heigham MK, Meyers R, Pham K, et al. ASHP-PPAG guidelines for providing pediatric pharmacy services in hospitals and health systems. Am J Health Syst Pharm. 2018;75:1151–65. doi: 10.2146/ajhp170827. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 39. Johnson TJ. Pharmacist work force in 2020: Implications of requiring residency training for practice. Am J Health Syst Pharm. 2008;65:166–70. doi: 10.2146/ajhp070231. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 40. Mukattash TL, Alattar M, Abu-Farha RK, Alsous M, Jarab AS, Darwish Elhajji FW, et al. Evaluating scientific research knowledge and attitude among medical representatives in Jordan: A cross-sectional survey. Curr Clin Pharmacol. 2017;12:245–52. doi: 10.2174/1574884712666170828124950. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 41. Jee S, Schafheutle E, Noyce PR. Changes in behavioural professionalism during pre-registration training and early pharmacy practice. Int J Pharm Pract; Abstracts of the papers presented at the Health Services Research & Pharmacy Practice Conference; 3–4 April 2014; University of Aberdeen, Aberdeen, UK. 2014. pp. S41–2. [ Google Scholar ] 42. Mukattash TL, Nuseir KQ, Biltaji E, Jarab AS, Alefan Q. Students’ perceptions of pharmacy as a specialization and their future career, a cross sectional study of final year pharmacy students in Jordan. Jordan J Pharm Sci. 2015;8:195–204. [ Google Scholar ]',15,'2025-10-10 08:36:19','2025-10-10 08:36:19');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1474,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11829927','How Consistent are Anti-Infective Drug Dosing Recommendations Across Three European Paediatric Formularies?','Higi L, Carydias E, Burden AM, Vonbach P.','2024','bodyweight swisspeddose digitalisation s40272 bodyweight bodyweight bodyweight caspofungin posology kinderformularium swisspeddose digitalisation personalisation extensible clavulanic bodyweight bodyweight milligram milligram kilogram bodyweight kilogram bodyweight milligram milligram bodyweight milligram milligram kilogram bodyweight bodyweight mosteller conditionally bodyweight kilogram conditionally milligram kilogram milligram milligram discontinuities swisspeddose roa roa roa swisspeddose tiles tiles signify swisspeddose swisspeddose q25 q75 swisspeddose swisspeddose q25 q75 roa clostridioides prokinetic benzylpenicillin benzylpenicillin bodyweight jirovecii bodyweight roa swisspeddose gpd kinderformularium benzylpenicillin benzylpenicillin kilogram milligram bodyweights heights bodyweights heights bodyweight bodyweight bodyweights heights bodyweight bodyweight clostridioides meaningless neighbours kinderformularium kinderformularium digitalisation sematic digitalisation kinderformularium expedient digitalisation oskar jenni higi pedeus priska vonbach pedeus pedeus higi carydias bartelink rademaker schobben afam rodieux wilbaux seibel neely caspofungin ick ghaleb tully carrington dornan teferi pruitt optimizations schrier hadjipanayis stiris valiulis rylance zanden liem offringa hoog tilen panis aeschbacher sabine schwabedissen lexidrug wolterskluwer lexidrug shaniv bolisetty mangum ainsworth elbers zahn wimmer rodle toni sedlmayr prokosch amending repealing zanden hoog windster rosmalen sijs higi kaser walti grotzer vonbach e001726 kinderformularium kinderformularium kindermedika kindermedika koble koble kinderformularium kinderformularium swisspeddose datenbank dosierung arzneimitteln swisspeddose pharmaceuticalpress higi kaser walti grotzer vonbach braegger jenni luciano paediatrica paediatrieschweiz wachstumskurven mosteller dowell giebink jernigan musher garbutt geme storch shackelford','Abstract Background Drug dosing recommendations in paediatrics are mainly based on the age and bodyweight of the child. Because of the limited amount of label information, several paediatric drug formularies have been developed. This study compares anti-infective drug dosing recommendations across three European formularies. Methods Recommendations from three paediatric formularies (German Pediatric Formulary [GPF], SwissPedDose [SPD] and the British National Formulary for Children [BNF]) were collected. Using population growth curves, we simulated one child for each month from 1 month up to 18 years of age. The recommendations from each formulary were used to calculate doses for each simulated child. Equivalence and difference in calculated doses were analysed. Results In total, dosing recommendations for 34 anti-infective substances were collected with 74 corresponding indications, which resulted in 47,154 calculated doses. The mean (± standard deviation) proportion of equivalent doses (difference ≤10%) across all three formularies was 40% (±16), while for pairwise comparisons it was 53% (±19) for GPF versus BNF, 67% (±14) for GPF versus SPD and 52% (±19) for SPD versus BNF. The median [25th quantile, 75th quantile] differences in daily doses across all three formularies were 0%, [0, 26] while for pairwise comparisons it was 4% [0, 32] for GPF versus BNF, 0% [0, 17] for GPF versus SPD and 7% [0, 33] for SPD versus BNF. Conclusions The majority of recommended anti-infective drug doses were consistent, with the highest equivalence found between GPF and SPD. Maintaining formularies is resource intensive; therefore, a common standard in Europe could prove beneficial when moving towards digitalisation of the healthcare systems. Supplementary Information The online version contains supplementary material available at 10.1007/s40272-024-00674-2. Key Points The majority of anti-infective drug dosing recommendations in three European paediatric formularies were found to be consistent. A unified European formulary could set a standard for innovative software solutions. Open in a new tab Introduction In paediatric pharmacotherapy, drug dosing is commonly performed individually based on bodyweight as a proxy of the child’s developmental status. However, across the full age spectrum, bodyweight alone is often not sufficiently precise to account for the developmental changes affecting the pharmacokinetics of a drug [ 1 ]. For this reason, dosing recommendations are often split into separate recommendations for different age categories. In doing that, different stages of child development can be accounted for (i.e. as a proxy for organ function and maturation) [ 1 , 2 ]. Additionally, substance-specific factors may result in the use of alternative strategies such as dosing based on body surface area (BSA) or even fixed doses. Body surface area-based dosing is mainly conducted for drugs where clearance processes correlate with BSA rather than bodyweight [ 1 ], such as for caspofungin [ 3 ]. Fixed doses by age are easy to administer as no individualised calculation is required, which is a well-described source of error [ 4 – 6 ]. However, as a fixed dose does not account for the individual patient’s characteristics, a higher variability in exposure is expected. Historically, the availability of approved drugs for paediatric populations has always been low, resulting in high rates of off-label or unlicensed use [ 7 ]. This led to the development of paediatric formularies to provide healthcare professionals with the required information for the pharmacological treatment. There are several paediatric formularies available that synthesise posology information using the available literature in combination with expert opinions [ 8 – 12 ]. In Europe, the three well-known formularies are the British National Formulary for Children (BNF) [ 8 ], the Kinderformularium (GPF) [ 9 , 13 ] and SwissPedDose (SPD) [ 10 ]. Both the BNF and the GPF provide dosing information and comprehensive drug-related information (e.g. on kidney impairment, drug–drug interactions or adverse drug reactions). The SPD focuses on dosing information, with minimal additional information. All three formularies currently provide both label and off-label recommendations, although the SPD database was mandated to provide off-label recommendations only. Moreover, all of them are endorsed or supported by governmental authorities and national specialist societies and thus represent the official dosing standard in their countries. The next step towards the digitalisation and personalisation of pharmacological treatment is the development of software and algorithms as medical devices to guide clinical decision making. In paediatrics, the automated calculation of drug doses is the logical next step to improve safe prescribing by limiting human errors [ 6 ]. In Europe, the application of such clinical decision support systems is regulated under the European Medical Device Regulation [ 14 ]. For applications that communicate with a database, the availability of carefully maintained and structured data is imperative. It has been shown previously that automated dose calculation has the potential to reduce calculation errors and improve prescribing safety [ 15 , 16 ]. All of the aforementioned formularies maintain their database by conducting periodic content reviews to ensure that they are up-to-date. This study aims to compare anti-infective drug dosing recommendations of three European paediatric formularies. We restricted the analysis to anti-infective drugs because of their high relevance in paediatric patients. Notably, anti-infective drugs constitute the majority of the World Health Organization Model List of Essential Medicines for Children [ 17 ]. Currently, this is the first study that compared dosing recommendations across paediatric formularies in Europe. We developed a novel method to compare dosing recommendations by calculating age-dependent child doses using population standards for height and weight. The minimally required information for dose calculation was used to simulate the range of available doses across the child age spectrum. Methods Data Sources Data were collected from the three paediatric drug formularies, the BNF, the GPF and the SPD databases. The GPF database was initially developed by the Dutch Pediatric Formulary [ 9 ]. However, it has evolved into an international collaboration including dedicated formularies for Germany [ 13 ], Austria and Norway sharing a core dataset [ 12 ] as the basis for the country-specific databases. They are publicly available via websites [ 18 – 21 ]. The SPD database provides a publicly available website [ 22 ]. The BNF is available as a book [ 23 ] or via online subscription services [ 24 ]. These three formularies were selected as they have published their workflow in a peer-reviewed scientific journal in English. The BNF data were collected from the book (BNF for Children 2023–24) [ 23 ] and transferred to a pre-structured spreadsheet. The SPD data were available as an Extensible Markup Language (XML) file (version 2023-07-25). The GPF data were retrieved from the website in November 2023 [ 1 ]. The GPF was used, as there was no English version of the Dutch Pediatric Formulary available. Data Collection and Processing Eligibility Criteria for Dosing Data In a first step, we created a list of relevant anti-infective drugs that were present in all three formularies. To create a list of relevant anti-infective drugs, we extracted all anti-infective drugs available in the database of the certified medical device software PEDeDose [ 25 ], which is based on the standard medication lists used in the three stand-alone children’s hospitals in Switzerland. The intersection of the substances available in all three formularies was then used as the sample. We collected dosing data recommended for children aged from 1 month up to 18 years (i.e. up to 17 years and 11 months). Dosing data for intravenous, per oral and inhalation administration was included. In a next step, we extracted from each formulary all indications available for these drugs. Indications were manually assessed and matched so that only indications with similar meaning were collected for the comparison. The matching was performed by one author and reviewed by two others. Disagreements were discussed until a consensus was reached. Variables We systematically collected the dosing recommendations from the monographs from each of the three formularies. A monograph was defined as the combination of a substance (or a substance combination, e.g. amoxicillin and clavulanic acid), indication and route of administration. Each monograph was subdivided into records for each different dose type (e.g. loading dose), age and bodyweight condition. In each record, the information on dose values, dose units and reference units, frequency of administration and maximal doses was collected. An example is shown in Table 1 . Table 1. Overview of collected variables Monograph: data structure Variable name Record 1 Record 2 Substance or substance combination Fluconazole Indication Mucosal candidiasis Route of administration Per oral Age condition 1 month to 12 years Bodyweight condition – Dose type Loading dose Maintenance dose Dose value 6–12 3–6 Dose units Milligram Milligram Dose reference unit Per kilogram bodyweight Per kilogram bodyweight Frequency of administration 1 1 Maximal single dose – – Maximal single dose unit – – Maximal daily dose 800 400 Maximal daily dose unit Milligram Milligram Open in a new tab As an example, fluconazole for mucosal candidiasis from the German Pediatric Formulary is shown. The variables substance, indication and route of administration uniquely identified a monograph. Multiple records were created for each monograph based on dose type, age and bodyweight condition. Additional records, for children aged <1 month and >12 years were present in the monograph but were not reported for simplicity Data Processing The data were processed and analysed using R (version 4.4.1) [ 26 ]. Dose types were categorised as loading, maintenance, single dose or “none”, where “none” was used in case no specific dose type was specified. To facilitate a comparison, we converted all numerator units to milligram (mg) and all doses to daily doses. Dose ranges and frequency of administration were used to calculate the lowest and highest recommended daily doses (i.e. 10–20 mg three to four times daily became 30–80 mg/day). Simulated Child Data In order to compare relative dose definitions (e.g. milligram per kilogram per dose) to fixed doses (mg per dose), a reference value is required (e.g. a bodyweight for weight-based dose definitions). For this reason, we simulated 215 children, i.e. one for each month between 1 month and 18 years of age. To each child, we assigned the mean of the median bodyweight and height values of girls and boys using the data from the Swiss growth charts [ 27 ]. The height was used to estimate the BSA with the Mosteller equation [ 1 , 28 ]. body surface area ( BSA ) [ m 2 ] = height [ cm ] × weight [ kg ] 3600 1 To explore potential differences across age groups, we categorised the simulated children into four age groups using a modified definition of the International Council for Harmonisation of Technical Requirements for Pharmaceuticals for Human Use [ 29 ] as follows: infants (1 month to 2 years), toddlers (2–6 years), children (6–12 years) and adolescents (12–18 years). Data Matching and Dose Calculation We conditionally assigned the dosing information from the monographs to the simulated child data based on the age and bodyweight condition so that each child was assigned the corresponding record from the monograph with the recommended daily dose. The resulting data sets will be referred to as dosing set s. We calculated individual dosages for each child based on the reference unit (per kilogram or per m 2 ) as appropriate. Maximal daily doses were assigned when the calculated daily dose exceeded the maximal daily dose. A schematic of the algorithm is visualised in Fig. 1 . Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Schematic of the algorithm to create a dosing set. For each monograph, the simulated child data were conditionally assigned to the dosing recommendations. Based on the reference unit, the individual child doses were calculated as daily doses. This resulted in the generation of the dosing sets. Each dosing set contains individual daily doses for the full age range defined within the monograph The dosing sets were matched across the formularies by child age and dose types. An exception was made for “maintenance” and “none” dose types, which were allowed to be matched. The dosing sets were matched for each pairwise combination (GPF vs BNF, GPF vs SPD and SPD vs BNF) as well as for an “overall” comparison by matching the intersection of the three formularies. Analyses and Comparisons Missing Data Not all dosing sets covered the same or the full age spectrum. For this reason, the number of calculated child doses may differ depending on the formularies compared. Therefore, we only analysed the intersection for each comparison and excluded missing data. Descriptive Statistics We counted the total number of distinct indications for all included substances for each formulary and the percentage included after matching. Additionally, we calculated the percentage dosing recommendations stratified by reference units (i.e. separately for milligram per kilogram, milligram per BSA and milligram per dose). Dose Differences Dose differences were calculated as the relative difference in percent. For this, the absolute difference between the two doses was divided by the higher dose. Median, 25th quantile and 75th quantile of the dose differences were calculated to provide a summary of the spread of the data that is not influenced by outliers or discontinuities in the data. The dose differences were stratified by route of administration, dose type and child age category. Dose Equivalence We defined equivalence as a dose difference of ≤10% in the calculated daily dose for a given child. The difference for lower and upper doses was calculated separately for each comparison. As not all dosing sets were of equal size (because of age constraints in dosing recommendations), the means were calculated as the grand means, i.e. the pooled mean of the individual dosing set means, giving each dosing set an equal weight. For reporting, the grand mean of lower and upper doses was combined using the mean of the lower and upper differences. We calculated the overall percentage of equivalent dosages per dosing set as well as the percentages when stratified by child age category. Maximal Differences in Daily Doses To understand the worst-case scenario, the distribution of the maximal differences across the dosing sets was analysed. We plotted the maximal differences across all dosing sets and comparisons and looked at the maximal differences when stratified by child age category. Additionally, we selected the 12 doses exhibiting the highest maximum differences to characterise the reason for the difference. Results Description of Collected Data Table 2 describes the key numbers of the initial dataset extracted as monographs from the formularies. There were 74 dosing sets for 34 anti-infective substances created per formulary. Initially, we collected monographs from the GPF, SPD and BNF for 127, 120 and 268 indications. We split or repeated seven indications from the GPF, four from the SPD and seven from the BNF to facilitate a comparison, the resulting number of selected indications across the formularies is shown in Table 2 . The list of substances is provided in Fig. 2 and the list of indications is provided in Table 1 of the Electronic Supplementary Material (ESM). Table 2. Key numbers of the collected dosing data. Percentages may not sum to 100% because of rounding Monographs N Selected indications German Pediatric Formulary 62 SwissPedDose 65 British National Formulary for Children 62 Dosing sets N (%) Overview Number of drugs 34 (100) Number of drug-indication combinations 69 (100) Number of drug-indication–RoA combinations 74 (100) By RoA Intravenous 36 (49) Per oral 36 (49) Inhalation 2 (3) Open in a new tab RoA route of administration Fig. 2. Open in a new tab Prevalence of dose reference units in each monograph for the German Pediatric Formulary (GPF), SwissPedDose (SPD) and the British National Formulary for Children (BNF). The saturated colour tiles indicate the presence of a reference unit, while the transparent tiles signify its absence. The numbers may exceed 100%, as a dosing set may contain more than one dosing strategy (e.g. weight based for children aged <12 years and fixed for children aged >12 years) In each formulary, the fraction of monographs providing weight-based dosages was 97%. Fixed doses were used in 50%, 29% and 9% of BNF, GPF and SPD monographs, respectively. Whereas BSA-based dosages were only used in 6% of GPF and SPD, and in 9% of BNF monographs (Fig. 2 ). Description of Simulated Child Doses A total of 74 dosing sets were created for each formulary, which resulted in 222 dosing sets with 49,351 (100%) simulated child doses across all three formularies. Out of these, 47,154 (96%) were analysed and 2197 (4%) were not available for analysis (e.g. because of gaps in age thresholds across formularies). The numbers for the overall comparison (i.e. the intersection of all three comparisons) and for each pairwise comparison are summarised in Table 3 . Table 3. Overview of the numbers of simulated child doses analysed for each pairwise comparison: GPF versus BNF, GPF versus SPD and SPD versus BNF, respectively Overall GPF vs BNF G