INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1489,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7606169','Preparedness to practice paediatric hospital medicine.','Vetere P, Cooke S.','2019','croup dalhousie conjoint q15 q55 q56 q57 q59 ahd trach ctu weren anybody nontertiary scut ahd ctu uvc trach uvc delegating bekmezian manen interosseous hone dalhousie halifax irenke e20161823 merenstein egleston jerardi rassbach e20170698 bismilla dubrowski rspsc e131 rcsite hilliard kauffman poulton e54 bekmezian teufel manen dubrowski bismilla','Abstract Background The field of paediatric hospital medicine (PHM) is evolving to meet the needs of an increasingly complex paediatric population, lead quality improvement initiatives, and conduct field-specific teaching and research. Over 50 subspecialty PHM fellowship programs exist in the USA and Canada and more are under active development to ensure trainees are prepared to perform competencies specific to the field following transition to independent practice. Objective The objective of this study was to assess the perceived preparedness of recently graduated general paediatric residents and recently certified staff paediatricians in Canada with respect to the practice of PHM. Methods A survey based on the ‘Objectives of Training in Pediatrics’ of the Royal College of Physicians and Surgeons of Canada (RCPSC) was distributed to graduating paediatric residents and recently graduated staff paediatricians (2013 to 2017) practicing in the hospital setting. Qualitative comments were also obtained. Results Fifty-five surveys were completed (50%). Respondents perceived that, at the on-set of starting practice, they would require assistance or consultation with the majority of representative PHM task competencies. Differences in perception between the two groups were minimal. Our study identified sub-sets of perceived areas of particular strengths (Professional) and deficiencies (Medical Expert, Manager, and Scholar). Conclusions Results may help inform future curricula for general paediatric residency programs and provide insight into competencies that may be better targeted for PHM fellowship training programs. This study may also stimulate discussion regarding entrustable professional activities for paediatric curricula as the medical community shifts to a new paradigm of outcome-based assessment. Keywords: CanMEDS, Paediatric hospital medicine, Transition to practice, Competency by Design (CBD) The field of paediatric hospital medicine (PHM) is evolving to meet the needs of an increasingly acute and complex population of children requiring hospitalization ( 1 ). One factor contributing to this demographic shift is that a significant proportion of children previously requiring admission for common conditions (i.e., asthma, croup, gastroenteritis, diabetes, osteomyelitis) can now be effectively managed as out-patients ( 2 ). Concurrently, due to improvements in several facets of medical and surgical care, more infants and children are surviving prematurity, major congenital anomalies, and serious acquired injuries and infections. Consequently, the population of hospitalized children in tertiary care centres today is increasingly composed of patients with acute complications of common problems, multiple comorbidities, severe injuries, complex chronic diseases, technology-dependent conditions, and children requiring palliative care ( 3 ). A hospital paediatrician must not only possess the specialized knowledge and skills to provide clinical care to this high intensity population but also be prepared to lead inter-disciplinary teams, coach trainees, advocate effectively for patients, and engage in scholarly work ( 1 ). In response to the demand for these specific competencies, formal fellowship training programs have been created. The first PHM fellowship programs were launched in the USA in 2003 ( 4 ). Currently, more than 50 PHM fellowship programs exist in North America and this number continues to expand. In parallel with the need for advanced competencies, the American Board of Pediatrics now recognizes PHM as a formal subspecialty. PHM fellowship training program accreditation begins in 2019 ( 5 ). Despite the evolution of PHM as a recognized sub-specialty requiring an additional 2 years of training in the USA, there is currently no formal requirement in Canada to complete a PHM fellowship before obtaining a permanent position as a tertiary care hospital-based paediatrician. Paediatric residency training in Canada is currently 4 years’ duration and is designed to prepare paediatricians to practice in the hospital or community setting. However, a 2015 study surveying Canadian general paediatric program directors revealed they believe residents are less than adequately prepared to practice paediatric medicine in several of the required fields ( 6 ). The Royal College of Physicians and Surgeons of Canada (RCPSC) outlines the 2008 Objectives of Training in Pediatrics according to the seven CanMEDS roles of the doctor-specialist: Medical Expert, Communicator, Collaborator, Health Advocate, Manager, Scholar, and Professional ( 7 ). A physician who desires to practice PHM in Canada should be prepared to independently perform tasks in the workplace related to each competency. A Canadian study by Amin and colleagues found that paediatricians entering practice felt inadequately prepared for 25% of the core competencies outlined in the RCPSC objectives ( 8 ). To our knowledge, no study has examined the level of perceived preparedness of graduating paediatric residents in Canada with respect to PHM competencies. There also appears to be a gap in the literature with respect to whether recently certified general paediatricians working in the paediatric hospital medicine setting in Canada feel they were prepared to transition to independent practice. Understanding the perceptions of these stakeholders will prove valuable as PHM fellowship program curricula are being actively developed in Canada. The objective of the current study was to assess the perceived preparedness of recent graduating paediatric residents and recently certified staff paediatricians in Canada with respect to the practice of PHM. METHODS An electronic survey was designed based on the 2008 Objectives of Training in Pediatrics of the RCPSC. Survey questions were organized according to the seven CanMEDS roles ( 9 ). Our survey was based on the most current RCPSC Objectives of Training (2008) and for consistency, we elected to explore preparedness to engage in the role of Manager, synonymous with ‘Leader’ in the more recent 2015 CanMEDS framework. The survey also contained a qualitative component that aimed to explore in greater depth perceived preparedness or lack of preparedness to practice paediatric hospital medicine. The survey was distributed to fourth and fifth year paediatric residents (PR) who had just completed their RCPSC certifying examination in Paediatrics (June 2018) as well as recently graduated staff (RGS) paediatricians practicing in the hospital setting who had been certified by the RCPSC in the previous 4 years (2013 to 2017). Inclusion criteria for RGS hospital paediatricians required that at least 50% of one’s clinical work be dedicated to tertiary care, inpatient paediatric medicine. The survey was distributed to PR and RGS hospital-based paediatricians at seven major centres in Canada including the Universities of British Columbia, Calgary, Alberta, Toronto, Ottawa, McGill, and Dalhousie. Demographic data and details regarding the individuals’ training and current practice were collected at the initiation of the survey. The remainder of the survey assessed PR and RGS perceptions on how well prepared they felt at the end of their general paediatric residency training to perform five PHM-related tasks pertaining to each of the seven CanMEDS roles (35 items total). These items represent samples of tasks a tertiary care, in-patient paediatrician might be expected to perform within clinical and academic contexts and in relation to the CanMEDS roles. Respondents were asked to describe their perceived level of preparedness on a Likert scale of 1 to 4, where: 1—Not Prepared: I had no formal exposure to this aspect of practice in my general paediatric training. 2— Somewhat Prepared/ Require Consultation: I had minimal exposure to this aspect of practice during my general paediatric residency training and do not feel ready to manage it independently. I would need to refer this situation to a colleague. 3—Somewhat Prepared/Require Assistance: I had significant exposure and opportunities to assist in the management of this aspect of practice during my general paediatric residency training but do not feel ready to manage it independently. I will require assistance or an opportunity to review/ revise my plan with a physician in my practice group. 4—Very Well Prepared/Independent: I had significant exposure and opportunities to manage this aspect of practice during my general paediatric residency training and feel ready to manage it independently. Not Applicable (N/A): This competency is not required of an in-patient paediatrician in my institution. The quantitative data were analyzed using independent t-tests for PR and RGS scores in relation to preparedness for independent practice based on: 1) The seven CanMEDS roles, and, 2) The 35 individual PHM competency-related tasks. The survey also invited qualitative comments that aimed to explore perceived preparedness or lack of preparedness, factors that may have influenced perceived areas of deficiency and strength, and, changes the respondent would suggest to the current training model to better prepare trainees for a career in PHM. Qualitative responses were analyzed using thematic analysis ( 10 ). This study was approved by the University of Calgary Conjoint Health Research Ethics Board. RESULTS Of the 110 surveys distributed, 55 were completed (50%) including 36/80 paediatric residents and 19/30 hospital paediatricians. Contributions from PR and RGS were obtained from all seven participating institutions. We excluded 13 respondents who completed less than one section of the survey. Of the 55 respondents who completed the survey, 65% were fourth (n=34/36) or fifth year (n=2/36) PR and 35% (n=19/30) were RGS hospital-based paediatricians. The vast majority of respondents (50/55) graduated from a Canadian medical school prior to completing residency training in Canada. PR and RGS responses regarding preparedness to practice PHM with respect to the specific CanMEDS roles are presented in Figure 1 . Both groups perceived a lack of preparedness in several CanMEDS roles with the lowest scores reported in the roles of Manager (2.97), Medical Expert (3.16), and Communicator (3.17). Perceived strengths for PR and RGS included performing competencies related to the role of Professional (3.69). PR and RGS responses regarding preparedness to practice PHM with respect to specific competencies are presented in Figure 2 . This analysis revealed that both PR and RGS felt unprepared to independently perform most (34/35) of the specific competencies specified for PHM practice. Specific competencies that both PR and RGS perceived to be least prepared for (means <3) are presented in Table 1 . Figure 1. Open in a new tab Paediatric residents and recently graduated staff perceived preparedness to practice paediatric hospital medicine in relation to CanMEDS roles. Figure 2. Open in a new tab Paediatric residents and recently graduated staff perceived preparedness to practice paediatric hospital medicine in relation to specific competencies. Table 1. Paediatric residents and recently graduated staff perceived weaknesses to practice paediatric hospital medicine in relation to specific competencies CanMEDS Role Competency Medical Expert Tracheostomy Care Tone Management Communicator Bereavement Counselling Presenting to the Public Collaborator Collaborating with Community Health Care Workers Manager (Leader) Participate in Quality Improvement Project Describe Principles of Health Care Financing Assess Cost-Benefit Analysis of Clinical Interventions Health Advocate Coordinate Out-Patient Resources Advocating at Government Level Scholar Conducting Research Address Learner in Difficulty Open in a new tab When exploring potential differences between PR and RGS, there was no difference between groups in the CanMEDS roles. Only 2 of 35 task competencies demonstrated a significant difference between the groups. For Q4 (providing acute patient handover), RGS reported significantly greater perceived preparedness at the end of their residency than PR. For Q15 (addressing a learner in difficulty and developing a plan to improve deficiencies), PR report significantly greater perceived preparedness at the end of their residency than RGS. Qualitative comments were equally important. Emerging themes and representative comments are summarized for PR ( Table 2 ) and for RGS ( Table 3 ). RGS and PR were asked if there were any competencies they felt were important for a hospital paediatrician to possess and not prepared for at the completion of their residency (Q55). Themes common to both groups emerged and related to a perceived lack of preparedness to manage care related to the complex care patient population, engage in discussion regarding end of life care, perform procedures, and pursue academic initiatives including research and quality improvement. To explore perceived deficiencies further, PR and RGS groups were asked to identify contributing factors (Q56). Both groups perceived lack of autonomy to make clinical decisions and limited exposure in residency to particular patient populations and difficult conversations. Residents perceived that gaps in curriculum design and competition among trainees to perform procedures contributed to areas of perceived weakness. Q57 asked respondents to identify factors they believed influenced areas of perceived strength. Mentorship throughout residency, exposure through ‘hands-on’ experience and elective experiences were elements that had a positive impact on perceived preparedness for both groups. Finally, respondents were asked what, if any changes they would suggest to the current training model to better prepare trainees for a career in PHM (Q59). Suggestions from PR and RGS included moving to a competency-based curriculum and increasing the number of rotations a trainee must complete at a small urban centre. Table 2. Qualitative responses: Paediatric residents perceived preparedness to practice paediatric hospital medicine Question 55: Prepared/not prepared Procedures Not enough experience doing intubations, IV’s, and inserting advanced lines Managerial Tasks Practice management Billing and how to run an office End of Life I feel that we have limited exposure to goals of care discussions Complex Care Spasticity management, secretion management Community Paediatrics Behaviour management Question 56: Deficiencies Lack of Exposure Lack of exposure (i.e., not one of our clinical rotations or an infrequent presentation) Curriculum No formal education on complex care (i.e., AHD or while on complex care rotation) Tertiary Care Specialization Being used to having others primarily manage certain issues such as multidisciplinary professionals (i.e., trach care being done primarily by RTs) that could potentially be managed by a general paediatrician Competition Fellows and clinical associates/assistants would often take procedures There were too many learners Autonomy Lack of autonomy to perform certain activities during residency Question 57: Strengths Exposure Lots of exposure to hospital medicine through rotations and call Mentorship Active/Engaging preceptors Independence Being the senior resident on CTU/nights allowed for discussion with parents when staff weren’t around (the independence was helpful) Electives I took extra interest in the area of complex care, and this was reflected in the selection of my electives Question 58: Changes to Better Prepare Institute Scholarly Requirements Make requirements for research/scholarly activity during residency to include all steps (i.e., resident must participate in getting ethics) Necessitate participation in quality improvement project (either an individual or as a group) Include training on advocacy, writing to government or talking to media Competencies Curriculum Must meet certain check marks for mandatory procedures prior to graduation. If cannot complete must show on mock model Small Urban Centre Experience More out-of-site paediatric electives would prepare anybody to have a better, more independent practice in a tertiary centre, where you don’t rely as much on subspecialists. More time in nontertiary care hospitals In-Scope Activities Allow residents to do more hands-on medicine, and less of the excessive paperwork and scut work that we had to do Currently Prepared None. Currently many trainees are likely over prepared for a tertiary care hospital setting and under prepared for community/rural/remote hospital setting Open in a new tab AHD Academic half-day; CTU Clinical teaching unit; IV Intravenous; RT Respiratory therapist. Table 3. Qualitative responses: Recently graduated staff perceived preparedness to practice paediatric hospital medicine Question 55: Prepared/not prepared Complex Care Management Initiation of medications for secretions, tone medications, when to consider an NG permanently (without G-tube), which kids are ‘actually’ higher risk for GA (i.e., difference between patients with neuromuscular disease and a young infant) Navigation of Challenging Situations/ Difficult Conversations How to navigate complex social situations (i.e., ways to diffuse situations, who to call when it’s beyond SW) Procedures Procedures including intubation, IV access, UVC/UAC, immunizations especially for paediatricians working in remote areas Question 56: Deficiencies Exposure Exposure to certain situations or issues may or may not occur directly during training Complex Care Lack of exposure (i.e., brain rehabilitation population, trach population) Navigating Difficult Conversations/Issues Not given opportunity to manage difficult cases/conversations independently Other Potentially lack of mentorship on transition to staff Question 57: Strengths Exposure Ample clinical opportunities Mentorship Great modeling/educators Autonomy Having done it independently previously Electives More elective time in a particular area Question 58: Changes to Better Prepare Complex Care More exposure to complex care, tracheostomy patients, brain rehabilitation patients Focused Areas of Competence More emphasis on medical technologies in academic half day Autonomy More autonomy in preparation to practice Rural Community Provide more opportunities for resident to work in remote areas with limited resources in order to realize their own deficiencies and go back to work on them Open in a new tab NG Nasogastric; GA General anaesthetic; SW Social work; UAC Umbilical arterial catheter; UVC Umbilical venous catheter. DISCUSSION Paediatric hospital medicine is an evolving subspecialty in North America with an increasing number of PHM fellowships being established in the USA and Canada. Given the growing acuity and complexity of the paediatric in-patient population, it is important that trainees feel prepared at the end of their residency to transition into the staff role in a hospital setting. Our study indicates three key findings: 1) Both PR who had just completed their RCPSC subspecialty examination, as well as RGS perceived that, at the on-set of starting independent practice, they would require assistance or consultation with the majority of representative PHM task competencies. 2) Differences in perception between the two groups were minimal, and 3) Sub-sets of perceived areas of particular strengths (Professional) and deficiencies (Medical Expert, Manager, and Communicator) are identified. This is the first Canadian study to look at preparedness to practice PHM based on the seven CanMEDS roles. Within the role of Communicator, there was a perceived lack of preparedness to provide bereavement counselling. This is similar to results obtained in two Canadian studies ( 11 , 12 ) and a recent American study ( 13 ). Review of our qualitative comments indicates that some residents do not believe they have sufficient opportunities to lead difficult conversations with patients and families during training. It is unclear whether trainees possess the competency and are not granted the opportunity to lead these conversations or do not possess the competency and subsequently staff feel uncomfortable delegating this task. We propose that supporting residents in being gradually more involved in navigating difficult conversations is essential in preparing them for a successful career as a hospital-based paediatrician. To ensure patient care needs are addressed in the hospital setting and during transition to the community setting, hospital paediatricians must be competent in the role of health advocate. They must be able to direct patients and families to appropriate resources and support groups given the patients unique medical needs. The RGS surveyed in this study perceived a lack of preparedness to independently perform these tasks at the completion of their training. Constrained time, lack of specific curricula for health advocacy training, and the lack of a gold standard for assessment have been previously identified as barriers to developing advocacy skills in graduate medical education ( 14 ). Additionally, there is a paucity of literature related to advocacy in PHM. Incorporating a longitudinal advocacy curriculum into protected academic time might better prepare trainees to be successful advocates for hospitalized paediatric patients at a local, provincial, and national level. With respect to the role of Medical Expert, our results highlight a perceived lack of preparedness to manage issues related to children requiring tracheostomy/ventilation as well as medical management for muscle tone issues. Participant comments reveal this lack of preparedness may result from insufficient exposure to medically complex children during training. Despite the fact that complex care teams exist in many tertiary care sites, it is unclear if PR are required to engage in these rotations. In addition, subspecialty groups and allied health professionals may participate substantially in the care of children with medical complexity. This may unintentionally lead to trainees having fewer opportunities to acquire the knowledge and skills related to managing these patients. Dedicated exposure to complex airway and physiatry clinics may enhance skills required to care for these specific paediatric inpatient populations. Scholarly output is an essential means to advance care for paediatric patients and promote the growth of PHM as a distinct subspecialty. Our results are consistent with those found in an American study by Bekmezian and colleagues, indicating that many hospital paediatricians would like to conduct research or quality improvement projects but feel that they lack the time, skills, resources, and mentorship to do so ( 15 ). These themes are also reflected in a recent Canadian study by Chan and van Manen, surveying paediatricians who had recently entered general practice ( 16 ). Instituting scholarly requirements and pairing learners with a scholarly mentor early in training are strategies that can be employed by paediatric residency program directors to improve perceived preparedness in these competencies. With respect to procedural skills, our qualitative data were consistent with results published by Levy and colleagues, demonstrating that graduating paediatric residents perceived a lack of preparedness to perform certain procedures including insertion of an interosseous device and placing a chest tube ( 17 ). Training at a tertiary care centre (with multiple trainees) may impact opportunities to participate in procedures, leading to perceived lack of preparedness. Experience with common procedures in tertiary care settings may be facilitated via communication with nursing (i.e., IV starts and NG tube insertions), whereas acquiring experience with rare procedures may be facilitated by targeted simulation practice. Exposing trainees to rural or small urban pediatric rotations (where there are fewer learners) may also support opportunities for residents to hone these procedural skills. In all CanMEDS roles ( Figure 1 ) and in several individual PHM competencies ( Figure 2 ), PR perceived greater preparedness than RGS. These differences may be reflective of: 1) better training and/ or 2) the difficulty in identifying areas of deficiency until encountered in independent clinical practice. If a trainee has not encountered many situations in a particular area, they may not recognize how well (or ill) prepared they are for independent practice. Limitations of this study include participant sample size and geographic location that may affect the generalizability of results. Significant challenges are associated with capturing the perspectives of residents shortly after completing the RCPSC exam as well as recently certified staff paediatricians. This study also asked paediatricians about their perceived preparedness. This is not exactly the same as their actual preparedness. Our responses relied on self-reporting that is subject to recall bias and memory effect. The survey included 35 items related to PHM to assess preparedness for practice. These competency-based activities represent a sampling of tasks a hospital paediatrician in a tertiary care setting could be reasonably expected to independently perform at the completion of residency. Finally, the use of a four-point Likert scale may serve as a limitation to our study. Our intention for this scale was to clearly delineate preparedness for ‘independent’ PHM practice and if not ‘independent’, to identify the degree of support perceived as being required to complete the task associated with PHM competencies. CONCLUSION This study highlights areas of perceived preparedness and lack of preparedness of PHM competencies at the time of expected transition to independent practice. These results may help inform future curricula for general paediatric residency programs as well as offer consideration of competencies that may be better targeted for a PHM fellowship program. By addressing areas of perceived weakness, educational leaders may facilitate the evolution of curricula and training programs to support physicians in being more fully prepared to transition to independent practice in Paediatric Hospital Medicine. Acknowledgements The authors wish to thank the participants including paediatric residents and recently graduated hospital paediatrician staff from the University of Alberta (Edmonton), University of British Columbia (Vancouver), University of Calgary, University of Ottawa, University of Toronto, Dalhousie University (Halifax), and McGill University (Montreal). We also extend our gratitude to Ms. Irenke Payne for her assistance with preparation of this manuscript. Funding: There are no funders to report for this submission. Potential Conflicts of Interest: All authors: No reported conflicts of interest. All authors have submitted the ICMJE Form for Disclosure of Potential Conflicts of Interest. Conflicts that the editors consider relevant to the content of the manuscript have been disclosed. References 1. Barrett DJ, McGuinness GA, Cunha CA, et al. Pediatric hospital medicine: A proposed new subspecialty. Pediatrics. 2017;139(3). e20161823. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Berry JG, Hall M, Hall DE, et al. Inpatient growth and resource use in 28 children’s hospitals: A longitudinal, multi-institutional study. JAMA Pediatr 2013;167(2):170–7. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Merenstein D, Egleston B, Diener-West M. Lengths of stay and costs associated with children’s hospitals. Pediatrics 2005;115(4):839–44. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Jerardi KE, Fisher ES, Rassbach C, et al. Framework for pediatric hospital medicine fellowships. Pediatrics. 2017;140(1): e20170698. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. The American Board of Pediatrics. Pediatric Hospital Medicine Certification < https://www.abp.org/content/pediatric-hospital-medicine-certification > (Accessed September 21, 2018). 6. Bismilla Z, Dubrowski A, Amin HJ. Program directors’ perceptions of importance of pediatric procedural skills and resident preparedness. BMC Res Notes 2015;8:550. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. The Royal College of Physicians and Surgeons of Canada. 2008. Objectives of training in pediatrics < http://rspsc.medical.org > (Accessed July 1, 2017). 8. Amin HJ, Singhal N, Cole G. Validating objectives and training in Canadian paediatrics residency training programmes. Med Teach 2011;33(3):e131–44. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. The Royal College of Physicians and Surgeons of Canada. CanMEDS: Better standards, better physicians, better care < http://www.royalcollege.ca/rcsite/canmeds/canmeds-framework-e > (Accessed July 9, 2017). 10. Braun V, Clarke V. Using thematic analysis in psychology. Qual Res Psycho. 2007;3:77–101. [ Google Scholar ] 11. Lieberman L, Hilliard RI. How well do paediatric residency programmes prepare residents for clinical practice and their future careers? Med Educ 2006;40(6):539–46. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. Kauffman M. 2016. Professional Bereavement Education for Pediatric Residents < pediatrics.med.sc.edu > (Accessed March 23, 2019). 13. Pohl CE, III. Evaluation of residents’ level of comfort with palliative care concepts before and after educational intervention. Pediatrics. 2018;141(1). Meeting Abstract. Section on Hospice and Palliative Medicine Program. [ Google Scholar ] 14. Poulton A, Rose H. The importance of health advocacy in Canadian postgraduate medical education: Current attitudes and issues. Can Med Educ J 2015;6(2):e54–60. [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 15. Bekmezian A, Teufel RJ 2nd, Wilson KM. Research needs of pediatric hospitalists. Hosp Pediatr 2011;1(1):38–44. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 16. Chan M, van Manen MA. Exploring the transition into practice of general paediatricians from a Canadian residency program. Paediatr Child Health 2018;23(5):314–8. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 17. Levy R, Dubrowski A, Amin H, Bismilla Z. Procedural skills in paediatric residency: Re-evaluating the competencies. Paediatr Child Health 2014;19(4):180–4. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',12,'2025-10-10 08:39:34','2025-10-10 08:39:34');
