ers in Pediatrics 10 (2022): 928476. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 43. Moon M., Taylor H. A., McDonald E. L., Hughes M. T., and Carrese J. A., “Everyday Ethics Issues in the Outpatient Clinical Practice of Pediatric Residents,” Archives of Pediatrics & Adolescent Medicine 163, no. 9 (September 2009): 838–843. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 44. Mitchell S., Slowther A. M., Coad J., and Dale J., “Experiences of Healthcare, Including Palliative Care, of Children With Life‐Limiting and Life‐Threatening Conditions and Their Families: A Longitudinal Qualitative Investigation,” Archives of Disease in Childhood 106, no. 6 (June 2021): 570–576. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 45. Koh M. Y. H., Gallardo M. D., Khoo H. S., and Hum A., “Burnout in Palliative Care—Difficult Cases: Qualitative Study,” BMJ Support Palliat Care 13, no. e3 (January 2024): e1383–e1389. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 46. Parola V., Coelho A., Cardoso D., Sandgren A., and Apóstolo J., “Prevalence of Burnout in Health Professionals Working in Palliative Care: A Systematic Review,” JBI Database of Systematic Reviews and Implementation Reports 15, no. 7 (July 2017): 1905–1933. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 47. Attard‐Montalto S., “Ethical Issues in Paediatric Practice—Part II: Issues Relating to Disability,” Images in Paediatric Cardiology 4, no. 1 (January 2002): 30–32. [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 48. Hester D. M., “Ethical Issues in Pediatrics,” in Guidance for Healthcare Ethics Committees [Internet], eds. Hester D. M. and Schonfeld T.. (Cambridge University Press, 2012), 114–121, https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/guidance-for-healthcare-ethics-committees/ethical-issues-in-pediatrics/171696C68A1B120749A5F197BD05BE0A . [ Google Scholar ] 49. Vemuri S., Hynson J., Williams K., and Gillam L., “Decision‐Making Approaches for Children With Life‐Limiting Conditions: Results From a Qualitative Phenomenologic