] [ Google Scholar ] 41. Rose P, DeGeest K, McMeekin S, Fusco N. A phase I study of gemcitabine followed by cisplatin concurrent with whole pelvic radiation therapy in locally advanced cervical cancer: A Gynecologic Oncology Group study. Gynecol Oncol. 2007;107:274–279. doi: 10.1016/j.ygyno.2007.06.012. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 42. Thomas G. Are we making progress in curing advanced cervical cancer? J Clin Oncol. 2011;29:1654–1656. doi: 10.1200/JCO.2010.34.1966. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 43. Xu H, Fairman JW, Wijerathna SR, Kreischer NR, LaMacchia J, et al. The structural basis for peptidomimetic inhibition of eukaryotic ribonucleotide reductase: A conformationally flexible pharmacophore. J Med Chem. 2008;51:4653–4659. doi: 10.1021/jm800350u. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 44. Davis ME, Zuckerman JE, Choi CH, Seligson D, Tolcher A, et al. Evidence of RNAi in humans from systemically administered siRNA via targeted nanoparticles. Nature. 2010;464:1067–1070. doi: 10.1038/nature08956. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 45. Eriksson S, Munch-Petersen B, Johansson K, Eklund H. Structure and function of cellular deoxyribonucleoside kinases. Cell Mol Life Sci. 2002;59:1327–1346. doi: 10.1007/s00018-002-8511-x. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 46. Fujiwaki R, Hata K, Moriyama M, Iwanari O, Katabuchi H, et al. Clinical value of thymidine kinase in patients with cervical carcinoma. Oncology. 2001;61:47–54. doi: 10.1159/000055352. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 47. Belt JA, Marina NM, Phelps DA, Crawford CR. Nucleoside transport in normal and neoplastic cells. Adv Enzyme Regul. 1993;33:235–252. doi: 10.1016/0065-2571(93)90021-5. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 48. Boothman DA, Davis TW, Sahijdak WM. Enhanced expression of thymidine kinase in human cells following ionizing radiation. Int J Radiat Oncol Biol Phys. 1994;30:391–398. doi: 10.1016/0360-3016(94)90019-1.