tob e0152838 samir hydrosulphide wray gyy4137 relaxes e46278 mijuskovic kokic dusic slavic spasic blagojevic sulphide mijuskovic wray arrowsmith sulphide atb crosignani coulam kutteh raghupathy jaslow kutteh polgar longobardi matarese konkel tet1 demethylation velicky knofler pollheimer migr arbeille groom oyston chamley cindrova villus zochio possomato chimini fetoplacental butylphthalide kodela chattopadhyay kashfi nosh','Abstract The placenta plays a crucial role in maintaining pregnancy stability, regulating fetal growth, and facilitating maternal–fetal exchange. Its proper development relies on placental vasculogenesis and angiogenesis, along with adaptive remodeling of the maternal uterine vasculature. However, the mechanisms underlying placental development and the successful establishment of the maternal–fetal interface remain incompletely understood. As the third gaseous signaling molecule identified after nitric oxide (NO) and carbon monoxide (CO), hydrogen sulfide (H₂S) has been demonstrated to regulate vascular adaptation during pregnancy by promoting angiogenesis and neovascularization. Dysregulated H₂S biosynthesis has been implicated in pregnancy complications, including preeclampsia (PE), fetal growth restriction (FGR), and preterm birth (PTB), underscoring its potential as a therapeutic target. Recent studies have revealed that H₂S regulates placental function via multiple signaling pathways, including activating adenosine triphosphate (ATP)-sensitive K + (K ATP ) channels, large-conductance calcium-activated potassium channels (BK Ca channels), endothelial nitric oxide synthase (eNOS) signaling, and mitochondrial dynamics. Notably, H₂S-mediated persulfidation of mitochondrial Rho GTPase 2 (Miro2) has been shown to maintain trophoblast invasiveness and promote placental vascular homeostasis. Additionally, exogenous H₂S donors (e.g., GYY4137 and NaHS) have demonstrated therapeutic potential in experimental models, effectively reversing PE-like pathologies, improving placental perfusion, and restoring trophoblast function. Research further indicates that BK Ca channels play a key role in H₂S-mediated vasodilation by modulating intracellular Ca²⁺ flux, which influences placental vascular tone and perfusion, reinforcing the importance of H₂S in maternal–fetal circulation regulation. This review provides a comprehensive summary of H₂S biosynthesis and metabolism and its regulatory role in early placental development. Notably, elevated estrogen levels during pregnancy have been identified as key regulators of H₂S production, and we discuss the molecular mechanisms by which estrogen modulates H₂S synthesis. Furthermore, we discuss the vascular-protective and anti-inflammatory properties of H₂S donors and dual-donor strategies. As research continues to reveal H₂S-mediated mechanisms in placental function and pregnancy disorders, optimizing the pharmacological application and clinical translation of H₂S donors and combination therapies will be a key research focus. Advancing H₂S metabolic regulation, signaling pathways, and targeted delivery systems may drive the development of novel diagnostic tools and therapeutic strategies for pregnancy complications. Supplementary Information The online version contains supplementary material available at 10.1186/s12958-025-01465-1. Keywords: Hydrogen sulfide (H 2 S), Placental development, Spiral artery remodeling (SAR), Uterine blood flow (UtBF), H₂S donors, Estradiol-β, Preeclampsia (PE), Fetal growth restriction (FGR) Introduction During pregnancy, the placenta plays a pivotal role in maintaining maternal‒fetal homeostasis by regulating nutrient and gas exchange, hormone secretion, and immune tolerance at the maternal–fetal interface [ 1 , 2 ]. Its proper development relies on placental vasculogenesis and angiogenesis, as well as the adaptive remodeling of maternal uterine vasculature [ 3 – 5 ]. However, the placenta lacks intrinsic vascular regulation, and its perfusion relies on dynamic maternal hemodynamic changes, especially in the uterine arteries (UAs) [ 5 , 6 ]. A key process in placental vascular adaptation is spiral artery remodeling (SAR), which is initiated by extravillous trophoblast (EVT) invasion and completed through endothelial and smooth muscle cell (SMC) replacement and vascular dilation [ 7 – 10 ]. This remodeling process creates a high-capacity, low-resistance circulatory bed critical for optimal fetal perfusion [ 11 – 14 ] (Fig. 1 ). Abnormal EVT invasion and inadequate SAR are strongly associated with major pregnancy complications, including preeclampsia (PE) [ 15 – 18 ], fetal growth restriction (FGR) [ 19 – 21 ], recurrent spontaneous abortion (RSA) [ 22 – 24 ], and preterm birth (PTB) [ 25 ], all of which can affect long-term maternal and fetal health [ 26 – 29 ]. Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Maternal-Fetal Interface During Early Placental Development. In early pregnancy, EVTs originating from the cytotrophoblast shell invade the maternal decidua and SAs, orchestrating essential vascular remodeling. iEVTs infiltrate the decidual stroma, while eEVTs migrate into the SA lumen, progressively replacing endothelial cells and inducing VSMC degradation. This transformation converts the tightly coiled, high-resistance SAs into widened, low-resistance vessels, optimizing maternal blood flow to the placenta and sustaining fetal development. Notes: EVTs: extravillous trophoblasts; iEVTs: interstitial EVTs; eEVTs: endovascular EVTs; SA: spiral Arteries; VSMC: vascular smooth muscle cell Recent genetic studies suggest that placental dysfunction is often underrecognized as a primary cause of congenital anomalies. Mouse models have shown that embryo lethality caused by certain gene deletions can be rescued by functional placentas, indicating that the placenta may play a decisive role in fetal survival and organogenesis [ 30 – 35 ]. For example, mutations in Pparg- and Mapk14 (p38α)-mutant have been linked to cardiac malformations secondary to placental defects [ 36 , 37 ]. Clinical studies further support a strong association between placental abnormalities and neonatal neurodevelopmental disorders [ 38 – 45 ]. These findings underscore the need to understand the molecular signals that govern placental vascular function and remodeling. Among emerging molecular regulators, hydrogen sulfide (H₂S) has attracted growing interest. Recognized as the third gasotransmitter [ 46 ] following nitric oxide (NO) [ 47 ] and carbon monoxide (CO) [ 48 ]. H₂S exerts diverse effects on vascular biology, including vasodilation, angiogenesis, anti-inflammation, and antioxidant defense [ 49 – 54 ]. In the context of pregnancy, H₂S enhances uteroplacental blood flow by activating adenosine triphosphate (ATP)-sensitive K+ (K ATP ) and large-conductance calcium-activated potassium (BK Ca ) channels [ 51 – 54 ], potentiating NO-mediated vasodilation [ 55 , 56 ], and modulating EVT invasion and vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF)-driven angiogenesis [ 49 , 57 , 58 ]. Notably, it maintains placental vascular homeostasis even in pathological conditions such as PE and FGR. Despite its promising vascular-protective potential, the precise roles and regulatory mechanisms of H₂S in placental development remain incompletely defined. Questions persist regarding how H₂S modulates trophoblast behavior, SAR, and local hemodynamics during early pregnancy. Moreover, the interplay between H₂S and estrogen, a key hormone elevated during pregnancy, may offer additional insight into H₂S biosynthesis and action within the uteroplacental environment. Given that many pregnancy complications such as PE, FGR, PTB, and placenta accreta originate in early placentation but typically manifest later in gestation [ 59 , 60 ], understanding early molecular disruptions is crucial for advancing predictive and therapeutic strategies. This review aims to comprehensively summarize the emerging roles of H₂S in placental physiology, including its effects on vascular remodeling, trophoblast function, oxidative stress, and immune regulation. We further discuss the regulation of H₂S biosynthesis by estrogen and the therapeutic potential of H₂S donors, highlighting future directions for clinical translation. H₂S biosynthesis and mechanisms Historical background and significance of H₂S H₂S was historically viewed as a toxic gas, primarily due to its inhibitory effects on mitochondrial respiration at high concentrations. However, research in the mid-20th century revealed its endogenous production in mammals, challenging the traditional toxicological paradigm [ 61 , 62 ]. In 1996, Hideo Kimura identified H₂S as neuromodulator synthesized in the brain and proposed its role as a signaling molecule [ 63 ]. This discovery, followed by studies demonstrating H₂S-induced vasodilation and anti-inflammatory activity, laid the foundation for its recognition as the third endogenous gasotransmitter alongside NO and CO [ 46 , 64 , 65 ]. Additionally, H₂S plays crucial regulatory roles in both male and female reproductive systems and pregnancy [ 66 – 69 ], supporting processes such as oocyte maturation, corpus luteum maintenance, sperm production and motility, trophoblast invasion, and placental blood flow optimization. These functions collectively ensure normal reproductive processes and healthy pregnancy progression. Biosynthesis and metabolic pathways of H₂S The synthesis and metabolism of H₂S in vivo involve a complex interplay of enzymatic and nonenzymatic pathways. Its biosynthesis occurs through enzymatic reactions, while its degradation involves oxidation, methylation, binding to methemoglobin, and exhalation in a gaseous form. Enzymatic pathways of H₂S synthesis H₂S is primarily synthesized by three key enzymes: cystathionine β-synthase (CBS), cystathionine γ-lyase (CSE), and 3-mercaptopyruvate sulfurtransferase (3-MST). These enzymes collectively regulate H₂S production through interrelated metabolic pathways and function in specific physiological environments, including the uterus, placenta, ovaries, embryonic development, and pregnancy maintenance [ 69 ] (Fig. 2 ). CBS/CSE-H₂S systems are expressed in the UA across species such as sheep [ 70 ], rats, and humans [ 71 ], suggesting a potential role in uterine blood flow (UtBF) regulation. Fig. 2. Open in a new tab Biosynthesis of H₂S and tissue distribution of key enzymes (CBS, CSE, and 3-MST). A H₂S biosynthesis pathways. H₂S is synthesized through three key enzymes: CBS, CSE, and3-MST. CBS catalyzes the conversion of Hcy and Ser into Cth, which is further degraded by CSE to produce Cys and H₂S. CBS can also generate H₂S by catalyzing Hcy condensation or using Cys as a substrate. CSE produces H₂S through cystathionine degradation and alternative reactions involving L-cysteine or homocysteine, leading to the formation of lanthionine, homolanthionine, pyruvate, α-KB, and H₂S. 3-MST converts 3-MP, derived from L-cysteine via CAT, into a persulfide intermediate, which is reduced to release H₂S. Additionally, 3-MP can undergo non-enzymatic degradation to form H₂S. DAO also contributes to H₂S production by converting D-cysteine into 3-MP, particularly in the kidney and cerebellum. B Tissue distribution of CBS, CSE, and 3-MST. The expression patterns of these enzymes vary across different organs. Notes: CBS: Cystathionine β-Synthase; CSE: Cystathionine γ-Lyase; CAT: Catalase; DAO: D-Amino Acid Oxidase; 3-MST: 3-Mercaptopyruvate Sulfurtransferase; 3MP: 3-Mercaptopyruvate; α-KB: α-Ketobutyrate; Pyr: Pyruvate; Cys: L-cysteine; Hcy: Homocysteine; Ser: Serine; α-KA: α-Keto Acid; Cth: Cystathionine༛Glu: Glutamate; -SSH: Persulfide; Trx: Thioredoxin; DHLA: Dihydrolipoic Acid CBS, encoded on chromosome 21, is a pyridoxal-5’-phosphate (PLP)-dependent enzyme and the first in the transsulfuration pathway. It catalyzes the conversion of homocysteine (Hcy) and serine (Ser) or cysteine to cystathionine (Cth) and H₂S [ 63 , 72 – 77 ]. CBS is highly expressed in the central nervous system, liver, gastrointestinal tract, placenta, and uterine vasculature, where it supports placental angiogenesis and fetal perfusion via H₂S-mediated signaling involving VEGF, placental growth factor (PlGF), and mitogen-activated protein kinases (MAPK) pathways [ 68 , 70 , 71 , 78 , 79 ]. During pregnancy, CBS expression is upregulated in both the placenta and UA [ 70 , 71 ], and its dysregulation is implicated in pathologies such as Down syndrome [ 80 – 82 ]. Knockout mouse models reveal that CBS deficiency impairs placental growth, uterine weight, and follicular maturation, with disrupted estrous cycles and reduced conception rates, highlighting its importance in maintaining a supportive reproductive environment [ 83 – 87 ]. Beyond H₂S synthesis, CBS can also produce NO or peroxynitrite (ONOO⁻) through interactions with substrates like nitrite (NO₂⁻), implicating its role in vascular tone regulation and oxidative stress balance [ 88 ]. CSE, encoded on chromosome 1, is another PLP-dependent enzyme predominantly expressed in the cardiovascular system, respiratory system, kidneys, liver, uterus, and peripheral tissues [ 89 – 91 ]. CSE is highly expressed in the cytoplasm but can translocate to mitochondria under specific conditions [ 92 ]. It catalyzes H₂S production from Cth or cysteine via multiple β- or γ-cleavage reactions [ 76 , 93 – 95 ]. While CBS is estrogen-sensitive and dynamically expressed during pregnancy, CSE expression remains relatively stable [ 70 , 71 ]. CSE-knockout mice show vascular dysfunction, decreased serum H₂S, and impaired pregnancy outcomes, including reduced implantation success and increased fetal inflammation, suggesting roles in maternal vascular adaptation and anti-inflammatory regulation [ 84 , 96 ]. 3-MST, encoded on chromosome 22, is a non-PLP-dependent mitochondrial enzyme that uses 3-mercaptopyruvate (3-MP) as substrate, with cysteine aminotransferase (CAT) and dihydrolipoic acid as cofactors [ 86 , 97 – 102 ]. It is widely distributed in the liver, kidneys, testes, brain, and endocrine organs, whereas lower levels are found in the spleen, thymus, lungs, and intestines [ 98 , 99 ]. It catalyzes the formation of persulfides (–SSH) and contributes to redox regulation and mitochondrial H₂S reservoirs [ 102 – 105 ]. While 3-MST and its partner enzyme CAT are expressed in UA, their expression levels remain unchanged throughout the menstrual cycle and pregnancy [ 71 ].Of note, a fourth enzyme D-amino acid oxidase (DAO), can generate 3-MP from D-cysteine, which is subsequently processed by 3-MST/CAT to produce H₂S. This pathway is highly expressed in the kidneys and cerebellum, with studies indicating that D-cysteine generates H₂S approximately 60 times more efficiently than L-cysteine (Cys) does [ 106 ], making it a potential therapeutic strategy for targeted H₂S delivery in these organs. However, DAO expression in vascular cells remains unreported. Microbial and dietary sources of H₂S: enzymatic and nonenzymatic pathways Beyond endogenous enzymatic synthesis, gut microbiota, diet, and nonenzymatic chemical reactions provide significant contributions to systemic H₂S production, particularly in the gastrointestinal and circulatory systems. These sources work synergistically under physiological and pathological conditions to maintain H₂S homeostasis and expand its biological functionality. The gut microbiota represents a significant source of H₂S, which is generated through both enzymatic and nonenzymatic mechanisms. Sulfate-reducing bacteria (SRB), such as Desulfotomaculum , Desulfovibrio , and Desulfobacter [ 107 ], dominate the anaerobic colonic environment, utilizing sulfate (SO₄²⁻) as a terminal electron acceptor to produce sulfide (S²⁻), which subsequently converts to H₂S under acidic conditions [ 108 ]. In addition, non-SRB taxa including Escherichia coli , Salmonella enterica , and Clostridium species, generate H₂S via Cys metabolism through Cys desulfhydrase [ 109 , 110 ]. Some gut-resident bacteria such as Staphylococcus and Enterobacter generate sulfite (SO₃²⁻) or thiosulfate (S₂O₃²⁻), which can undergo nonenzymatic reduction to produce H₂S [ 111 , 112 ]. Dietary sulfur compounds also contribute to H₂S availability. Enzymatically, cruciferous vegetables (e.g., mustard greens, Chinese kale, and broccoli) are rich in isothiocyanates and glucosinolates, which are metabolized in the gut to release H₂S [ 113 ]. Garlic-derived organosulfur compounds such as diallyl trisulfide (DATS) can release H₂S through thiol-disulfide exchange reactions with glutathione (GSH), also modulating cellular redox status [ 113 – 116 ]. Recent evidence suggests that nonenzymatic H₂S formation can also occur in peripheral tissues via interactions between cysteine and diet-derived ferric iron (Fe³⁺) [ 117 ] and PLP, the active form of vitamin B6 [ 117 , 118 ]. These reactions are promoted under oxidative stress, particularly in the lungs, heart, and spleen, and may serve as compensatory mechanisms for maintaining local H₂S levels when enzymatic activity is impaired. Recent studies have shown that certain dietary sulfur compounds can modulate mammalian signaling pathways by releasing H₂S, which plays essential roles in cardiovascular, gastrointestinal, neural, and immune functions. Reduced H₂S levels are associated with disorders such as hypertension, diabetes, and immune dysregulation [ 119 ]. Meat-derived proteins serve as key substrates for microbial H₂S production in the colon [ 120 ]. Once absorbed into the intestinal circulation, H₂S can act on blood vessels, activate enteric neurons, and influence systemic blood pressure regulation [ 121 ]. Additionally, long-term adherence to sulfur-rich dietary patterns has been linked to increased risk of distal colorectal cancer [ 122 ], likely mediated by sulfur-metabolizing bacteria. These findings highlight the systemic impact of diet–microbiota–H₂S interactions and their potential relevance to maternal and placental health. In summary, these microbial, dietary, and endogenous metabolism form an interconnected network, which complements enzymatic pathways and contributes to immune modulation, vascular regulation, and oxidative homeostasis—processes highly relevant in pregnancy. Under hypoxic or inflammatory conditions, such as those seen in placental insufficiency or gestational disorders, these alternative sources may become particularly active and influence maternal-fetal adaptation. Metabolic pathways of H₂S To maintain physiological H₂S homeostasis, biological systems use three primary metabolic pathways to eliminate or degrade H₂S, ensuring that H₂S levels remain within physiological limits and preventing its accumulation. These pathways include the mitochondrial enzymatic sulfide oxidation pathway, the cytosolic methylation pathway, and the heme-dependent sulfide oxidation pathway [ 93 ]. Mitochondrial enzymatic oxidation: the primary clearance route Within mitochondria, H₂S is oxidized by sulfide quinone oxidoreductase (SQR) into protein-bound -SSH [ 123 , 124 ], with released electrons entering the electron transport chain, linking H₂S metabolism to cellular respiration and energy production [ 125 , 126 ]. Subsequent oxidation involves persulfide dioxygenase (ETHE1) and sulfite oxidase (SUOX), producing sulfate (SO₄²⁻) for urine excretion [ 127 – 129 ]. However, when SO₃²⁻ accumulates beyond the metabolic capacity of SUOX, some SO₃²⁻ reacts with -SSH via thiosulfate sulfurtransferase (TST), forming thiosulfate (S₂O₃²⁻) [ 76 , 113 , 130 ], which serves as a reversible H₂S storage form, releasing H₂S when needed. Tissue-specific variations exist: the liver favors SO₄²⁻ generation, kidneys produce both S₂O₃²⁻ and SO₄²⁻, and colonic epithelial cells—constantly exposed to microbially derived H₂S—exhibit high SQR and TST expression to rapidly oxidize and buffer excess H₂S [ 93 , 131 ]. This pathway not only detoxifies H₂S but also integrates redox and energy homeostasis. Cytosolic methylation: a secondary backup In the cytoplasm, thiol S-methyltransferase catalyzes H₂S methylation using S-adenosylmethionine to genetate methanethiol (CH₃SH). CH₃SH can then be further converted into thiocyanate or dimethyl sulfide for excretion via urine or exhalation [ 93 , 113 , 127 , 131 ].Although the methylation pathway contributes to H₂S metabolism, its physiological significance is relatively minor compared with that of mitochondrial oxidation. Studies suggest that under low H₂S conditions, methylation may be enhanced to assist in H₂S clearance, whereas when H₂S levels exceed physiological thresholds, mitochondrial oxidation remains the predominant degradation mechanism to maintain homeostasis [ 113 ]. Heme-dependent oxidation: a buffering and regulatory mechanism In plasma, H₂S binds to deoxyhemoglobin forming sulf-hemoglobin (S-Hb), reducing free H₂S toxicity and releasing it in response to fluctuating blood oxygen levels [ 132 , 133 ]. Under oxidative conditions, methemoglobin or Fe³⁺ mediates H₂S oxidation to S₂O₃²⁻ and polysulfides (HSxS⁻) [ 134 – 136 ], which serve as temporary reservoirs or signaling molecules. S₂O₃²⁻ primarily serves as an intermediate in H₂S oxidation, eventually converting to SO₄²⁻ and excreted in the urine. Under specific conditions, it may also function as a reversible storage form of H₂S. Moreover, HSxS⁻, a byproduct of H₂S oxidation, can serve as a temporary H₂S reservoir and be cleared through renal, pulmonary, or metabolic pathways [ 137 – 139 ]. This mechanism not only buffers systemic H₂S fluctuations but also modulates vascular tone, especially relevant in pregnancy, where H₂S influences uteroplacental blood flow, fetal lung development, and hypoxic adaptation [ 93 , 140 – 142 ]. H₂S metabolism involves both irreversible degradation and reversible storage-release cycles, forming a homeostatic buffering system. Under oxidative stress or hypoxia, thiosulfate and HSxS⁻ may regenerate H₂S, contributing to vascular protection, redox signaling, and metabolic adaptation. Importantly, dysregulated H₂S metabolism is implicated in diverse pathologies, including mitochondrial dysfunction (such as ETHE1 deficiency leading to excessive H₂S accumulation) [ 129 , 140 ], cardiovascular diseases (H₂S metabolism abnormalities contributing to hypertension and atherosclerosis), and inflammatory disorders (both H₂S excess and deficiency affecting immune regulation) [ 86 , 143 ]. H₂S metabolism may also be closely linked to pregnancy-related complications, where elevated Hcy may interfere with H₂S production and contribute to PE, RSA, elevated uterine arterial resistance, and fetal congenital anomalies [ 144 – 146 ]. Excessive H₂S may inhibit mitochondrial cytochrome c oxidase, impairing energy metabolism [ 147 ]. While H₂S exerts diverse physiological effects across multiple organ systems, this review centers on its roles in placental development and pregnancy-related pathologies, with systemic functions discussed only insofar as they contribute to maternal-fetal health. The role of H₂S in pregnancy-associated vascular adaptation and placental function Successful placental development depends on the intricate interactions between fetal trophoblasts and the maternal decidua, particularly the differentiation of trophoblasts and remodeling of uterine vasculature. During early pregnancy, cytotrophoblasts (CTBs) give rise to syncytiotrophoblasts (STBs) for nutrient exchange, and EVTs, which invade the decidua and drive SAR [ 8 , 148 ]. SAR is a critical process for uterine vascular adaptation during pregnancy, whereby eEVTs invade the vascular lumen, replace endothelial cells (ECs), and restructure the vessel wall [ 9 , 149 ]. Immune cells such as macrophages and uterine natural killer (uNK) cells assist by releasing matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs) and proangiogenic signals [ 9 ]. In early gestation, EVT plugs transiently block maternal red blood cell entry into the intervillous space. Imaging studies, such as ultrafast functional photoacoustic microscopy, reveal that this hypoxic environment supports vascular expansion and placental development until maternal blood flow is fully established by 10–12 weeks [ 150 – 153 ]. Meanwhile, UAs and other branches undergo structural adaptation, with UtBF increasing up to 50-fold in late pregnancy [ 154 , 155 ]. However, UA hemodynamic abnormalities or inadequate SAR can lead to restricted placental perfusion, contributing to pregnancy complications such as FGR [ 21 , 156 ], PE [ 15 , 157 ], stillbirth, and RSA [ 12 ]. Gasotransmitters such as H₂S, NO, and CO are increasingly recognized as key regulators of placental vascular adaptation. H₂S, in particular, promotes uterine and placental vasodilation, supports trophoblast function, reduces oxidative stress, and modulates inflammation [ 158 ]. The following sections focus on the molecular mechanisms by which H₂S contributes to vascular regulation, trophoblast function, UA dilation, and oxidative stress/inflammation modulation, offering insights into potential therapeutic targets for pregnancy-related disorders. The role of H₂S in placental vascular regulation and trophoblast function Normal placental development relies on trophoblast proliferation, migration, and invasion, which are essential for successful embryo implantation and maternal–fetal vascular remodeling. H₂S is increasingly recognized as a key modulator of these processes, not only by supporting trophoblast function but also by regulating vascular tone within the placental circulation. Mohammed et al. [ 159 ]. reported vessel-specific responses to H₂S in human placental vasculature: while H₂S did not induce vasodilation in human umbilical arteries and instead triggered mild contraction via inhibition of the NO/soluble guanylate cyclase (sGC) signaling pathway, it caused vasorelaxation in human umbilical veins through the activation of K ATP channels. These findings underscore the context- and vessel-dependent nature of H₂S action in shaping feto-placental hemodynamics. The role of H₂S in embryo implantation H₂S facilitates embryo implantation by promoting endometrial receptivity and embryo–epithelial adhesion. In vitro, silencing CBS in JAR cells significantly impairs embryo attachment to Ishikawa cells, indicating the role of CBS-derived H₂S in supporting adhesion via the transsulfuration pathway [ 160 ]. In vivo, inhibition of H₂S synthesis reduces implantation site numbers, likely by interfering with CFTR-mediated transepithelial anion transport and altering the uterine microenvironment [ 161 ]. These findings suggest that H₂S contributes to the establishment of implantation niches, thereby initiating successful placental development. H₂S and placental angiogenesis Placental angiogenesis is primarily regulated by local signaling molecules rather than autonomic innervation. H₂S complements the roles of NO [ 5 ] and CO [ 162 ] by promoting endothelial proliferation, migration, and tube formation. CBS and CSE are highly expressed in placental endothelial and trophoblast cells, with CBS also found in Hofbauer cells [ 158 , 163 – 165 ]. Reduced expression of these enzymes in preeclamptic placentas correlates with impaired angiogenesis, diminished trophoblast invasion, and SAR defects [ 158 , 165 ]. Existing evidence suggests that H₂S critically modulates placental angiogenesis (Fig. 3 ). Fig. 3. Open in a new tab Multiple Mechanisms of H₂S in Regulating Placental Angiogenesis. H₂S modulates placental vascular function and trophoblast invasion through multiple pathways, facilitating adaptive adjustments in placental blood flow. In early pregnancy, CBS-mediated H₂S production increases, which downregulates sFlt-1 and sEng expression, thereby enhancing PlGF levels and promoting trophoblast invasion, facilitating placental implantation and early development. In late pregnancy, H₂S regulates VEGF release by inhibiting miR-200c targeting VEGF mRNA and suppressing miR-20a and miR-20b targeting VEGF protein. This process enhances VEGF release, promotes placental vascular dilation, and optimizes placental blood flow supply. Overexpression of miR-21 inhibits CSE and SP1 transcription factors, reducing placental H₂S production. Additionally, miR-21 promotes the proliferation of VSMCs while inhibiting their differentiation, thereby affecting placental vascular function. H₂S primarily enhances NOS3 phosphorylation through the AKT1-NOS3 signaling pathway, which promotes NO production, leading to vasodilation and improved blood perfusion. The PI3K-specific inhibitor Wortmannin blocks NOS3 phosphorylation, whereas the MAPK3/1-specific inhibitor U0126 does not affect NOS3 activity. Moreover, H₂S activates the MAPK3/1 signaling pathway, enhancing placental endothelial cell proliferation and migration, further promoting placental angiogenesis. The H₂S donor NaHS significantly enhances these effects. Notes: sFlt-1: Soluble fms-like tyrosine kinase-1; sEng: Soluble endoglin; PlGF: Placental growth factor; VEGF: Vascular Endothelial Growth Factor; PE: Preeclampsia; SP1: Specificity Protein 1; NO: Nitric Oxide; miR-21: miRNA-21; VSMCs: Vascular Smooth Muscle Cells; AKT1: AKT Serine/Threonine Kinase 1; NOS3: Nitric Oxide Synthase 3; MAPK3/1: Mitogen-Activated Protein Kinase 3/1 Mechanistically, H₂S enhances VEGF expression by stabilizing VEGF mRNA and downregulating microRNAs such as miR-200c, miR-20a, and miR-20b, which suppress VEGF synthesis and release [ 57 , 166 ]. Wang et al. reported that H₂S donors also upregulate PlGF while suppressing antiangiogenic factors such as soluble fms-like tyrosine kinase-1 (sFlt-1) and soluble endoglin (sEng), thus favoring a proangiogenic environment [ 158 ]. Beyond VEGF signaling, H₂S activates the PI3K/AKT1–NOS3 and MAPK3/1 pathways, enhancing endothelial NO production and angiogenic potential [ 167 ]. Beyond VEGF signaling, H₂S promotes placental angiogenesis via the AKT1–NOS3 and MAPK3/1 pathways. Exogenous H₂S donors (e.g., NaHS) enhance placental EC proliferation, migration, and tube formation, while trophoblast-derived H₂S further facilitates endothelial migration in a paracrine manner [ 49 , 167 – 169 ]. Coculture studies show that HTR-8/SVneo cells drive ovine fetoplacental artery ECs migration via CBS-derived H₂S, whereas BeWo cells exhibit a stronger promigratory effect requiring both CBS and CSE. The coordinated inhibition of CBS (with CHH) and CSE (with BCA) abolishes H₂S synthesis in BeWo cells, highlighting cell type-specific regulation. Primary villous trophoblasts, coexpressing CBS and CTH, display the strongest ability to promote endothelial migration. Mechanistically, H₂S activates phosphorylation of AKT1, MAPK3/1, and NOS3; NO production is specifically dependent on the PI3K–AKT1 axis, as blocked by wortmannin, while MAPK3/1 activation may further enhance endothelial proliferation. These findings support a central role for H₂S in coordinating trophoblast–endothelial crosstalk during placental vascular development. H₂S and placental vascular dysfunction in pregnancy complications Defective H₂S signaling has been linked to impaired placental perfusion and vascular maladaptation in disorders such as PE and intrauterine growth restriction (IUGR). H₂S donors (e.g., NaHS) promote vasodilation in placental vessels through mechanisms partially dependent on K ATP channels and NO, indicating synergistic crosstalk between gasotransmitters [ 64 ]. In PE placentas with abnormal umbilical artery flow, CSE expression and H₂S production are significantly reduced, which may contribute to increased vascular resistance and trophoblast–endothelial dysfunction [ 163 , 170 ]. Additionally, microRNA-21 downregulates CSE and its transcription factor SP1, reducing H₂S biosynthesis while promoting SMC proliferation, thereby exacerbating vascular stiffness [ 171 ]. Cindrova-Davies et al. reported that loss of CBS function in animal models results in defective decidualization and implantation failure, further emphasizing the importance of H₂S at the maternal–fetal interface [ 83 , 172 ]. Through its effects on immune modulation, oxidative stress attenuation, and trophoblast invasiveness, H₂S supports both structural and functional adaptation of the uteroplacental vasculature. H₂S promotes trophoblast invasion by regulating mitochondrial dynamics The migration and invasion of CTBs are critical biological processes for SAR [ 173 ] and normal placental development [ 173 , 174 ]. Studies suggest that mitochondrial dynamics—particularly the balance between mitochondrial fusion and fission—are pivotal in maintaining CTB motility, with their disruption linked to impaired invasion and vascular remodeling [ 175 , 176 ]. In the human trophoblast cell line HTR-8/SVneo, inhibition of endogenous CBS impairs CTB invasion and migration, while the exogenous H₂S donor GYY4137 enhances CBS/H₂S mitochondrial translocation, preserves mitochondrial function, and promotes CTB motility [ 177 ]. Similar pro-invasive effects of H₂S have been observed in CBS⁺/⁻ mouse models, where it prevents early pregnancy loss and enhances CTB migration in vitro [ 24 ]. Mechanistically, H₂S mediates its effects through the persulfidation of mitochondrial Rho GTPase 2 (Miro2) at cysteine residues C185 and C504, which is essential for maintaining mitochondrial morphology and CTB invasion capacity [ 177 ]. Mutation of these sites or knockdown of Miro2 leads to mitochondrial fragmentation and impaired trophoblast migration, and notably, GYY4137 fails to rescue mitochondrial dysfunction in Miro2 mutants, confirming the critical role of Miro2 persulfidation in H₂S signaling. These findings suggest that CTB invasion is modulated by low concentrations of H₂S, with CBS/H₂S maintaining mitochondrial function through Miro2C185/C504 persulfidation, thereby increasing the CTB migration capacity. Previous research has identified mitochondrial fission regulator 1-like protein (MTFR1L) in HTR-8/SVneo cells as a protein with two distinct H₂S-mediated persulfidation sites [ 76 ]. In addition to Miro2, MTFR1L has also been identified as a downstream target of H₂S. MTFR1L is expressed in HTR-8/SVneo cells and plays a key role in mitochondrial structure maintenance [ 178 ]. Under CBS/H₂S signaling, MTFR1L undergoes persulfidation at cysteine residues C222 and C230, enhancing mitochondrial integrity and promoting CTB function. Mutation at either site disrupts mitochondrial network formation and reduces CTB invasion capacity, underscoring the functional importance of MTFR1L modification. Importantly, reduced MTFR1L expression and persulfidation levels have been observed in models of great obstetric syndrome models, such as PE and IUGR [ 178 ]. Restoration of MTFR1L levels by GYY4137 in hypoxic mouse placentas further confirms the protective role of H₂S in maintaining mitochondrial dynamics and trophoblast invasiveness. Together, these findings underscore the importance of the H₂S-CBS-mitochondria axis in CTB invasion and pregnancy-related disorders (Fig. 4 ), offering new insights for IUGR and PE intervention strategies. Fig. 4. Open in a new tab H₂S Promotes Trophoblast Invasion by Regulating Mitochondrial Dynamics. In normal pregnancy, H₂S promotes the persulfidation of MTFR1L, maintaining its stable expression and ensuring mitochondrial quantity, morphology, and function, including adequate ATP production, low ROS levels, and a high Δψm. These factors are crucial for trophoblast invasion and migration. The H₂S donor GYY4137 further enhances this regulatory effect, supporting normal placental function. Low-dose H₂S can stimulate CBS translocation to mitochondria, inhibit its degradation, and promote CBS aggregation on mitochondria, enabling Cys to be catalyzed into H₂S within mitochondria. Mitochondrial CBS/H₂S subsequently sulfhydrates Miro2-C185, promoting mitochondrial dynamics, thereby enhancing trophoblast invasion and migration. Notes: MTFR1L: Mitochondrial Fission Regulator 1-Like; ROS: Reactive Oxygen Species; Δψm: Mitochondrial membrane potential; Cys: L-cysteine Chu et al. [ 179 ] further supported these findings by demonstrating that the downregulation of CBS and CSE—but not 3-MST—may account for the reduced free H₂S production and diminished cellular H₂S levels observed in pre-eclamptic placentas. Their data also suggest that 3-MST expression in trophoblasts may be developmentally regulated across gestation. This decline in H₂S biosynthesis may exacerbate trophoblast dysfunction by promoting vasoconstriction, oxidative stress, and inflammatory responses—hallmarks of pre-eclampsia. Consistently, Du et al. showed [ 180 ] that H₂S supplementation alleviated PE symptoms in vivo by suppressing Toll-like receptor 4 (TLR4)-mediated neuroinflammation in the rostral ventrolateral medulla. H₂S regulates UA vasodilation and perfusion to support placental development Adequate uterine perfusion is a prerequisite for proper placental development and fetal growth. During pregnancy, the maternal cardiovascular system undergoes significant adaptive changes, including increased blood volume and cardiac output, systemic vasodilation, and decreased vascular resistance, whereas blood pressure remains stable or only slightly decreases [ 154 , 155 , 181 ]. These physiological changes prioritize blood flow to the uterus, significantly increasing perfusion at the maternal‒fetal interface to meet the metabolic demands of rapid placental and fetal growth. In nonpregnant sheep, reproductive organs receive only 1%−2% of cardiac output, whereas in late pregnancy, this proportion increases to 15%−20%, with over 95% of the flow directed to the uteroplacental vasculature, increasing UtBF from 10 mL/min in nonpregnant sheep to 500–800 mL/min in pregnancy [ 154 ]. This substantial increase in UtBF is crucial for maternal–fetal gas exchange (O₂/CO₂) and placental–fetal growth, making UtBF a direct determinant of pregnancy outcomes. In recent years, H₂S has emerged as a novel vasodilator, gaining increasing attention for its role in regulating UtBF during pregnancy [ 182 – 184 ]. Studies indicate that H₂S influences UAs by modulating CSE/CBS-mediated H₂S production, promoting UA relaxation, regulating potassium ion channels (particularly BK Ca channels) through S-sulfhydration, and interacting with NO and CO to maintain vascular homeostasis. The following sections will explore the molecular mechanisms underlying H₂S regulation of UA blood flow and its interactions with NO and CO (Fig. 5 ). Fig. 5. Open in a new tab H₂S-Mediated UA Vasodilation. Pregnancy enhances VEGF-induced signaling, selectively upregulating CBS without affecting cystathionine γ-lyase CSE. This promotes CBS-mediated H₂S production, which in turn enhances S-sulfhydration of L-cysteine residues, leading to BK_Ca channel activation and subsequent vascular relaxation, thereby increasing uterine arterial blood flow. Additionally, H₂S facilitates eNOS phosphorylation, augmenting NO production, which further amplifies NO-dependent vasodilation, contributing to improved uteroplacental circulation. Notably, CBS inhibition CHH completely blocks VEGF-induced H₂S production, whereas CSE inhibition BCA has no significant effect, confirming CBS as the primary source of H₂S in this process. Notes: CHH: O-(carboxymethyl)hydroxylamine hemihydrochloride; BCA: b-Cyano-L-alanine; eNOS: Endothelial Nitric Oxide Synthase Molecular mechanisms of H₂S-mediated UA relaxation H₂S acts as a potent vasodilator and proangiogenic factor, primarily synthesized in UAs via CSE and, under pregnancy or estrogen stimulation, via upregulated CBS expression [ 58 , 70 , 71 , 84 , 184 ]. In pregnant human UA endothelial cells (P-hUAECs), VEGF selectively enhances CBS-mediated H₂S production, independent of CSE, a process further amplified during pregnancy [ 58 ]. This indicates a shift toward CBS dominance in pregnancy-specific vascular adaptation. Distinct from NO and CO, H₂S induces endothelium-independent relaxation of vascular smooth muscle cells (VSMCs) by activating K ATP channels, while also enhancing NO signaling through endothelial nitric oxide synthase (eNOS) activation [ 64 , 185 ]. This dual mechanism allows H₂S to compensate for impaired endothelial function and maintain UA tone during gestation. Functional assays support this role of H₂S donors, such as GYY4137 and NaHS, induce dose-dependent relaxation in UAs of both rodents and humans, with stronger responses observed during pregnancy [ 54 , 71 , 186 ]. Notably, this vasodilatory effect is vascular bed specific, with UAs demonstrating higher sensitivity to H₂S than in mesenteric arteries [ 71 , 186 ], suggesting a specialized regulatory role of H₂S in uteroplacental hemodynamics. S-sulfhydration and potassium ion channels in H₂S-mediated UA relaxation S-sulfhydration, a key posttranslational modification by H₂S, enhances protein reactivity by converting Cys-SH groups to -SSH [ 187 , 188 ]. Bai et al. reported that the levels of S-sulfhydrated proteins in UAs from pregnant women were significantly greater than those in nonpregnant sheep women [ 51 ]. Additionally, Yadav et al. demonstrated that dithiothreitol, a thiol-reducing agent, inhibits NaHS-induced UA relaxation in rats, further supporting the essential role of S-sulfhydration in H₂S-mediated vasodilation [ 186 ]. Notably, disrupted S-sulfhydration is associated with pregnancy complications such as PE [ 52 , 158 , 189 – 191 ]. Among the downstream targets, K ATP channels are critical mediators of H₂S-induced vasorelaxation. Although systemic arteries often rely on K ATP channels activated via S-sulfhydration of the Kir6.1 pore subunit (Cys43) and the ATP-binding cassette protein SUR2B subunit (Cys24, Cys2455) [ 52 , 192 ], their role in UAs is limited [ 54 ]. Nevertheless, Bai et al. detected significantly higher S-sulfhydration levels of Kir6.1 and SUR2B in UAs from pregnant women compared to nonpregnant women [ 51 ], suggesting that S-sulfhydration may contribute to K ATP channel regulation during pregnancy, although its exact role in H₂S-mediated UA relaxation requires further investigation. Bai et al. also identified the BK Ca channel α-subunit as a novel S-sulfhydration target, with Cys710 undergoing S-sulfhydration [ 51 ], consistent with its essential role in H₂S-induced relaxation [ 54 , 186 ]. Inhibitors of BK Ca —but not K ATP or voltage-gated K⁺ channels—abolish H₂S-induced vasodilation in both rats and humans [ 54 , 186 ], and patch-clamp data confirm selective BK Ca activation by H₂S [ 54 , 186 ]. In addition to potassium channels, L-type Ca²⁺ channels are also abundantly expressed in uteroplacental smooth muscle and play a central role in regulating vascular tone, particularly under hypoxic conditions [ 193 , 194 ]. Tropea et al. reported [ 195 ] that L-type Ca²⁺ channels and extracellular signal regulated kinase (ERK1/2) signaling contribute to G1-mediated vasorelaxation in UAs. Moreover, Corcoran et al. [ 196 ] demonstrated that pre-incubation with the L-type Ca²⁺ channel agonist Bay K8644 enhances estrogen-induced vasodilation in placental arteries. While the direct interaction between H₂S and L-type Ca²⁺ channels in uteroplacental vessels has not been fully elucidated, Munaron et al. showed [ 197 ] that H₂S interferes with calcium homeostasis in neurons, cardiomyocytes, and ECs through modulation of Ca²⁺ channels, suggesting a potential cross-talk mechanism worth further investigation. Collectively, these findings highlight a pregnancy-specific mechanism in which H₂S promotes uterine vasodilation predominantly through S-sulfhydration and BK Ca channel activation, underscoring its potential as a therapeutic target in pregnancy-related vascular disorders. Interactions of H₂S, NO, and CO in UA regulation The interactions among NO, CO, and H₂S add complexity to gaseous signaling in the vascular system, particularly in the uterine vasculature. Studies indicate that these three gaseous molecules are synthesized in UA ECs and/or VSMCs, where they independently or synergistically regulate vascular tone and homeostasis [ 64 , 198 ]. During pregnancy, their interplay contributes to the dynamic vascular adaptations required for adequate placental perfusion. H₂S and NO often exhibit cooperative effects on vasodilation and angiogenesis. H₂S can enhance NO signaling by upregulating eNOS phosphorylation and NO production [ 49 , 168 ], and its vasodilatory effect is partially inhibited by NOS blockade, suggesting upstream modulation of the NO pathway [ 64 ]. However, their interaction is not always synergistic. Some studies have shown that NaHS treatment attenuates the vasodilation induced by NO donors (SNP, SIN-1, and SNAP) [ 199 ], suggesting that H₂S and NO may form products lacking vasodilatory properties. For example, NO/H₂S interactions can generate SSNO⁻, a molecule with prolonged activity [ 200 ]. In rat uterine studies, GSNO (an NO donor) had a weaker relaxant effect on uterine smooth muscle contraction than H₂S did, whereas the combination of H₂S and GSNO reduced vasodilation, even at a 1:10 ratio [ 201 ]. The interaction between H₂S and CO is less understood. H₂S may enhance CO production via heme oxygenase-1 (HO-1) upregulation under hypoxic conditions, indicating possible synergy in pulmonary vascular regulation [ 202 ]. Yet, suggesting that H₂S and CO may act synergistically in pulmonary arterial SMCs to regulate vascular tone. In contrast, in vitro studies on aortic SMCs indicate that H₂S and CO may have antagonistic effects. Inhibition of HO-1 (a CO-producing enzyme) reduces CSE expression and H₂S production, whereas CSE inhibition increases HO-1 expression and CO production. However, exogenous NaHS (100 µM) decreases HO-1 expression [ 203 ]. Moreover, CO can either stimulate or inhibit sGC depending on NO levels, futher complicating the gasotransmitter crosstalk [ 204 ]. The complex interplay among NO, CO, and H₂S in the vascular system, particularly in the uterine circulation, requires further investigation to determine their precise physiological significance in pregnancy-related vascular adaptation and placental perfusion regulation. The role of H₂S in maternal–fetal immune tolerance and oxidative stress regulation The establishment of maternal–fetal immune tolerance is essential for a successful pregnancy and relies on the coordination between trophoblasts, decidual stromal cells, and maternal immune cells [ 205 , 206 ]. A shift from a T-helper 1 (Th1)- to T-helper 2 (Th2) -dominant immune response is required to promote fetal tolerance [ 207 ]. H₂S, H₂S, mainly synthesized by CBS at the maternal–fetal interface, regulates immune homeostasis by modulating inflammatory signaling and immune cell function. H₂S inhibits the TLR4/nuclear factor-kappa B (NF-κB) pathway, reducing proinflammatory cytokines such as IL-1β, IL-6, and TNF-α [ 208 – 214 ]. Wang et al. [ 24 ] revealed that the expression of NF-κB-related genes in HTR8/SVneo cells is affected by changes in CBS levels. Additionally, H₂S promotes regulatory T cell (Treg) differentiation and maintains uNK cell populations, both essential for decidualization and SAR [ 172 , 215 ]. Beyond immune regulation, H₂S also protects against oxidative stress and ischemia-reperfusion injury by activating pro-survival pathways, such as AKT, PKC, and ERK1/2 pathway [ 216 , 217 ], enhancing eNOS-mediated NO production [ 217 ] and promoting antiapoptotic genes expression via NF-κB [ 218 ]. H₂S exerts antioxidant effects by directly scavenging reactive oxygen species and activating the nuclear factor erythroid 2-related factor 2 pathway, which upregulates antioxidant enzymes such as HO-1 and glutathione peroxidase [ 219 – 221 ]. It also supports endoplasmic reticulum stress adaptation by enhancing GSH synthesis via the transsulfuration pathway [ 222 ]. In the placenta, hypoxia/reoxygenation (H/R) stress impairs trophoblast viability, migration and invasion [ 223 ]. H₂S donors have shown protective effects against H/R injury in the heart [ 224 ], kidneys [ 225 ], and neurons [ 226 ], suggesting a potential role for H₂S in restoring trophoblast function and mitigating and pregnancy-related complications. Regulation of H₂S synthesis and function by Estrogen H₂S plays a crucial role in regulating UtBF, promoting SAR, and maintaining placental oxidative homeostasis during pregnancy. Its biosynthesis and signaling are modulated by various factors, among which estrogen is a key hormonal regulator. Studies have shown that H₂S production in UA endothelium and smooth muscle is positively correlated with endogenous estrogen levels [ 71 ]. Moreover, CBS mRNA/protein expression and H₂S production in the endometrium increase significantly during the proliferative phase and pregnancy, correlating positively with estrogen-driven angiogenesis. These findings underscore the importance of estrogen in orchestrating H₂S-mediated vascular and placental adaptations, offering insights into pregnancy physiology and potential targets for related complications. Types of estrogens and their dynamic changes during pregnancy Estrogens are steroid hormones produced primarily by the ovaries, placenta, with dynamic shifts during pregnancy. In early pregnancy, the corpus luteum remains the primary source of estrogen, but by weeks 9–10, the placenta becomes the primary source via STB, relying on androgen precursors from maternal and fetal adrenal glands [ 227 , 228 ]. Due to the absence of 17α-hydroxylase-17,20-lyase in the placenta, estrogens are synthesized through peripheral precursor aromatization [ 229 , 230 ]. : estrone (E1), estradiol (E2), estriol (E3) and estetrol (E4). E1 is mainly derived from ovaries and adipose tissue, biologically weak. E2 is the most biologically active estrogen, initially from the ovaries, later predominantly placental [ 231 , 232 ]. E3 is predominantly secreted by the placenta, with over 90% released into the maternal circulation by STBs in humans due to their haemochorial [ 233 , 234 ]. E4 is synthesized mainly by the fetal liver, unique to humans and higher primates [ 235 , 236 ]. During pregnancy, estrogen levels exhibit significant dynamic changes, particularly E2, which may reach concentrations up to 1,000 times than in the nonpregnant state [ 230 , 237 , 238 ]. These elevations accompany systemic vascular changes, including decreased resistance, increased cardiac output and blood volume, and a significant redistribution of blood flow to the uterus and placenta (15–20% of cardiac output) [ 3 , 154 , 239 ]. This estrogen-driven hemodynamic remodeling provides a favorable environment for H₂S biosynthesis and vascular actions. The complex role of Estrogen in the hemodynamic regulation of UtBF A critical function of estrogen during pregnancy is to increase UtBF and support placental perfusion [ 240 ]. UtBF exhibits a cyclical increase [ 3 , 154 , 239 ], peaking in mid- and late pregnancy, coinciding with placental formation and rapid fetal growth [ 3 ]. Moreover, circulating and/or local estrogen levels increase synchronously as UtBF peaks [ 241 – 243 ]. As early as 1932, Markee reported that estrogen induced hyperemia in uterine tissue transplanted into the eyes of monkeys [ 244 ], and later, Rosenfeld and Magness confirmed estrogen’s profound impact on reproductive tract vasculature using ovariectomized (OVX) nonpregnant and pregnant ewe models [ 245 , 246 ]. Estrogen acts on UA endothelial and SMCs via estrogen receptors (ERs) to regulate vascular tone [ 247 – 250 ]. This approach allows E₂ to influence vascular function via genomic and nongenomic mechanisms. In genomic mechanisms (ERα/ERβ), E₂ activates NOS through ERs, promoting NO synthesis [ 251 , 252 ] and inducing vasodilation [ 196 , 253 , 254 ]. Nongenomic mechanisms effects via G protein-coupled estrogen receptor (GPER) may enhance H₂S production in UAECs and UASMCs [ 71 , 184 ], contributing to smooth muscle relaxation through BK Ca channels activation, mimicking NO effects. Although NO is a major mediator of estrogen-induced vasodilation, studies show that NO inhibition accounts for only part of the effect (≈ 79% of UtBF increase; ≈26% of basal UA dilation), implying the existence of additional mediators, notably H₂S [ 47 , 251 ]. Estrogen also regulates UtBF via NO-independent pathways, including direct actions on UA SMCs to reduce myogenic tone and enhance BK Ca -mediated relaxation [ 255 – 258 ], involving signaling pathways such as ERK1/2, PKC signaling [ 259 ], and cyclic guanosine monophosphate (cGMP) [ 251 , 259 – 261 ]. Other vasodilators (prostaglandin I2 [ 262 ], CO [ 48 ], and EDHR [ 263 ]) may act synergistically with H₂S and NO. Beyond vasodilation, estrogen exerts trophic effects at the maternal-fetal interface [ 264 ]. E₂ acts on STBs, CTBs, and invasive EVTs via ERs, promoting EVT invasion, regulating trophoblast survival, differentiation, and proliferation, and facilitating SAR to enhance placental perfusion [ 265 – 267 ]. Estrogen also maintains placental microenvironment homeostasis by modulating ECM remodeling and migration [ 268 ]. Overall, estrogen increases UtBF through multiple pathways, including both NO-dependent and NO-independent mechanisms. The identification of H₂S as a novel regulatory mediator downstream of estrogen highlights its emerging role in maintaining uteroplacental hemodynamics and points to new therapeutic targets for pregnancy-related vascular disorders. Mechanisms of estrogen-mediated regulation of H₂S production H₂S is synthesized primarily by CBS, CSE, and 3-MST. Estrogen regulates the expression and activity of these enzymes through both genomic and nongenomic mechanisms, thereby increasing H₂S production (Fig. 6 ). Fig. 6. Open in a new tab Estrogen-Regulated H₂S Pathways in Uterine Vasodilation. H₂S mediates estrogen (E₂β)-induced uterine vasodilation through genomic and non-genomic mechanisms. Genomic Pathway: E₂β crosses the cell membrane and binds to ERα and ERβ, which function as nuclear TFs. Upon binding to EREs, these receptors promote CBS gene transcription. The human CBS promoter contains multiple ERE sites, including αERE (ERα binding site), three βEREs (ERβ binding sites), a full α/βERE, and a half α/βERE (½α/βERE). Among these, α/βERE2 and ½α/βERE1 are crucial for basal CBS expression and E₂-induced CBS promoter activation. ERα and ERβ can form homodimers (ERα/ERα or ERβ/ERβ) or heterodimers (ERα/ERβ), regulating H₂S production.Non-Genomic Pathway: E₂β binding to membrane-associated ERα and ERβ directly activates Gαi-2/3, which subsequently stimulates pGC-A, catalyzing GTP conversion to cGMP. cGMP then activates cGMP-dependent PKG-I, which phosphorylates CSE, enhancing H₂S production. The generated H₂S diffuses into SMCs, promoting vasodilation via K-ATP or BK-Ca channels, thereby increasing uterine arterial blood flow. Notes: EC: endothelial cell; SMC: smooth muscle cell; TFs: transcription factors; EREs: estrogen response elements; pGC-A: particulate guanylate cyclase A; PKG-I: protein kinase I ER receptor-mediated transcriptional regulation of CBS Estrogen receptors ERα and ERβ display tissue-specific expression patterns. ERα is predominantly expressed in the pituitary, kidneys, and reproductive tissues, while ERβ is more widely distributed, particularly in reproductive organs and the brain [ 269 ]. In the utetus and ovaries, both ERα and ERβ are highly expressed, reflecting the female reproductive system as a primary target of estrogen signaling [ 270 , 271 ]. UAs, comprising ECs and SMCs, express ERα, ERβ, and GPER [ 249 , 272 , 273 ]. Estrogen selectively upregulates CBS, not CSE, to increase H₂S production. In OVX nonpregnant ewes, estrogen replacement increases CBS mRNA/protein expression and H₂S levelswithout affecting CSE [ 184 ]. During the follicular phase, proliferative phase, and pregnancy, increased endogenous estrogen levels parallels elevated CBS expression and H₂S synthesis, whereas CSE and 3-MST remain unchanged [ 71 ]. In vitro studies in sheep and human UAECs and UASMCs confirme that E₂ induces CBS mRNA/protein expression [ 78 , 274 , 275 ]. Although CSE is induced by E₂ in sheep UAECs, this effect is absent in human UAECs [ 275 ]. Regardless, both exogenous and endogenous estrogen consistently promote H₂S production via CBS. In human primary UAECs, E₂ upregulates CBS and H₂S more robustly in cells derived from pregnant than nonpregnant individuals [ 275 ]. CBS is a classical estrogen-responsive gene, the transcription of which is regulated by estrogen genomic signaling. The ER antagonist ICI 182, 780 blocks E₂-induced CBS promoter activity, confirming transcriptional regulation via ERs [ 78 , 274 ]. ERα and ERβ act as nuclear transcription factors (TFs) binding to estrogen response elements (EREs) within the CBS promoter [ 248 ], which includes one αERE (binding ERα), three βEREs (binding ERβ), one full α/βERE, and one half α/βERE (½α/βERE). of these, α/βERE2 and ½α/βERE1 are essential for both basal CBS expression and E₂-stimulated CBS promoter activation [ 274 ]. UAECs and UASMCs express both ERα and ERβ, which can form homodimers (ERα/ERα or ERβ/ERβ) or heterodimers (ERα/ERβ) [ 78 ]. This effect is blocked by ERα antagonist (MPP) or ERβ antagonist (PHTPP) [ 275 ], while ERα-selective agonist (PPT) and ERβ-selective agonist (DPN) mimic E₂ action, suggesting equal regulatory potential [ 275 ]. while the CSE promoter also contains EREs, it is not transcriptionally responsive to estrogen, reinforcing CBS’s unique sensitivity to estrogenic regulation in the uterine vasculature. Additionally, the CBS promoter contains Sp1 and YY1 binding sites, and VEGF enhances CBS transcription through these TFs, promoting H₂S production [ 276 ]. ERα and ERβ can also interact with Sp1 [ 277 ], suggesting that future research should explore ER-associated TFs involved in estrogen-induced CBS transcription and H₂S production. Estrogen promotes H₂S production via nongenomic signaling Beyond transcriptional control, estrogen rapidly promote H₂S production through nongenomic signaling pathways. Estrogen binding to membrane ERs (primarily ERα/ERβ) activates Gαi-2/3 proteins, which in turn stimulate particulate guanylate cyclase A (pGC-A), converting GTP to cGMP. cGMP activates protein kinase G (PKG-I), which phosphorylates CSE, boosting enzymatic activity and H₂S synthesis [ 278 ]. The generated H₂S diffuses into SMCs, promoting vasodilation. This pathway is functionally critical, as silencing of CSE or pGC-A impairs E₂-induced vasodilation in mouse aortic experiments [ 278 ]. PKG directly phosphorylates CBS (at Ser227), further increasing its catalytic function [ 279 ]. While GPER contributes to uterine vasoregulation, its direct role in nongenomic H₂S production remains uncertain. GPER may indirectly influence H₂S via PI3K/Akt-eNOS-NO signaling cascades [ 280 ], warranting further investigation. Overall, E₂ enhances H₂S production by rapidly phosphorylating CSE through the ER-Gαi-2/3–cGMP–PKG-I pathway, whereas the direct involvement of GPER remains inconclusive. However, further studies are needed to elucidate whether GPER influences H₂S production through alternative signaling pathways. The potential role of H₂S and its donors in pregnancy-related disorders As an essential gasotransmitter, H₂S freely permeates cell membranes without requiring specific transport proteins, thereby rapidly activating biological signaling [ 281 , 282 ]. The rational design and delivery of H₂S donors are crucial for mimicking endogenous H₂S function and optimizing pharmacological effects. To date, a wide range of H₂S donors have been developed, including inorganic salts [ 283 – 285 ], organic compounds [ 286 – 288 ], slow-releasing systems [ 289 , 290 ], fluorescently labeled probes [ 291 ], and polymer-based smart delivery platforms [ 292 , 293 ]. These formulations aim to optimize H₂S release kinetics, enhance tissue targeting, and improve biocompatibility [ 294 ]. Additionally, several emerging H₂S donors, such as thiol-activated donors, metal complex-based donors, sulfur-containing amino acid derivatives, and persulfide-based donors, are being explored [ 295 ]. Despite promising in vitro and in vivo data, most H₂S donors still lack comprehensive pharmacokinetic and toxicological profiling, and their clinical translation remains limited. Research and applications of H₂S donors in pregnancy complications H₂S has been increasingly recognized as a critical regulator of pregnancy physiology, exerting vasodilatory, anti-inflammatory responses, antioxidation, and cytoprotection. However, the direct clinical application of gaseous H₂S is constrained by its volatility, toxicity, and challenges in dose control. To overcome these limitations, various H₂S-releasing compounds have been developed to enable controlled, sustained delivery of H₂S. These donors offer a promising strategy for restoring H₂S homeostasis in pregnancy-related disorders, particularly PE, FGR, and placental dysfunction. Table 1 summarizes the major types of H₂S donors and their potential applications in obstetric complications. Table 1. Effects and mechanisms of H 2 S donors in pregnancy-related disorders Disease type, H 2 S donor, Chemical type Research model Mechanism of action Key effects & findings Country Reference PE NaHS Inorganic salt (fast-releasing donor) Human placental tissue Activates K ATP channels Dose-dependent vasodilation of placental villous stromal vessels, reducing perfusion pressure and vascular resistance UK [ 163 ] Rat PE model Inhibits microglia-mediated inflammation in RVLM Alleviates PE and LPS-induced neuroinflammation and sympathetic overactivation China [ 180 ] HUVECs Reduces sFlt-1 and sEng levels Reverses anti-angiogenic effects in PE, restores tubular formation capacity of HUVECs USA [ 158 ] HTR-8/SVneo Upregulates Bcl-2 and reduces PRAP, stabilizing mitochondr