expression Rapidly alleviates inflammation, enhances immune tolerance, reduces pregnancy loss China [ 24 ] GYY4137 Organic slow-releasing donor HTR8/SVneo JEG3 Upregulates MMP-2 and VEGF expression Enhances trophoblast migration and invasion in a dose-dependent manner China [ 24 ] Pregnant mouse model Long-term inhibition of the NF-κB pathway to prevent excessive activation of COX2 and PGE2; Reduction of pro-inflammatory factors TNF-α and IFN-γ; Maintenance of Th1/Th2 balance and sustained promotion of TGF-β expression Long-term immune homeostasis maintenance, suppression of chronic inflammation, enhancement of maternal-fetal immune tolerance, and prevention of recurrent miscarriage China [ 24 ] FGR DATS Organic sulfur compound Human placental tissue Upregulates MHC, SMA, desmin, and CRBP1 Promotes SMC differentiation and stability, prevents abnormal vascular remodeling, optimizes placental blood flow in FGR UK [ 351 ] NaHS Inorganic salt (fast-releasing donor) Human placental tissue Activates K ATP channels, enhances NOS activity, promotes vasodilation Improves placental perfusion, protects fetal growth in pregnancy complications UK [ 163 ] GYY4137 Organic slow-releasing donor Pregnant rat model Reduces hypertension-induced placental oxidative stress Improves placental function, significantly increases placental and fetal weight Brazil [ 352 ] Open in a new tab H2S hydrogen sulfide, PE preeclampsia, KATP activating adenosine triphosphate (ATP)-sensitive K + channel, RVLM rostral ventrolateral medulla LPS lipopolysaccharide, HUVECs human umbilical vein endothelial cells, sFlt-1 soluble fms-like tyrosine kinase-1, sEng soluble endoglin, Bcl-2 B-cell lymphoma-2, PRAP proline-rich acidic protein, MMP matrix metalloproteinase, TIMP tissue inhibitor of metalloproteinases, H/R hypoxia/reoxygenation, MAPK mitogen-activated protein kinase, RIPA1 receptor-interacting serine/threonine-kinase-1, RIPK3 receptor-interacting serine/threonine-kinase-3, eNOS endothelial nitric oxide synthase, RI resistance index, PI pulsatility index, MTFR1L mitochondrial fission regulator 1-like protein, Miro2 mitochondrial Rho GTPase 2, GDM gestational diabetes mellitus, NLRP3 nucleotide-binding oligomerization domain-like receptor 3, UMSCs Uterine smooth muscle cells, TLR4 Toll-like receptor 4, NF-κB nuclear factor-kappa B, CAPs contraction-associated proteins, ERK1/2 extracellular signal regulated kinase, COX cyclooxygenase, Th1: T-helper 1 Th2: T- helper 2, VEGF vascular endothelial growth factor, PGE2 prostaglandin-E2, FGR fetal growth restriction, MHC myosin heavy chain, SMA smooth muscle actin, CRBP1 cellular retinol-binding protein 1 PE PE is a major hypertensive disorder of pregnancy, affecting 4–7% of pregnancies worldwide and contributing to significant maternal and perinatal morbidity and mortality worldwide [ 296 ]. Despite extensive research, effective preventive and treatment remain limited. Placental ischemia and maternal endothelial dysfunction are key pathological features of PE [ 297 , 298 ]. Recent studies have demonstrated that placental H₂S synthesis is impaired in PE, characterized by reduced expression of CBS and CSE, leading to decreased H₂S production and lower maternal plasma H₂S levels [ 158 , 163 , 164 , 177 ]. This deficiency is associated with a proantiangiogenic environment and endothelial dysfunction in PE [ 49 , 299 ]. Feng et al. [ 177 ] reported that CBS-derived H₂S maintains mitochondrial function via Miro2C185/C504 persulfidation, which is essential for CTB invasion, migration, and SAR. Wang et al. reported that the inhibition of CSE with DL-propargylglycine reduces placental PlGF expression and suppresses trophoblast invasion, whereas in human umbilical vein ECs, adenovirus-mediated CSE overexpression inhibits the release of sFlt-1 and sEng, suggesting that CSE/H₂S dysregulation may contribute to abnormal placental angiogenesis [ 158 ]. Additionally, Bai et al. reported that circulating sFlt-1 levels are significantly elevated in PE mice and that sFlt-1 suppresses VEGF-induced CBS expression, reducing H₂S synthesis [ 276 ]. Given the central role of H₂S deficiency in PE pathogenesis, exogenous H₂S supplementation has emerged as a potential therapeutic strategy. Wang et al. [ 158 ] reported that in CSE-inhibited PE mouse models, the slow-releasing H₂S donor GYY4137 reduced circulating sFlt-1/sEng levels and reversed PE-like symptoms. Similarly, Yadav et al. [ 300 ] showed that in sFlt-1-overexpressing rats, GYY4137 lowered blood pressure, improved UA blood flow, and upregulated eNOS and NO levels, partly by inhibiting adrenergic vasoconstriction. Other donors, such as MZe786 (a novel H₂S-releasing aspirin derivative), ameliorated hypertension and kidney injury in PE mice under high sFlt-1 conditions while enhancing placental antioxidant capacity [ 301 ]. Additionally, Du et al. [ 180 ] has also shown efficacy in reducing neuroinflammation via suppression of microglial activation in the rostral ventrolateral medulla, potentially regulating sympathetic overactivity in PE. In vitro and ex vivo studies further support these findings. NaHS induced dose-dependent vasodilation in human placental villous vessels, mediated primarily via K ATP channels [ 163 ]. In HTR-8/SVneo trophoblast cells under oxidative stress, NaHS restored mitochondrial function, inhibited apoptosis, and enhanced invasion by modulating Drp1-mediated fission and the B-cell lymphoma-2/Bax-caspase-3 axis [ 302 ]. GYY4137 preserved mitochondrial integrity through CBS-mediated persulfidation of Miro2 and MTFR1L, promoting trophoblast motility and SAR in hypoxia-induced models [ 177 , 178 ]. Moreover, H₂S donors may regulate necroptosis and cytoskeletal dynamics in trophoblasts. Zhang et al. [ 303 ] reported that caspase-8/3 expression was downregulated in PE placental tissue, whereas the necroptosis-related proteins receptor-interacting serine/threonine-kinase-1and receptor-interacting serine/threonine-kinase-3 were significantly upregulated. Further studies confirmed that NaHS reduced ceramide-induced trophoblast necroptosis by regulating the p38 MAPK signaling pathway, thus exerting protective effects. To investigate trophoblast migration, Gupta et al. [ 18 ] found that in PE placentas, the expression levels of H₂S-producing enzymes (CSE and CBS) were reduced, whereas the Rho family of small GTPases (Rac1 and RhoA) was downregulated. In addition, H₂S may restore the balance of MMPs and their inhibitors disrupted in PE, partly through miR-30b/CSE signaling [ 304 ]. Despite these promising results, several challenges remain. Most current studies rely on HTR-8/SVneo cell lines and animal models that may not fully reflect the complexity of human PE. The pharmacokinetics, safety profile, and optimal dosing of H₂S donors during pregnancy also require further investigation. Future work should focus on developing stable, targeted, and biocompatible H₂S-releasing platforms, along with rigorous preclinical and clinical evaluations to explore their translational potential in PE therapy. Gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) The role of H₂S in metabolic regulation has gained increasing attention. Endogenously produced by pancreatic β-cells, liver, adipose tissue, skeletal muscle, and the hypothalamus, H₂S participates in maintaining glucose homeostasis by regulating both local and systemic glucose metabolism [ 305 ]. It can inhibit insulin secretion and modulate β-cell apoptosis, with its effects depending on the metabolic microenvironment [ 306 – 310 ]. Clinical studies have shown decreased plasma H₂S levels in diabetic patients, suggesting its involvement in maintaining insulin sensitivity and β-cell function [ 311 – 313 ]. For instance, Okamoto et al. [ 314 ] found that CSE-derived H₂S protects β-cells against glucotoxicity by regulating TBP-2 (Txnip), while in CSE-deficient mice on a high-fat diet (HFD), H₂S donor NaHS reduced β-cell DNA fragmentation and TBP-2 expression. Similarly, reduced plasma H₂S levels and CSE expression were associated with insulin resistance (IR) severity in HFD-fed rats [ 315 ]. Beyond direct pancreatic effects, H₂S also modulates the gut-insulin axis. Pichette et al. [ 316 ] reported that NaHS and GYY4137 stimulated glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1) secretion in mouse L-cells (GLUTag cell line) via the p38 MAPK signaling pathway. In mouse experiments, supplementation chondroitin sulfate increased SRB (e.g., Desulfovibrio piger ), enhanced colonic H₂S production, and improved glucose tolerance through elevated GLP-1 and insulin secretion. These findings highlight the potential of H₂S donors or microbiota-targeted prebiotics as novel antidiabetic strategies. GDM, defined as glucose intolerance first diagnosed during pregnancy, is primarily driven by IR and insufficient β-cell compensation [ 317 ]. While often considered transient, GDM is a strong predictor of future type 2 diabetes mellitus and has long-term health implications for both mother and child [ 317 – 320 ]. Emerging evidence indicates that H₂S deficiency may contribute to GDM pathogenesis. Teng et al. [ 321 ] observed significantly reduced peripheral blood H₂S levels in GDM patients. Inflammatory cytokines such as IL-6 and TNF-α are elevated in GDM, correlating positively blood glucose and negatively with H₂S levels, suggesting that H₂S may modulate GDM by suppressing inflammation [ 321 , 322 ]. Moreover, placental and adipose-derived cytokines such as IL-1β and IL-18 exacerbate maternal IR [ 323 – 325 ], and the nucleotide-binding oligomerization domain-like receptor 3 (NLRP3) inflammasome plays a central role in this inflammatory response [ 326 , 327 ]. Studies by Zhang et al. [ 328 ] and Wu et al. [ 329 ] demonstrated upregulation of NLRP3 and caspase-1 in GDM placentas, along with decreased expression of H₂S-synthesizing enzymes (CSE and CBS). Wu et al. further show that NaHS or its precursor Cys inhibited NLRP3 signaling and decreased IL-1β and IL-18 production in human placental cells [ 329 ]. These findings suggest that H₂S may alleviate placental inflammation and maternal IR by modulating the NLRP3 inflammasome. The anti-inflammatory, antioxidant, and antiapoptotic properties of H₂S [ 306 , 330 ] further support its therapeutic potential in GDM. However, current research remains at an early stage. Existing studies have focused primarily on animal models or in vitro experiments, and key mechanisms—such as H₂S involvement in insulin signaling, glucose transport, gut microbiota, and placental function, require further elucidation. Notably, the placenta plays a crucial role in maternal–fetal metabolic regulation by secreting cytokines and hormones that influence IR [ 331 , 332 ]. Dysfunction placental signaling may intensify systemic inflammation and worsen metabolic homeostasis in GDM. Although H₂S is essential for placental development, its potential role in modulating early placental function during GDM pathogenesis remains largely unexplored. Further investigation into this axis could offer new insights into GDM prevention, early screening, and targeted intervention strategies. PTB Uterine quiescence is essential for maintaining pregnancy to term. H₂S production increases during pregnancy but drops sharply at labor onset [ 170 ], suggesting its pivotal role in preventing PTB [ 69 , 214 ]. Infection and inflammation are major triggers of preterm labor and H₂S has been shown to suppress uterine inflammation by inhibiting the TLR4/NF-κB pathway, thereby downregulating NLRP3 inflammasome activation, contraction-associated proteins and proinflammatory cytokines in human myometrial tissue [ 333 ]. In mouse models, NaHS delays lipopolysaccharide (LPS)-induced PTB by dampening inflammatory signaling [ 96 , 334 ]. Nonetheless, the role of H₂S in inflammation-associated PTB may vary depending on the underlying stimuli. H₂S also exerts tocolytic effects by relaxing uterine smooth muscle. In pregnant rats, both NaHS and its precursor cysteine dose-dependently inhibited spontaneous myometrial contractions [ 335 ]. Similar effects were observed in human myometrial tissue, where NaHS reduced contraction amplitude and frequency in a dose-dependent manner [ 336 ]. Robinson et al. [ 337 ] reported that both GYY4137 and NaHS significantly suppressed oxytocin- and high K⁺-induced contractions in pregnant rat and human myometrial tissue, mainly via L-type calcium and K ATP channels. Anti-inflammatory actions of H₂S further contribute to uterine quiescence. You et al. [ 214 ] demonstrated that in human uterine SMCs, both the H₂S donor NaHS and its precursor Cys inhibited NF-κB activation and proinflammatory cytokine production, thereby reducing CAP expression. Chen et al. [ 333 ] also reported that NaHS downregulated CAP expression by inhibiting the TLR4/NF-κB/NLRP3 axis. In vivo, NaHS delayed LPS-induced PTB in mice by reducing leukocyte infiltration and inhibiting ERK1/2 and NF-κB pathways [ 334 ]. Other mechanisms have also been implicated. Na₂S-induced uterine relaxation involves DIDS-sensitive Cl⁻ channels and redox- and Ca²⁺-mediated pathways [ 338 ]. A recent study discovered that ATB-346, a novel H₂S-releasing compound, exhibited a stronger uterine relaxation effect than Na₂S, naproxen, or TBZ at the same concentration did, significantly reducing the contraction amplitude and total contractile force [ 339 ]. Collectively, H₂S plays a multifaceted role in maintaining uterine quiescence by suppressing inflammation and regulating uterine smooth muscle. Donors such as NaHS, GYY4137, and ATB-346 show promise as tocolytics. However, theirpharmacokinetics, safety, and efficacy require further optimization and clinical validation. RSA RSA, defined as three or more consecutive miscarriages before 20–28 weeks of gestation [ 340 ], affects approximately 5% of reproductive-age couples worldwide [ 341 ]. Notably, about 60% of cases are classified as unexplained recurrent spontaneous abortion (URSA), with immune dysregulation at the maternal–fetal interface considered a key pathogenic factor [ 342 , 343 ]. During implantation, inflammatory cues trigger a maternal immune shift from a Th1- to a Th2-dominant state, essential for immune tolerance throughout pregnancy [ 207 , 344 ]. H₂S promotes regulatory T cells (Tregs) differentiation, thereby enhancing immune tolerance to fetal antigens [ 345 , 346 ]. Wang et al. [ 24 ] reported that placental CBS expression was significantly reduced in URSA patients compared to normal pregnancies, while CSE levels remained unchanged. In mouse models, CBS deficiency led to increased embryo resorption and a Th1/Th2 imbalance in decidual tissues. Importantly, exogenous H₂S donors (NaHS or GYY4137) partially reversed early pregnancy loss in both CBS⁺/⁻ maternal mice and the CBA/J × DBA/2 RSA model mice, suggesting therapeutic value in immune-mediated RSA. Impaired trophoblast invasion is another contributor to RSA, as failure to remodel maternal spiral arteries can lead to placental insufficiency and pregnancy failure [ 347 ]. Wang et al. [ 24 ] demonstrated that NaHS enhanced trophoblast migration and invasion in HTR8/SVneo and JEG3 cells, accompanied by increased MMP-2 and VEGF expression-effects similar to CBS overexpression. Transcriptomic analysis further revealed CBS-dependent changes in immune and inflammatory responses, including IL-1R and prostaglandin-endoperoxide synthase 2, both involved in NF-κB-mediated inflammatory signaling. These findings suggest that H₂S donors not only enhance trophoblast migration and invasion but also modulate inflammation and immune balance at the maternal–fetal interface, supporting their potential role in improving immune tolerance and reducing the risk of RSA. FGR FGR is a major contributor to adverse perinatal outcomes, including intrauterine fetal death, neonatal asphyxia, and hypoxemia. Moreover, FGR has long-term consequences, increasing the risk of cognitive developmental delays in childhood and metabolic disorders such as type 2 diabetes and cardiovascular diseases in adulthood [ 348 ]. Approximately 65–70% of FGR cases present with abnormal umbilical artery Doppler waveforms [ 349 ], reflecting placental hypoperfusion and increased placental vascular resistance, which are central to FGR pathogenesis [ 349 , 350 ]. Cindrova-Davies et al. [ 163 ] found that CSE expression in trophoblasts from FGR and PE placentas with abnormal Doppler flow, which correlated with increased placental vascular resistance, implicating CSE/H₂S dysregulation in impaired perfusion. In human placental tissues precontracted with thromboxane mimetics, NaHS induced vasodilation by activating K ATP channels and enhancing NOS activity, thus improving blood flow. Lu et al. [ 351 ] further characterized vascular remodeling in stem villous arteries of FGR placentas, noting decreased expression of contractile markers, including myosin heavy chain, smooth muscle actin, and desmin, and elevated dedifferentiation markers such as cellular retinol-binding protein 1 (CRBP1) and MMP-2. These changes were accompanied by lumen narrowing and increased wall thickness-to-lumen ratio. CSE expression positively correlated with contractile markers and birth weight, while CRBP1 and MMP-2 showed inverse correlations. In vitro, hypoxia-reoxygenation or CSE inhibition recapitulated these structural abnormalities, whereas treatment with the H₂S donor DATS prevented pathological remodeling, suggesting that H₂S supports vascular integrity by maintaining SMC differentiation. Zochio et al. [ 352 ] investigated that GYY4137, a slow-releasing H₂S donor, improved placental functionin hypertensive pregnancy models, increasing placental and fetal weights while reducing oxidative stress. These findings further support the potential of GYY4137 in placental dysfunction associated with FGR by improving placental perfusion, alleviating oxidative stress, and stabilizing the placental microenvironment. Overall, H₂S appears to be a key regulator of placental perfusion, vascular remodeling, and oxidative stress control. H₂S donors such as GYY4137 and DATS show therapeutic promise in improving placental function and promoting fetal growth. However, most findings are preclinical; robust clinical studies are needed to evaluate safety, efficacy, and optimal delivery strategies to guide potential translation into FGR therapies. Co-Therapeutic potential of H₂S donors: exploring combined treatment strategies H₂S and CO are both endogenous gaseous signaling molecules with overlapping biological activities, including vasodilation, anti-inflammation, and cytoprotection. This functional similarity has prompted the development of dual-donor compounds capable of simultaneously releasing H₂S and CO. Bai et al. first synthesized such CO-H₂S dual-donors, which exhibited enhanced bioactivity and significant anti-proliferative effects [ 353 ]. These compounds also demonstrated anti-inflammatory, antihypertensive, and cardioprotective properties. However, at higher concentrations (> 10 µmol·L⁻¹), they caused developmental malformations in zebrafish embryos, highlighting the need for careful evaluation of their pharmacological profiles and safety. The combined application of H₂S and NO donors has also attracted growing attention. NO is a well-established vasodilator that supports vascular tone, inhibits platelet aggregation, and enhances microcirculation. By combining NO and H₂S donors, researchers aim to leverage their synergistic effects, particularly in vascular protection and oxidative stress reduction during pregnancy. For example, Wang et al. [ 354 ] synthesized NOSH-NBP-5, a dual-donor compound integrating butylphthalide (a neuroprotective agent), NO and H₂S. Similarly, Kodela et al. [ 355 ] developed dual-donor aspirin derivatives (NOSH-1 to NOSH-4), which simultaneously release NO and H₂S. These compounds exhibit potent anticancer activity, particularly NOSH-1, which had an IC₅₀ of (48 ± 3) nmol·L⁻¹ against colorectal cancer cells (HT-29), which is significantly lower than that of aspirin (IC₅₀ >5 mmol·L⁻¹), indicating that the combination of NO and H₂S donors could greatly enhance the therapeutic efficacy of conventional drugs. Although current research has focused primarily on cancer, cardiovascular diseases, and neuroprotection, H₂S-NO and H₂S–CO dual donors show synergistic anti-inflammatory, antioxidant, and vasodilatory effects that could be highly relevant for pregnancy complications. These include modulation of inflammation at the maternal–fetal interface, enhancement of placental perfusion, and amelioration of vascular dysfunction in pregnancy-induced hypertension and PE. However, studies on dual gasotransmitter donors in the context of pregnancy remain scarce, and many lack comprehensive assessments of pharmacokinetics, long-term safety, and translational feasibility. Future research should focus on integrating in vivo models, ex vivo placental systems, and clinical trials to validate the therapeutic potential of H₂S-based combination strategies in maternal–fetal medicine. Conclusions H₂S, an essential endogenous gasotransmitter, plays multifaceted roles in placental development by regulating trophoblast invasion, angiogenesis, vascular remodeling, and immune homeostasis. Emerging evidence indicates that dysregulated H₂S production is closely associated with pregnancy-related complications such as PE, FGR, and PTB. Preclinical studies suggest that H₂S donors can improve placental perfusion and alleviate vascular dysfunction, highlighting their therapeutic potential. However, clinical translation remains limited by challenges in targeted delivery, dosing, and safety. Future efforts should focus on developing stable, tissue-specific H₂S-releasing compounds, clarifying molecular mechanisms, and exploring synergy with other gasotransmitters like NO and CO. Clinical trials and biomarker-driven strategies are needed to validate efficacy and enable personalized interventions. H₂S-based therapies hold promise as innovative approaches for preventing and treating pregnancy-related disorders, with further research critical to advancing their clinical application. Supplementary Information Supplementary Material 1. (15.8KB, docx) Acknowledgements Not applicable. Abbreviations UA Uterine artery SAR Spiral artery remodeling EVT Extravillous trophoblast SMC Smooth muscle cell PE Preeclampsia FGR Fetal growth restriction RSA Recurrent spontaneous abortion PTB Preterm birth H 2 S Hydrogen sulfide NO Nitric oxide CO Carbon monoxide K ATP Activating adenosine triphosphate (ATP)-sensitive K + channels BK Ca Large-conductance calcium-activated potassium channels VEGF Vascular endothelial growth factor CBS Cystathionine β-synthase CSE Cystathionine γ-lyase 3-MST 3-mercaptopyruvate sulfurtransferase UtBF Uterine blood flow PLP Pyridoxal-5’-phosphate Hcy Homocysteine Ser Serine Cth Cystathionine PlGF Placental growth factor MAPK Mitogen-activated protein kinases ONOO⁻ Peroxynitrite NO₂⁻ Nitrite 3-MP 3-mercaptopyruvate CAT Cysteine aminotransferase SSH Persulfides DAO D-amino acid oxidase Cys L-cysteine SRB Sulfate-reducing bacteria SO₄²⁻ Utilizing sulfate S²⁻ Sulfide SO₃²⁻ Sulfite S₂O₃²⁻ Thiosulfate DATS Diallyl trisulfide GSH Glutathione Fe³⁺ Ferric iron SQR Sulfide quinone oxidoreductase ETHE1 Persulfide dioxygenase SUOX Sulfite oxidase SO₄²⁻ Sulfate SO₄²⁻ Sulfate TST Thiosulfate sulfurtransferase S₂O₃²⁻ Thiosulfate CH₃SH Methanethiol S-Hb Sulf-hemoglobin HSxS⁻ Polysulfides CTBs Cytotrophoblasts STBs Syncytiotrophoblasts ECs Endothelial cells uNK Uterine natural killer MMPs Matrix metalloproteinases sGC Soluble guanylate cyclase sFlt-1 Soluble fms-like tyrosine kinase-1 sEng Soluble endoglin IUGR Intrauterine growth restriction MTFR1L Mitochondrial fission regulator 1-like protein TLR4 Toll-like receptor 4 VSMCs Vascular smooth muscle cells eNOS Endothelial nitric oxide synthase ERK1/2 Extracellular signal regulated kinase HO-1 H