INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1514,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12488208','Predictive value of fetal Doppler velocimetry, fetal growth trajectory and maternal serum biomarkers for short-term adverse perinatal outcome: secondary analysis of DRIGITAT study.','Kamphof HD, Marijnen MC, Damhuis SE, Wolf H, Gordijn SJ, Ganzevoort W; DRIGITAT Trial Group.','2025','preplanned drigitat velocimetry efw umbilicocerebral ucr emulate efw ucr efw ucr efw ucr efw ucr cerebroplacental velocimetry umbilicocerebral efw constitutionally biometric isuog efw efw velocimetry cerebroplacental pigf drigitat drigitat drigitat efw unwillingness smallness velocimetry umbilicocerebral ucr velocimetry ucr arduini cardiotocography cardiotocography efw efw velocimetry efw efw expectantly verburg efw hadlock hadlock efw efw efw efw truffle hadlock ucr velocimetry ucr ucr velocimetry velocimetry intermittently velocimetry ucr ucr ucr ucr ucr elecsys electrochemiluminescence cobas e411 leukomalacia emulate efw ucr castor drigitat nl62923 ntr6663 drigitat velocimetry ucr efw ucr occurence efw efw efw ucr ucr aredv ucr ucr velocimetry aredv ucr leukomalacia umbilicocerebral ucr efw aredv ucr velocimetry ucr velocimetry ucr ucr ucr ucr prelabor cardiotocography occurence leukomalacia leukomalacia efw ucr efw velocimetry efw efw ucr efw ucr efw efw efw ucr umbilicocerebral ucr efw efw ucr umbilicocerebral occurence drigitat efw ucr efw stampalija efw ucr efw velocimetry efw vaginally drigitat await drigitat minimalization ucr truffle efw hadlock efw biometric constitutionally biometric velocimetry ratio37 cepra velocimetry drigitat velocimetry efw ucr efw ucr drigitat flevo almere zwart deventer deventer huisjes gelre apeldoorn veerbeek diakonessenhuis laar maxima eindhoven nasiry reinier delft hermsen olvg nieuwenhof jeroen hertogenbosch sueters leeuwarden leeuwarden baalman medisch twente enschede derks sikkema ziekenhuisgroep twente almelo ikazia 18kb stichting care4neo vereniging ouders couveusekinderen drigitat zonmw zonmw unconditional kamphof kamphof umcg drigitat zwart huisjes veerbeek laar nasiry hermsen nieuwenhof sueters baalman derks sikkema jauniaux s745 s761 sovio dacey pasupathy kamphof gordijn ganzevoort crispi crovetto gratacos flenady koopmans pels knaven wijnberg djp gordijn beune thilaganathan soothill bobrow vasak koenen ganzevoort thilaganathan baschat gordijn impasse bricker medley cd001451 monier blondel ego kaminski goffinet zeitlin henrichs verfaille jellema l5517 hendrix magann scardo berghella vagaries stampalija mylrea e100 vollgraff heidweiller schreurs heymans cerebroplacental sovio goulding chappell duckworth hennessy makris smies damhuis duijnhoven leemhuis gordijn ganzevoort umbilicocerebral ucr drigitat marijnen kamphof damhuis drigitat arduini ayres spong chandraharan cardiotocography verburg steegers ridder hadlock harrist poyer hadlock harrist sharman stampalija mylrea stampalija zeisler llurba chantraine yulia e00387 verlohren herraiz lapaire gordijn ganzevoort stampalija marlow kamphof roekel henrichs gordijn ganzevoort marijnen damhuis smies gordijn ganzevoort figueras gratacos thilaganathan figueras caradeux crispi eixarch peguero gratacos s790 s802 rial crestelo lubusky cordero cerebroplacental ratio37 damhuis ganzevoort duijnhoven cerebro cepra 18kb','ABSTRACT Objectives To evaluate the predictive value of markers of placental insufficiency and fetal growth restriction for a composite adverse perinatal outcome (CAPO) in a small‐for‐gestational‐age (SGA) population. We also aimed to identify profiles that discriminate fetuses as low or high risk for CAPO, and to evaluate the association of onset of labor and mode of birth with CAPO. Methods This was a preplanned post‐hoc analysis of the DRIGITAT study, a Dutch multicenter cohort study of management strategy in 690 singleton SGA pregnancies at 32.0–36.9 weeks'' gestation, between 2018 and 2022. We used data from 440 participants with available biomarker measurements, who were not randomized for immediate birth before 36 weeks'' gestation on the basis of recurrent abnormal Doppler velocimetry. We defined CAPO as fetal death, adverse condition at birth, major neonatal morbidity and/or neonatal mortality. We analyzed the predictive value for CAPO of maternal body mass index (BMI), gestational age, estimated fetal weight (EFW) and soluble fms‐like tyrosine kinase‐1 to placental growth factor ratio (sFlt‐1/PlGF ratio) at inclusion, development of pre‐eclampsia, highest value of the umbilicocerebral ratio (UCR) and fetal growth velocity. We also used these variables to develop a prediction model for CAPO using forward stepwise logistic regression to emulate real‐world clinical evaluation. Results In this population of 440 singleton SGA pregnancies, maternal BMI at inclusion ( P = 0.02), gestational age at inclusion ( P ≤ 0.001), EFW at inclusion ( P ≤ 0.001), sFlt‐1/PlGF ratio at inclusion ( P ≤ 0.001), development of pre‐eclampsia ( P ≤ 0.001), highest value of the UCR measured at any time during pregnancy ( P ≤ 0.001) and fetal growth velocity ( P ≤ 0.001) were all associated significantly with CAPO. When combined into a prediction model using logistic regression analysis, maternal BMI at inclusion, gestational age at inclusion, development of pre‐eclampsia and fetal growth velocity did not add to the predictive value of the model, because of their correlation with other variables. The area under the receiver‐operating‐characteristics curve of the final prediction model, comprising EFW at inclusion, sFlt‐1/PlGF ratio at inclusion and the highest UCR value at any time, was 0.75 (95% CI, 0.70–0.81). At false‐positive rates of 5%, 10% and 25%, the sensitivities of the prediction model for CAPO were 35.6%, 44.2% and 63.5%, respectively. The median gestational age at birth and birth weight were lower in neonates that experienced CAPO compared with those that did not (37.0 weeks vs 38.3 weeks and 1.993 kg vs 2.518 kg, respectively). Vaginal birth occurred in 69.3% of our population. In the group that experienced CAPO, a higher number of (emergency) Cesarean sections were performed. Conclusions In a SGA population, maternal BMI, gestational age, EFW and sFlt‐1/PlGF ratio at inclusion, highest UCR at any time, development of pre‐eclampsia and fetal growth velocity were associated with CAPO. A model incorporating EFW at inclusion, sFlt‐1/PlGF ratio at inclusion and highest UCR was most effective for the prediction of CAPO. © 2025 The Author(s). Ultrasound in Obstetrics & Gynecology published by John Wiley & Sons Ltd on behalf of International Society of Ultrasound in Obstetrics and Gynecology. Keywords: cerebroplacental ratio, fetal Doppler velocimetry, fetal growth restriction, fetal growth trajectory, FGR, maternal serum biomarker, sFlt‐1/PlGF ratio, SGA, small‐for‐gestational age, umbilicocerebral ratio INTRODUCTION Placental insufficiency is the dominant pathophysiological mechanism underpinning fetal growth restriction (FGR), which describes the failure of a fetus to reach its intrinsic growth potential 1 . FGR increases both the short‐ and long‐term risks of adverse perinatal outcome, especially if it remains undetected 2 , 3 , 4 , 5 , 6 , 7 . There is no pathognomonic feature to diagnose FGR, and it has often been defined as small‐for‐gestational age (SGA), i.e. fetal abdominal circumference (AC), estimated fetal weight (EFW) or birth weight < 10 th percentile. However, some fetuses and newborns are constitutionally small, while others with measurements ≥ 10 th percentile might not have reached their intrinsic growth potential 3 , 8 , 9 , 10 . If SGA is used as a proxy for FGR in research, it is more difficult to identify associations between antenatal clinical features and adverse perinatal outcomes, because healthy SGA fetuses may obscure these associations. This may result in trials concluding that the routine use of biometric measurements alone does not reduce perinatal mortality and morbidity 2 , 11 , 12 , 13 , 14 . Another issue with using ultrasound biometry as a standalone diagnostic tool is the imprecision of these estimates 15 , 16 . In 2016, an international Delphi consensus definition for FGR was developed and later adopted by the International Society of Ultrasound in Obstetrics and Gynecology (ISUOG) and the International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIGO), aiming to better identify fetuses with FGR 8 . The definition incorporated the following markers of impaired placental function: AC and/or EFW < 10 th or < 3 rd percentile; slow fetal growth, defined as the AC or EFW crossing more than 50 percentiles from mid‐pregnancy onwards; and abnormal fetal Doppler velocimetry, defined as a cerebroplacental ratio (CPR) < 5 th percentile or umbilical artery (UA) pulsatility index (PI) > 95 th percentile 8 . Consensus was reached that additional variables, including maternal serum biomarkers, such as placental growth factor (PIGF) and soluble fms‐like tyrosine kinase‐1 (sFlt‐1), did not receive sufficient support among the expert panel to qualify as markers for FGR diagnosis 11 , 17 , 18 , 19 , 20 , 21 . We aimed to evaluate the predictive value of the above‐mentioned markers for FGR for a composite adverse perinatal outcome (CAPO) in late preterm SGA fetuses and to identify profiles that discriminate fetuses as low or high risk, using data from the DRIGITAT study 22 , 23 . METHODS Study design This was a secondary analysis of the DRIGITAT study, the study protocol, methods and results of which have been reported previously 22 , 23 . The DRIGITAT study was a Dutch multicenter cohort study of management strategy in SGA pregnancies of at least 32.0 weeks'' gestation. Singleton pregnancies between 32.0 and 36.9 weeks'' gestation with AC and/or EFW < 10 th percentile were eligible for inclusion. Exclusion criteria were maternal age < 18 years, unwillingness or inability to give informed consent, uncertain due date, chromosomal abnormality or suspicion of congenital abnormality that could influence the prognosis independent of fetal smallness 22 . The trial also included a nested randomized controlled trial (RCT) comparing immediate birth before term with expectant monitoring until early term in SGA fetuses with recurrent abnormal Doppler velocimetry measurements. For all study participants, a routine ultrasound biometry scan was performed every 10–14 days from inclusion onwards and a Doppler ultrasound scan was performed at least weekly. The Doppler scan included measurement of UA‐PI and middle cerebral artery (MCA) PI. The umbilicocerebral ratio (UCR) is the inverse of the CPR and was calculated by dividing the UA‐PI by the MCA‐PI. Abnormality of fetal Doppler velocimetry measurements was defined as UCR > 0.8 and/or UA‐PI > 90 th percentile on two consecutive occasions with an interval > 15 h. Percentiles for fetal Doppler indices were calculated according to the reference ranges of Arduini and Rizzo 24 . All pregnant women in the cohort received routine care according to local hospital protocol. Cardiotocography was performed at the discretion of the treating healthcare team. Guidance and safety criteria were provided for the timing of birth in the entire cohort and the expectant monitoring arm of the RCT. Birth was indicated in the following situations. (1) Cardiotocography was suggestive of fetal distress according to FIGO criteria 25 . (2) Doppler ultrasound examination of the UA suggested high antenatal risk: (i) reversed end‐diastolic velocity in the UA on two consecutive occasions with a significant interval (minimum of 15 h) before 32 weeks'' gestation; (ii) absent end‐diastolic velocity in the UA on two consecutive occasions with a significant interval (minimum of 15 h) before 34 weeks'' gestation; or (iii) UA‐PI > 95 th percentile with positive end‐diastolic velocity on two occasions with a significant interval (minimum of 15 h) before 37 weeks'' gestation. (3) EFW and/or AC was < 3 rd percentile at ≥ 37 weeks'' gestation. (4) EFW and/or AC was ≥ 3 rd and < 10 th percentiles at ≥ 40 weeks'' gestation. (5) The presence of other clinical signs indicating immediate birth according to local hospital protocol, such as development of hypertension, pre‐eclampsia or (repetitive) episodes of reduced fetal movements 22 . The nested RCT aimed to investigate the effects of immediate birth in the case of recurrent abnormal fetal Doppler velocimetry measurements. Women were eligible for randomization from 34.0 until 36.9 weeks'' gestation if they had abnormal fetal Doppler in combination with EFW and/or AC < 3 rd percentile, and from 36.0 until 36.9 weeks'' gestation if they had EFW and/or AC < 10 th percentile. Participants who were eligible for the RCT but declined randomization were included in the cohort and were monitored expectantly. For the present analysis, we used the data from all participants of the cohort study for whom the required measurements were available (sFlt‐1 and PlGF at inclusion were not measured in all women). We excluded data from women who were randomized for immediate birth ( n = 18) because previous analysis suggested that CAPO occurred more often in this group 23 . Diagnostic tools The AC percentile was calculated using the reference curve of Verburg et al . 26 . EFW was calculated using the AC, head circumference and femur length with the Hadlock‐3 formula, and was converted into percentiles and multiples of the median using the Hadlock fetal growth chart 27 , 28 . Fetal growth velocity was calculated by linear regression of all available EFW measurements and birth weight, with gestational age as the independent variable. As a second EFW measurement was not always available and to prevent selective censoring, we used birth weight as proxy for the last antenatal measurement of EFW. Fetal growth velocity was expressed as the coefficient of the regression in g/week. Slow fetal growth was defined as an increase in EFW of < 100 g/week. This threshold, which was also used in a secondary analysis of the TRUFFLE‐2 feasibility study 29 , was chosen because it corresponds with the 3 rd percentile of fetal growth velocity between 32 and 36 weeks'' gestation according to Hadlock et al . 28 . In many publications, the ratio of MCA‐PI to UA‐PI is presented as the CPR but, in this study, we used the UCR (i.e. its inverse). When fetal Doppler velocimetry changes become increasingly abnormal, the CPR tends towards zero while the UCR tends towards infinity. Therefore, the UCR allows for improved differentiation of values in the abnormal range 30 . We categorized patients into three Doppler subgroups using all available Doppler measurements from inclusion onwards: (1) abnormal Doppler velocimetry on two consecutive readings; (2) abnormal Doppler velocimetry at least once or intermittently (but never on consecutive readings); and (3) never had abnormal Doppler velocimetry. Although we tested the association of UCR measured at different timepoints with CAPO, we used the highest recorded value of UCR for the prediction model. The first measurement of UCR was unsuitable because the interval from measurement to birth is usually long and the UCR may worsen throughout the third trimester. Testing the predictive value of the last measurement of UCR before birth is also unsuitable to predict CAPO because it is the very measurement on which the intervention is often based. Maternal blood samples were collected at inclusion for later in‐batch biomarker analyses after study completion. sFlt‐1 and PlGF values were measured using Roche Elecsys assays on the electrochemiluminescence immunoassay platform Cobas e411 (Roche Diagnostics, Basel, Switzerland). The cut‐off for abnormal sFlt‐1/PlGF ratio was > 33, based on the value used to rule out pre‐eclampsia from 20 weeks'' gestation onwards, while the cut‐off for abnormal PlGF was ≤ 99 pg/mL 31 , 32 , 33 . Outcomes The primary outcome of the present analysis was a CAPO based on the core outcome set for growth restriction 34 . CAPO included fetal death, adverse condition at birth, major neonatal morbidity and/or neonatal mortality within 28 days postpartum. Adverse condition at birth was defined as the presence of at least one of the following signs of asphyxia: 5‐min Apgar score < 7; UA pH < 7.0 or umbilical venous pH < 7.1; or neonatal resuscitation by ventilation, cardiac massage or need for vasoactive medication. Major neonatal morbidity was defined as the presence of at least one of the following: cerebral morbidity (intracranial hemorrhage Grade ≥ 3, periventricular leukomalacia Grade ≥ 2, convulsions); respiratory morbidity necessitating any respiratory support; circulatory morbidity; sepsis; necrotizing enterocolitis; or metabolic morbidity (hypoglycemia or jaundice necessitating treatment). Secondary outcomes of the current analysis were onset of labor (spontaneous, induced or planned Cesarean section (CS)) and mode of birth (vaginal or CS). The indication for CS was classified as presumed fetal distress or any other indication. Statistical analysis Categorical variables were presented as n (%) and continuous variables were summarized as median (interquartile range (IQR)). The chi‐square test and Mann–Whitney U ‐test were used, as appropriate, to determine if there was a significant difference in the occurrence of CAPO according to baseline characteristics, diagnostic variables and birth outcomes. Baseline and diagnostic variables that differed significantly between the group with CAPO and the group without were selected as independent variables for multiple forward logistic regression. From groups of variables that were associated strongly (such as Doppler parameters that were measured at different timepoints), we selected the variable with the largest effect size. The association of multiple variables with CAPO was assessed by forward logistic regression analysis with the criterion for inclusion set as P < 0.05. The forward stepwise selection was designed to emulate real‐life clinical evaluation using the following steps: (1) adding variables determined at inclusion that were associated with CAPO (maternal body mass index (BMI), gestational age and EFW); (2) adding maternal serum biomarkers that were associated with CAPO (collected at inclusion but analyzed after study completion); and (3) adding variables determined after birth that were associated with CAPO (development of pre‐eclampsia, highest UCR at any time, fetal growth velocity). A receiver‐operating‐characteristics (ROC) curve was constructed and the area under the ROC curve (AUC) with 95% CI was calculated to quantify the predictive performance of the model. We considered AUC < 0.75 as poor discriminative capacity, AUC between 0.75 and 0.90 as fair discriminative capacity and AUC > 0.90 as good discriminative capacity. P < 0.05 was considered statistically significant. Data were collected in a web‐based registry (Castor EDC, Amsterdam, The Netherlands) and statistical analysis was conducted using the Statistical Package for Social Sciences (SPSS) (version 28.0; IBM Corp., Armonk, NY, USA). The DRIGITAT study was approved by the Amsterdam University Medical Centers institutional review board on 18 January 2018 (NL62923.018.17), which covers the present analysis. Written informed consent was obtained from all participating women. The trial was registered prospectively in the Dutch Trial Register (registration number NTR6663) on 14 August 2017 22 . RESULTS Between 1 January 2018 and 25 April 2022, 692 pregnant women with a singleton SGA pregnancy between 32.0 and 36.9 weeks'' gestation were included in the observational DRIGITAT study. Of these, 40 were included in the RCT, comprising 18 women who were randomized for immediate birth 23 . The study was halted prematurely because the inclusion rate in the RCT was too low to reach the required sample size within the planned duration of the study. The reason for the low inclusion rate was that more than half of women who were eligible for the RCT declined randomization. Analysis showed that women eligible for inclusion in the RCT had a higher rate of CAPO compared with those who were not eligible. Pregnancies that were allocated to undergo immediate birth had a significantly lower birth weight and gestational age at birth, and showed a tendency towards more frequent CAPO. For the current analysis, we used data from 440/692 (63.6%) pregnancies, of which 104 (23.6%) experienced CAPO. There were 252 exclusions due to missing biomarker, Doppler velocimetry or fetal biometry data, or because the woman had been randomized for immediate birth (Figure 1 ). Most exclusions were due to missing biomarker data. Table S1 summarizes the demographic characteristics of the patients that were excluded because of missing biomarker data compared with those of the women with biomarker data available. The parameters used in the final logistic regression model did not differ significantly between women with and those without biomarker data available, with the exception of highest UCR at any time (median, 0.67 (IQR, 0.56–0.83) vs 0.70 (IQR, 0.59–0.83); P = 0.024). Maternal BMI at inclusion and birth weight also differed significantly between the two groups. The rate of CAPO was similar in women with and those without biomarker data available (24.8% vs 22.3%; P = 0.502). Figure 1. Open in a new tab Flowchart summarizing inclusion of patients in study population. *This group showed a tendency towards a higher rate of adverse perinatal outcome compared with the expectant monitoring group. The demographic and antenatal characteristics of the pregnancies that experienced CAPO and those that did not are presented in Table 1 . Compared with the group without CAPO, mothers of babies with CAPO had a significantly higher BMI at inclusion (median, 24.2 kg/m 2 vs 22.8 kg/m 2 ; P = 0.02) and developed pre‐eclampsia more often (26.9% vs 9.5%; P ≤ 0.001). Mothers of babies with CAPO were included in the study at an earlier gestational age (median, 33.7 vs 34.2 weeks'' gestation; P ≤ 0.001) and their interval to birth was shorter. At inclusion, their fetus had a smaller AC, lower EFW, higher UA‐PI and lower MCA‐PI. The incidence of UCR > 0.8 at inclusion was higher in the CAPO group (33.0% vs 14.5%; P ≤ 0.001). Table 1. Demographic and antenatal characteristics of 440 singleton pregnancies with small‐for‐gestational‐age fetus, overall and according to occurence of composite adverse perinatal outcome (CAPO) Characteristic CAPO ( n = 104) No CAPO ( n = 336) Total ( n = 440) At inclusion Maternal age (years) 31.0 (28.0–35.0) 31.0 (28.0–34.0) 31.0 (28.0–34.0) Nulliparous 67 (64.4) 182 (54.2) 249 (56.6) Smoker > 20 weeks'' gestation 5/90 (5.6) 16/293 (5.5) 21/383 (5.5) Maternal BMI (kg/m 2 ) * 24.2 (21.6–26.8) 22.8 (20.8–26.4) 23.2 (20.9–26.5) Pre‐existing hypertension 4 (3.8) 8 (2.4) 12 (2.7) Gestational diabetes 8 (7.7) 15 (4.5) 23 (5.2) GA (weeks) * 33.7 (32.5–34.7) 34.2 (33.0–35.6) 34.0 (32.9–35.4) Interval to birth (days) * 17.0 (7.0–32.7) 28.0 (16.0–37.0) 26.0 (14.0–36.0) Interval to birth < 7 days * 21 (20.2) 21 (6.3) 42 (9.5) AC (mm) * 261.0 (248.0–274.8) 270.0 (259.3–281.0) 268.0 (258.0–280.0) AC < 3 rd percentile * 16 (15.4) 15 (4.5) 31 (7.0) EFW (kg) * 1.595 (1.336–1.775) 1.756 (1.571–1.977) 1.712 (1.532–1.935) EFW MoM * 0.74 (0.68–0.80) 0.78 (0.74–0.81) 0.77 (0.73–0.80) EFW < 3 rd percentile * 67 (64.4) 131 (39.0) 198 (45.0) UA‐PI * 1.12 (0.94–1.33) 1.00 (0.89–1.17) 1.03 (0.90–1.21) UA‐PI > 90 th percentile * 23 (22.1) 32 (9.5) 55 (12.5) MCA‐PI * , † 1.52 (1.27–1.96) 1.75 (1.49–2.01) 1.73 (1.44–2.00) MCA‐PI < 5 th percentile * 37/103 (35.9) 52/325 (16.0) 89/428 (20.8) UCR * , † 0.69 (0.56–0.94) 0.56 (0.47–0.69) 0.60 (0.48–0.74) UCR > 0.8 * 34/103 (33.0) 47/325 (14.5) 81/428 (18.9) AREDV 0 (0) 0 (0) 0 (0) Biomarkers sFlt‐1 (pg/mL) * 5262 (2637–10 535) 3037 (1758–5778) 3319 (1979–6580) PlGF (pg/mL) * 80.0 (36.3–212.0) 197.0 (91.3–402.5) 165.5 (7