eight (EFW) measurements from inclusion, including birth weight. ¶ Before 39.0 weeks'' gestation. AC, abdominal circumference; AREDV, absent or reversed end‐diastolic velocity; BMI, body mass index; GA, gestational age; MCA, middle cerebral artery; MoM, multiples of the median; PlGF, placental growth factor; sFlt‐1, soluble fms‐like tyrosine kinase‐1. The highest UA‐PI reading at any time was > 90 th percentile in 74/440 (16.8%) participants and the highest UCR reading at any time was > 0.8 in 146/440 (33.2%) participants. Those participants who experienced CAPO were significantly more likely to exhibit abnormal Doppler velocimetry measurements (UA‐PI > 90 th percentile and/or UCR > 0.8) on two consecutive occasions (34.6% vs 13.4%; P ≤ 0.001). Participants who did not experience CAPO were significantly more likely to never exhibit abnormal Doppler velocimetry measurements (68.8% vs 43.3%; P ≤ 0.001). The highest UCR at any time during the study was significantly higher in those who experienced CAPO compared with those who did not (median, 0.84 vs 0.66; P ≤ 0.001). The corresponding values of the CPR were 1.19 and 1.52. The same pattern was observed for the last UCR measured before birth (but no later than at 39.0 weeks'' gestation), with a median of 0.76 for UCR and 1.32 for CPR in pregnancies with CAPO vs 0.61 for UCR and 1.64 for CPR in those without ( P ≤ 0.001). Fetuses that experienced CAPO had a lower median growth velocity (114.2 g/week vs 154.9 g/week; P ≤ 0.001). The categorical threshold of 100 g/week identified 18.0% (79/440) of the total study population as having slow growth velocity. In the CAPO group, 32.7% (34/104) had slow fetal growth. Finally, at inclusion, mothers in the CAPO group had a significantly higher level of sFlt‐1 (median, 5262 pg/mL vs 3037 pg/mL; P ≤ 0.001), a significantly lower level of PlGF (median, 80.0 pg/mL vs 197.0 pg/mL; P ≤ 0.001) and a significantly higher sFlt‐1/PlGF ratio (median, 71.1 vs 13.8; P ≤ 0.001). Birth data are shown in Table 2 . Overall, 305 (69.3%) women had a vaginal birth and 135 (30.7%) underwent CS. CS before labor and CS for presumed fetal distress were associated significantly with CAPO. In each group, 25 neonates were delivered by prelabor CS (24.0% with vs 7.4% without CAPO). In total, 53 emergency CS were performed for presumed fetal distress, of which 31 (58.5%) experienced CAPO. Presumed fetal distress was determined by clinicians and predominantly based on abnormal or non‐reassuring cardiotocography results. Table 2. Birth characteristics of 440 singleton pregnancies with small‐for‐gestational‐age fetus, overall and according to occurence of composite adverse neonatal outcome (CAPO) Characteristic CAPO ( n = 104) No CAPO ( n = 336) Total ( n = 440) Vaginal birth 44 (42.3) 261 (77.7) 305 (69.3) Spontaneous onset of labor * 8 (7.7) 74 (22.0) 82 (18.6) Induction of labor * 36 (34.6) 187 (55.7) 223 (50.7) Operative vaginal birth for presumed fetal distress 6 (5.8) 7 (2.1) 13 (3.0) † Cesarean section 60 (57.7) 75 (22.3) 135 (30.7) Spontaneous onset of labor 5 (4.8) 7 (2.1) 12 (2.7) Induction of labor * 30 (28.8) 43 (12.8) 73 (16.6) No labor * 25 (24.0) 25 (7.4) 50 (11.4) Emergency Cesarean for presumed fetal distress * 31 (29.8) 22 (6.5) 53 (12.0) GA at birth (weeks) * 37.0 (35.3–38.0) 38.3 (37.3–39.1) 38.0 (37.0–39.0) Birth weight (kg) * 1.993 (1.671–2.350) 2.518 (2.219–2.847) 2.428 (2.016–2.750) Birth weight MoM * 0.685 (0.610–0.760) 0.770 (0.700–0.828) 0.775 (0.673–0.820) Birth‐weight percentile * 0.6 (0.1–2.8) 3.5 (0.8–8.2) 2.5 (0.5–7.3) Male sex 52 (50.0) 134 (39.9) 186 (42.3) Congenital abnormality 3 (2.9) 5 (1.5) 8 (1.8) Open in a new tab Data are given as n (%) or median (interquartile range). CAPO was defined as presence of at least one of: fetal death; 5‐min Apgar score < 7; umbilical artery pH < 7.0 or umbilical venous pH < 7.1; neonatal resuscitation by ventilation, cardiac massage or need for vasoactive medication; cerebral morbidity (intracranial hem