summarized in Table 4 . For EFW, fetal growth velocity, Doppler velocimetry measurements and biomarkers, the single variable with the largest effect size was selected. In the first step of the regression analysis, all three variables available at inclusion (maternal BMI, gestational age and EFW at inclusion) added to the predictive value of the model, which had an AUC of 0.694 (95% CI, 0.633–0.754). When the sFlt‐1/PlGF ratio at inclusion was added to the model in the second step, the P ‐value for maternal BMI was ≥ 0.05, so this variable was no longer included in the model. The AUC of the model that included gestational age, EFW and the sFlt‐1/PlGF ratio at inclusion was 0.723 (95% CI, 0.663–0.783). In the final step of the forward stepwise selection process, development of pre‐eclampsia, highest UCR at any time and fetal growth velocity were added. Maternal BMI and gestational age at inclusion, fetal growth velocity and the development of pre‐eclampsia did not add predictive value to the model ( P ≥ 0.05) so were not included. The AUC of the final model, comprising EFW and sFlt‐1/PlGF ratio at inclusion and highest UCR at any time, was 0.754 (95% CI, 0.700–0.808). At false‐positive rates of 5%, 10% and 25%, the sensitivities of the final prediction model for CAPO were 35.6%, 44.2% and 63.5%, respectively. The correlation between the sFlt‐1/PlGF ratio and the development of pre‐eclampsia was 0.5 (Pearson''s coefficient). The odds ratios for the variables included in the final logistic regression model are displayed in Figure 2 . Table 4. Odds ratios for composite adverse perinatal outcome, calculated by forward logistic regression analysis Model Odds ratio (95% CI) P AUC (95% CI) Forward regression Step 1 * 0.694 (0.633–0.754) Maternal BMI at inclusion (in kg/m 2 , per 10‐unit increase) 1.734 (1.042–2.887) 0.034 GA at inclusion (in weeks) 1.393 (1.069–1.816) 0.014 EFW at inclusion (in kg) 0.033 (0.009–0.117) < 0.001 Forward regression Step 2 † 0.723 (0.663–0.783) GA at inclusion (in weeks) 1.371 (1.045–1.799) 0.023 EFW at inclusion (in kg) 0.048 (0.013–0.180) < 0.001 sFlt‐1/PlGF ratio at inclusion 1.003 (1.002–1.005) < 0.001 Maternal BMI at inclusion (in kg/m 2 , per 10‐unit increase) ‡ — ≥ 0.05 Forward regression Step 3 § 0.754 (0.700–0.808) EFW at inclusion (in kg) 0.275 (0.114–0.661) 0.004 sFlt‐1/PlGF ratio at inclusion 1.002 (1.001–1.004) 0.006 Highest UCR at any time 5.735 (2.301–14.292) < 0.001 Maternal BMI at inclusion (in kg/m 2 , per 10‐unit increase) ‡ — ≥ 0.05 GA at inclusion (in weeks) ‡ — ≥ 0.05 Fetal growth velocity (in g/week) ‡ — ≥ 0.05 Pre‐eclampsia ‡ — ≥ 0.05 Open in a new tab P ‐value for inclusion was set at < 0.05. * Including only variables available at inclusion. † Including variables from forward regression Step 1 plus biomarkers at inclusion. ‡ Not included in model because P ≥ 0.05. § Including variables from forward regression Step 2 plus highest umbilicocerebral ratio (UCR) at any time, fetal growth velocity and pre‐eclampsia. AUC, area under receiver‐operating‐characteristics curve; BMI, body mass index; EFW, estimated fetal weight; GA, gestational age; PlGF, placental growth factor; sFlt‐1, soluble fms‐like tyrosine kinase‐1. Figure 2. Open in a new tab Forest plot showing odds ratios for variables included in final logistic regression model for composite adverse perinatal outcome. EFW, estimated fetal weight; sFlt‐1, soluble fms‐like tyrosine‐kinase‐1; PlGF, placental growth factor; UCR, umbilicocerebral ratio. Table 5 shows the associations of sFlt‐1, PlGF and their ratio with the occurrence of pre‐eclampsia and with CAPO. All measures were significantly more abnormal in SGA pregnancies with pre‐eclampsia compared to SGA pregnancies without pre‐eclampsia. Among women without pre‐eclampsia, the biomarker measures were significantly more abnormal in those with CAPO compared to those without CAPO, whereas this was not the case in women with pre‐eclampsia. Abnormal sFlt‐1 levels had a stronger association with pre‐eclampsia than with CAPO. PlGF and the sFlt‐1/PlGF ratio were associated with both pre‐eclampsia and CAPO. Table 5. Maternal biomarker levels in 440 singleton pregnancies with small‐for‐gestational‐age fetus, overall and according to occurence of composite adverse perinatal outcome (CAPO) and pre‐eclampsia Parameter CAPO No CAPO Total Effect size Pre‐eclampsia n (%) 28 (46.7) 32 (53.3) 60 (100) — sFlt‐1 (pg/mL) 12 893 (7846–20 323) 12 427 (8158–15 450) 12 753 (8158–17 067) 0.029 PlGF (pg/mL) 42 (29–68) 62 (35–92) 53 (32–78) 0.075 sFlt‐1/PlGF ratio 266 (142–642) 198 (117–353) 224 (127–464) 0.079 No pre‐eclampsia n (%) 76 (20.0) 304 (80.0) 380 (100) — sFlt‐1 (pg/mL) 3973 (2390–6323) 2767 (1681–4366) 2975 (1801–4838) 0.186 * PlGF (pg/mL) 140 (47–334) 219 (108–428) 201 (93–403) 0.120 * sFlt‐1/PlGF ratio 27 (8–131) 11 (4–41) 13 (5–54) 0.207 * Open in a new tab Data are given as median (interquartile range), unless specified otherwise. All biomarker measures differed significantly between women with and those without pre‐eclampsia ( P < 0.05). * P < 0.05 for comparison between CAPO and no‐CAPO groups. PlGF, placental growth factor; sFlt‐1, soluble fms‐like tyrosine kinase‐1. DISCUSSION In this post‐hoc analysis of the DRIGITAT study, we developed a prediction model for CAPO using EFW at inclusion, sFlt‐1/PlGF ratio at inclusion and the highest value of the UCR measured during pregnancy, which achieved an AUC of 0.75. Although maternal BMI at inclusion, gestational age at inclusion, fetal growth velocity and development of pre‐eclampsia were associated with CAPO, they did not add predictive value to our final model because of their high correlation with other variables. Because the sFlt‐1/PlGF ratio is predictive of pre‐eclampsia and elevated values are observed before the development of pre‐eclampsia, the sFlt‐1/PlGF ratio remained in the model whereas pre‐eclampsia was rejected. Hence, although the sFlt‐1/PlGF ratio is associated strongly with pre‐eclampsia, the sFlt‐1/PlGF ratio might be useful for outcome prediction because it is an earlier sign of imminent perinatal risk. Slow fetal growth did not add to the predictive value of our model because of its association with EFW at inclusion. The observational study of Stampalija et al . had similar inclusion criteria to our study and defined CAPO similarly 35 . Gestational age at inclusion was similar in both studies (34 weeks), but EFW at inclusion was lower in our study (median, 1.712 (IQR, 1.532–1.935) kg vs 1.894 (IQR, 1.624–2.145) kg) and UCR was higher (median, 0.60 (IQR, 0.48–0.74) vs 0.56 (IQR, 0.47–0.69)). Probably as a result of these differences, the rate of CAPO was higher in our study (24% vs 11%). In both studies, gestational age and EFW at inclusion and fetal Doppler velocimetry measurements were the most relevant predictive parameters for CAPO. We found an association between a higher maternal BMI and CAPO, although maternal BMI did not demonstrate evidence of an effect on CAPO when adjusted for other predictive variables. Of note, only nine women in our population had BMI > 35 kg/m 2 . Obesity might increase the risk of CAPO, but our subgroup was too small to conduct a subgroup analysis to evaluate this hypothesis. In clinical practice, slow fetal growth, usually defined as the crossing of > 20 or > 50 percentiles, is a common tool to screen for FGR 8 . We found that slow fetal growth was associated with CAPO, but its discriminative ability was limited. These findings are in line with those of previous research in low‐risk populations, which found that slow fetal growth is associated only with adverse neonatal outcome if the fetus is small (AC and/or EFW < 10 th percentile) 2 , 36 . We conclude that the observation of slow fetal growth alone does not provide sufficient basis to justify intervention. In our high‐risk population, 69.3% of neonates were born vaginally. The proportion of neonates in the CAPO group born by CS was 57.7%. In this group, CS was planned or performed more frequently both before and during labor. Neonates who experienced CAPO were born earlier and had a lower birth weight and birth‐weight percentile. Therefore, it cannot be ruled out that some cases of CAPO were also caused by (relative) prematurity resulting from early intervention 37 . Strengths of the current study are the large sample size and the use of data collected in the context of a prospective multicenter cohort study. Differences in selection and management between the participating hospitals might have affected outcomes. However, the advantage of this potential variability is that combining data from different hospitals could improve the generalizability of our findings compared with a single‐center study. Our study also has some limitations. The DRIGITAT study was not designed to develop a prediction model for CAPO 22 , 23 . Therefore, these findings should be considered hypothesis‐generating, and clinical implementation should await external validation and refinement in other datasets. However, the data are valuable in informing clinicians today about the independent contribution of parameters to individual risk. Women who were enrolled in the DRIGITAT RCT and randomized for immediate birth were excluded from the present cohort because the results of the RCT showed a tendency towards a higher rate of CAPO in this group. This might be the result of the catch‐22 between the common complications that are related to prematurity but also to hypoxia. It is difficult to draw conclusions about the cause of the higher observed rate of CAPO, as it may relate to more severe FGR but also to prematurity. Although women who were randomized for immediate birth were excluded, there was still an intervention bias. In the cohort and the expectant monitoring group, the ‘care as usual’ protocol also aimed for the minimalization of CAPO 22 . This could explain the relatively weak association between pre‐eclampsia and CAPO and the moderate AUC of 0.75. Of note, there is reported practice variation in the ‘care as usual’ protocol between the participating hospitals 38 . For the current analysis, we excluded 214 women because of missing biomarker data, because maternal blood samples were not collected at all participating hospitals. However, the parameters used in the final logistic regression model did not differ significantly between women with and those without biomarker data, with the exception of the highest UCR at any time. We defined slow fetal growth as < 100 g/week from inclusion onwards and found that this was associated with CAPO. These results are in line with those of the TRUFFLE‐2 feasibility study, which used the same definition 29 . We could not use the more common definition of a decline in AC or EFW by > 20 or > 50 percentiles because such a definition is not relevant to SGA fetuses. Moreover, an approach based on a growth velocity Z ‐score of a population with a predetermined cut‐off for ‘slow growth’ was not appropriate for this population because we assumed that these fetuses on average grow more slowly compared with the general or a low‐risk population 36 . A growth velocity of < 100 g/week is more or less the 3 rd percentile of growth velocity according to Hadlock et al . and it is a pragmatic measure that can be implemented in a clinical setting 28 . We included birth weight as a proxy for the final EFW measurement in the calculation of growth trajectory, which is not ideal because this can only be done retrospectively. However, for some women, a second biometric measurement was not available or the two measurements were performed within a short interval. To avoid having to exclude many women and mitigate extreme fetal growth velocity outcomes due to interobserver variation between two measurements taken within a short interval, we decided to use birth weight as the final measurement. For this and other reasons, the findings of this study are exploratory only and are not intended for immediate implementation. In order to reduce perinatal morbidity and mortality due to placental insufficiency, future clinical practice and scientific research should focus on the differentiation between constitutionally small fetuses and those at increased risk due to FGR (including appropriate‐for‐gestational‐age (AGA) fetuses). In late‐onset placental insufficiency, the increasing respiratory needs of the fetus are the most imminent issue and fetal (intrapartum) demise due to hypoxia can develop rapidly 39 , 40 , 41 . Biometric measurements are ineffective as diagnostic tools in this situation because fetuses often have a relatively normal size. We conclude that adding information such as Doppler velocimetry and biomarkers contributes to the detection of placental insufficiency. This conclusion is in line with the results of the RATIO37 study, which found recently that assessment of CPR in low‐risk women from 36 weeks'' gestation onwards followed by planned birth in the case of CPR < 5 th percentile led to a significant reduction in severe neonatal morbidity compared with planned birth based on the evaluation of fetal size alone 42 . We hypothesize that this is also true in AGA fetuses, but this hypothesis needs to be tested. Analysis is awaited of the CEPRA study, which focuses on Doppler velocimetry abnormalities in AGA fetuses 43 . In the DRIGITAT study, induction of birth was postponed until 40 weeks'' gestation in the absence of indications for intervention other than SGA. We hypothesized that this was a safe management strategy as long as the fetuses were monitored with weekly Doppler velocimetry. The analysis of the safety of this management strategy is underway. In conclusion, in SGA pregnancy, maternal BMI, gestational age, EFW and sFlt‐1/PlGF ratio at inclusion, development of pre‐eclampsia, highest value of the UCR at any time during pregnancy and fetal growth velocity are associated with CAPO. A model using EFW at inclusion, sFlt‐1/PlGF at inclusion and highest UCR at any time was most effective for the prediction of CAPO, with an AUC of 0.75. DRIGITAT Trial Group J. M. Schaaf , Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, Flevo Hospital, Almere, The Netherlands M. A. de Boer , Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, Amsterdam University Medical Centers, Amsterdam, The Netherlands J. J. Zwart , Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, Deventer Hospital, Deventer, The Netherlands A. J. M. Huisjes , Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, Gelre Hospital, Apeldoorn, The Netherlands J. H. W. Veerbeek , Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, Diakonessenhuis, Utrecht, The Netherlands J. O. E. H. van Laar , Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, Máxima Medical Center, Eindhoven, The Netherlands S. Al‐Nasiry , Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, Maastricht UMC+, Maastricht, The Netherlands H. A. Bremer , Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, Reinier de Graaf Hospital, Delft, The Netherlands B. B. J. Hermsen , Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, OLVG, Amsterdam, The Netherlands H. P. van de Nieuwenhof , Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, Jeroen Bosch Hospital, ''s‐Hertogenbosch, The Netherlands M. Sueters , Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, Leiden University Medical Center, Leiden, The Netherlands D. P. van der Ham , Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, Martini Hospital, Groningen, The Netherlands M. S. Post , Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, Medical Center Leeuwarden, Leeuwarden, The Netherlands J. Baalman , Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, Medisch Spectrum Twente, Enschede, The Netherlands J. B. Derks , Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, University Medical Center Utrecht, Utrecht, The Netherlands J. M. Sikkema , Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, Ziekenhuisgroep Twente, Almelo, The Netherlands J. W. de Leeuw , Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, Ikazia Hospital, Rotterdam, The Netherlands. Supporting information Table S1 Comparison of demographic and pregnancy characteristics between patients that were excluded due to missing biomarker data and those with biomarker data available UOG-66-470-s001.docx (18KB, docx) ACKNOWLEDGMENTS We acknowledge the valuable contributions of patient organizations Hellp Stichting and Care4Neo (Vereniging van Ouders Couveusekinderen) and of the employees in the participating centers in this multicenter study. The DRIGITAT study was funded by ZonMw (The Netherlands organization for health research and development) (reference number 843002825) 22 . ZonMW provided funds to perform the study according to the predefined protocol and had no role or authority in any trial activities. Roche Diagnostics International Ltd supported the study by the unconditional in‐kind provision of sample kits for biomarker analysis. Contributor Information H. D. Kamphof, Email: h.d.kamphof@umcg.nl. the DRIGITAT Trial Group: J. M. Schaaf , M. A. de Boer , J. J. Zwart , A. J. M. Huisjes , J. H. W. Veerbeek , J. O. E. H. van Laar , S. Al‐Nasiry , H. A. Bremer , B. B. J. Hermsen , H. P. van de Nieuwenhof , M. Sueters , D. P. van der Ham , M. S. Post , J. Baalman , J. B. Derks , J. M. Sikkema , and J. W. de Leeuw DATA AVAILABILITY STATEMENT Data available on reasonable request to the author. REFERENCES 1. Burton GJ, Jauniaux E. Pathophysiology of placental‐derived fetal growth restriction. Am J Obstet Gynecol. 2018;218(2S):S745‐S761. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Sovio U, White IR, Dacey A, Pasupathy D, Smith GCS. Screening for fetal growth restriction with universal third trimester ultrasonography in nulliparous women in the pregnancy outcome prediction (POP) study: a prospective cohort study. Lancet. 2015;386(10008):2089‐2097. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Kamphof HD, Gordijn SJ, Ganzevoort W, et al. Associations of severe adverse perinatal outcomes among continuous birth weight percentiles on different birth weight charts: a secondary analysis of a cluster randomized tri