exposure to environmental chemicals can increase the risk of multiple adverse child health outcomes. However, biomonitoring data for young children remain limited. This study leveraged the nationwide Environmental influences on Child Health Outcomes (ECHO) Cohort to assess chemical exposures in 201 children aged 2–4 years between 2010 and 2021. A total of 111 analytes across multiple chemical classes were simultaneously quantified in single spot urine specimens collected from each child and their mother during pregnancy, and concentrations were compared between child and prenatal maternal samples. Among the 111 analytes, 96 were detected in at least five children and 48 analytes in over 50% of children. Thirty-four were ubiquitously detected (>90%), nine of which have not been included in U.S. national biomonitoring: benzophenone-1, triethyl phosphate, and metabolites of six phthalates and one alternative plasticizer. Concentrations of bisphenol S, three pesticide biomarkers, and two phthalate biomarkers were higher in children than in mothers, while those of triclosan and monoethyl phthalate were higher in mothers. This study reveals frequent exposure to multiple chemicals in young U.S. children, often exceeding prenatal levels. Expanded biomonitoring of emerging chemicals of concern and studies of their health effects in this vulnerable population are warranted. Keywords: early life exposure, chemicals, environmental phenols, pesticides, phthalates, predictors INTRODUCTION Prenatal and early life exposure to multiple chemicals is concerning, as these critical developmental periods are marked by a susceptibility to the detrimental effects of chemical insults, which can increase the risk of adverse health outcomes in children. 1 – 3 While substantial data on chemical exposures have been reported for pregnant women, less is known about exposures in young children. Toddlers and preschoolers are exposed to a diverse array of chemicals through multiple pathways, including ingestion of breast milk, contaminated food and water, and house dust, as well as inhalation of indoor and outdoor air and dermal contact with various consumer products. 4 Due to their distinct behavioral patterns, such as frequent hand-to-mouth activities and increased surface contacts and higher dietary intake and inhalation rates per body weight, children often have higher chemical burdens than adults. 5 – 7 Historically, there has been a significant gap in biomonitoring data for toddlers and preschoolers in the U.S. compared to children older than 6 years because toddlers and preschoolers were not included in the National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey (NHANES). 8 A pilot study of 122 children aged 3–5 years who participated in the 2013 NHANES quantified 52 urinary biomarkers, reporting ubiquitous detection (≥90% of samples) of 25 biomarkers of phthalates, phenols, parabens, polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (PAHs), and pesticides. 9 After having demonstrated the feasibility of urine collection in this young age group, NHANES recently expanded its protocols to include the collection of urine specimens from children aged 3–5 years, beginning with the 2015–2016 survey period, for the measurement of nonpersistent chemical biomarkers. 9 , 10 Nevertheless, studies investigating chemical exposures among children under 5 years of age remain relatively limited compared to those for older children. Characterizing chemical exposure levels in this vulnerable subpopulation separate from prenatal exposure levels and identifying exposure determinants are crucial for assessing associated health risks. 9 Chemical exposure patterns related to diet and consumer product use in children under 5 years of age may differ compared to those measured during pregnancy. 11 , 12 However, few studies have compared the concentrations of nonpersistent consumer product chemicals between young children and their pregnant mothers, 13 – 15 despite the importance of understanding how differential behavior patterns and distinct exposure sources and pathways can influence exposure difference between these groups. The National Institutes of Health (NIH) Environmental influences on Child Health Outcomes (ECHO) Cohort study, a longitudinal study of more than 50,000 U.S. children, represents a key opportunity to address these gaps. 1 In 2019, the ECHO Cohort and other NIH institutes established the Human Health Exposure Analysis Resource (HHEAR) to analyze environmental exposures in health studies. 16 The Wadsworth Center (WC)-HHEAR developed an analytical method capable of simultaneously measuring an array of contemporary chemicals (those currently in widespread use) and emerging chemicals of concern (replacements and/or newly identified substances with potential health risks). 17 Development of this chemical panel incorporated biomonitoring recommendations from ECHO investigators. 18 The resulting panel included not only contemporary chemicals that are prevalent and routinely measured in NHANES but also emerging chemicals that are either replacements or have not yet been measured in NHANES that exhibit a high potential for frequent human exposure and adverse health effects. 19 Utilizing this method, an ECHO pilot study quantified over 100 analytes in 171 pregnant participants, detecting 19 analytes ubiquitously, including five that had not been previously measured in NHANES biomonitoring. 19 In the present study, we applied this analytical method using an extended panel of analytes to quantify contemporary and emerging nonpersistent chemicals in the urine of ECHO children aged 2–4 years. Our objectives were to describe the detection frequencies and distributions of concentrations for these analytes. Because most children’s mothers also provided urine specimens during pregnancy that were analyzed for the same analytes, we compared analyte concentrations between the matched child and prenatal maternal spot urine samples. Additionally, we examined potential predictors of each analyte concentration in children’s urine. We hypothesized that many contemporary and emerging chemicals would be frequently detected in children’s urine and that child exposure levels would be higher than those measured in matched prenatal maternal urine samples. MATERIALS AND METHODS Study Population. The NIH-funded ECHO Cohort combines data from 69 prospective pregnancy and pediatric cohorts to examine the impacts of early environmental exposures on child health and development. 20 , 21 The goal of the current pilot study was to identify detectable chemical compounds in children’s urine specimens to (1) determine whether additional samples should be analyzed to examine associations between these chemicals and child health outcomes and (2) explore the relationships between concentrations in the samples of children and their mothers. To achieve this goal, this study used a convenience sample of childhood urine specimens. Specifically, different ECHO cohorts were given the opportunity to submit prenatal maternal specimens to be analyzed for the multiclass chemical panel. 1 Seven of these cohorts also provided available childhood urine specimens ( n = 206), collected between 0 and 5 years of age, for analysis using the same chemical panel. To focus on comparable exposure pathway profiles, we restricted the age range to 2–4 years, as children younger than 2 years may have different exposure patterns, such as greater influence from breastfeeding. After excluding a cohort from Utah due to a small sample size ( n < 5) within the target age range, we included a final sample of 201 children from six cohorts across four U.S. states (California, Georgia, New York, and Washington) ( Figure S1 ). For 180 of these children, matched prenatal spot urine samples were collected primarily during the second and third trimesters of pregnancy. After written informed consent was obtained from the participants, data on environmental exposures, health outcomes, and demographic information were collected in each cohort. This study used the chemical exposure and demographic data collected and harmonized by ECHO before February 2024. The study protocol was approved by the ECHO single Institutional Review Board (IRB) or the cohorts’ local IRBs. Chemical Analysis. Each cohort shipped child and maternal urine samples on dry ice to the WC-HHEAR laboratory, where the urine samples were analyzed for chemical panel quantification. The analytical methods, previously validated and applied for quantifying contemporary and emerging chemicals among pregnant ECHO participants, were adapted with minor modifications to accommodate an expanded panel of analytes. 17 , 19 In brief, 0.5 mL urine aliquots were spiked with 2.5 ng each of 93 deuterated internal standards ( Table S1 ). 17 For analytes without specific internal standards, an internal standard for an isomer or congener within the same class was used. The aliquots were then spiked with β -glucuronidase/arylsulfatase enzyme (2000 units; Sigma-Aldrich, St. Louis, MO) and incubated for 2 h at 37 °C, after which they were subjected to ABS Elut NEXUS solid-phase extraction (60 mg, 3 mL; Agilent Technologies, Santa Clara, CA). Subsequently, the extracts were concentrated to near dryness under a gentle nitrogen stream, reconstituted, and analyzed using high-performance liquid chromatography (SCIEX, Redwood City, CA) coupled with an AB SCIEX QTRAP 5500+ triple quadrupole mass spectrometer (Applied Biosystems, Foster City, CA). Detailed analytical protocols, including enzymatic treatment, sample extraction, optimal instrumental conditions, and quality assurance measures, are available elsewhere. 17 , 22 The analytes quantified included 111 compounds (104 individual compounds and 7 composites of coeluting chemicals) from multiple chemical classes: bactericides, benzophenones, bisphenols, parabens, fungicides and herbicides, insecticides, organophosphate esters (OPEs), phthalates and phthalate alternatives, and PAHs. Seven composites of multiple chemicals were quantified because of the inability to resolve them chromatographically and quantify them individually. Detailed information on analyte names, parent compounds, and their respective chemical abstracts service registry numbers (CASRNs) is provided in Table S1 . The limit of detection (LOD) was calculated based on the lowest point of the calibration curve with a signal-to-noise ratio of three in the sample matrix. The LOD for each analyte ranged from 0.004–0.53 ng/mL, except for phthalic acid (5 ng/mL). For quality control, each of the three batches included two reagent blanks, two matrix blanks (pooled urine), duplicate aliquots of matrix spike samples (analytes spiked into pooled urine at 10 ng/mL), along with 80 study samples. 17 The concentrations of certain analytes in the study samples were adjusted by subtracting the background concentrations measured in the reagent blanks. Additionally, three aliquots each of HHEAR urine quality control (QC) pools A and B and standard reference materials (SRMs) (SRM3672 and SRM3673; National Institute of Standards and Technology) were analyzed in every batch. 22 The HHEAR QC pools were provided by the HHEAR Laboratory Network as part of its quality assurance and quality control protocols for laboratory analyses and reporting. 16 The laboratories were blinded to the identities of the HHEAR QC urine pools, which were incorporated into analytical runs and processed as unknown samples. A coefficient of variation (CV) for each analyte was computed separately for pools A and B if detected above the LOD in at least two samples from each pool. To evaluate reproducibility, eight pairs of masked duplicate aliquots from three cohorts were also analyzed with the study samples. A relative percent difference (RPD = sample result–repeat result/[sample result + repeat result] × 100) was computed for each pair if detected above the LOD in both aliquots. Potential Predictors. All variables were collected by each cohort and harmonized across the cohorts by the ECHO Data Analysis Center at Johns Hopkins University. Specimen collection characteristics included the year and season of specimen collection and child age at specimen collection. Demographic characteristics included child sex (female, male), child race/ethnicity (Hispanic, non-Hispanic Black/multiple/other races, non-Hispanic White), maternal education (some college degree or less, bachelor’s degree, masters/professional/doctorate degree), maternal age at delivery (<30, 30–34, ≥35 years), parity (0, 1, ≥2), and child opportunity index at birth (low, medium, high). The census track-level child opportunity index measures the neighborhood environment by evaluating access to resources such as education, health, and socioeconomic conditions, which are crucial for child development. 23 For each child, the residential address closest to birth was used to compute the nationally normed percentile ranking value on a scale from 0 to 1, which was subsequently categorized into tertiles. Statistical Analysis. For both child and prenatal maternal samples, analyte concentrations below the LOD were replaced with the LOD divided by the square root of two. 24 The molar sums of benzophenones, parabens, di-2-ethylhexyl phthalate (DEHP), di(2-propylheptyl) phthalate (DPhP), di- n -octyl phthalate/di-iso-nonyl phthalate (DnOP/DiNP), and di-isononyl-cyclohexane-1,2-dicarboxylic acid (DINCH) were computed due to the common parent compounds and/or strong correlations among analytes. The molar sums were calculated by dividing the concentration of each analyte by its molecular weight and summing these values; the molar sum was then multiplied by the molecular weight of the primary analyte. The equations used to compute the molar sums are presented in Table S2 . These molar sums were used in subsequent statistical analyses to reduce multiple comparisons compared to analyzing individual analytes. To account for urinary dilution, analyte concentrations were corrected for specific gravity (SG) by multiplying concentrations by the ratio of cohort-specific median SG value minus one to the participants’ SG value minus one. 25 We calculated the detection frequency and descriptive statistics for SG-corrected concentrations of each analyte, including the geometric mean, median, minimum and maximum values, and 25th and 75th percentiles. For analytes detected in greater than 70% of children’s samples, we computed Spearman correlation coefficients among the analytes. For analytes detected in over 70% of both child and prenatal maternal samples, we performed the Wilcoxon rank-sum test to compare the SG-corrected analyte concentrations between these paired samples. To investigate potential predictors of analyte concentrations, we focused on analytes detected in over 70% of children’s samples. We estimated bivariate associations between potential predictors and SG-corrected, natural log-transformed analyte concentrations using generalized estimating equations, which allowed us to account for clustering within each cohort. We assumed an exchangeable working correlation matrix and Gaussian family distribution and presented results as beta coefficients with 95% confidence intervals. We calculated the percent change in each analyte concentration per unit increase in each potential predictor using the following equations: (exp( β )–1) × 100. As a sensitivity analysis, we conducted multivariate modeling by mutually adjusting for all potential predictors, with the exception of the child opportunity index due to its high proportion of missing observations (30%). To address missing data in other covariates (<6%), we performed multiple imputation by chained equations constructed using all covariates, analyte concentrations, and study cohort as predictors. All statistical analyses were performed using R version 4.2.2 (R Foundation for Statistical Computing, Vienna, Austria). RESULTS Demographic Characteristics. Children’s urine specimens ( n = 201) were collected from 2010 to 2021, with most children (57%) being 3 years of age at specimen collection; prenatal maternal urine specimens were collected from 2007 to 2016 ( n = 180) ( Table 1 ). The study population was racially and ethnically diverse, consisting of 41% Hispanic, 37% non-Hispanic Black/multiple/other races, and 22% non-Hispanic White participants. The majority of the children’s mothers had at least a bachelor’s degree or higher (70%) and were multiparous (76%). Table 1. Demographic and Specimen Collection Characteristics of the 201 ECHO Children a characteristics N (%) b demographic characteristics child sex female 93 (46) male 108 (54) child race/ethnicity hispanic 83 (41) non-Hispanic Black/multiple/other races c 74 (37) non-hispanic white 44 (22) maternal education (missing: n = 3) some college degree or less 60 (30) bachelor’s degree 63 (32) masters, professional, or doctorate degree 75 (38) maternal age at delivery <30 years 74 (37) 30–34 years 74 (37) ≥35 years 53 (26) parity (missing: n = 11) 0 46 (24) 1 63 (33) ≥2 81 (43) child opportunity index (missing: n = 63) low 65 (47) medium 29 (21) high 44 (32) child specimen collection characteristics ( n = 201) specimen collection year 2010–2013 71 (35) 2014–2017 72 (36) 2018–2021 58 (29) specimen collection season winter (December, January, February) 55 (27) spring (March, April, May) 41 (20) summer (June, July, August) 59 (29) autumn (September, October, November) 49 (24) child age at specimen collection 2 years 42 (21) 3 years 115 (57) 4 years 46 (23) maternal specimen collection characteristics ( n = 185) specimen collection year 2007–2009 56 (30) 2010–2013 75 (41) 2014–2016 54 (29) specimen collection season winter (December, January, February) 36 (19) spring (March, April, May) 49 (26) summer (June, July, August) 52 (28) autumn (September, October, November) 48 (26) sample collection trimester 1st <5 2nd 40 (22) 3rd <140 (76) Open in a new tab a Abbreviations: ECHO, Environmental influences on Child Health Outcomes. b Percentage was calculated without missing observations. c This category includes Black, Asian, American Indian or Alaska native, multiple, and other races. Quality Control/Quality Assurance. The SRMs contained 20 analytes, but mean recoveries were calculated for 19, excluding one analyte with a spiked level below the LOD, ranging from 70 to 129% ( Table S3 ). CVs in HHEAR QC pools, calculated for 66 analytes, ranged from 1 to 24%. CVs for matrix spike samples ranged from 2 to 17%, except for seven analytes (23–60%). Median RPDs for 74 analytes in duplicate aliquots ranged from 2 to 21%, except for 15 analytes (24–50%). Of the 39 analytes included in the statistical analysis (>70% detection), diphenyl phosphate (DPHP) was the only analyte with a CV over 15% in matrix spike samples (34%), while two analytes (DPHP and monoisopropyl phthalate [MiPP]) had median RPDs over 30% in duplicate aliquots (31–33%). Analyte Concentrations and Correlations among Children. Detection frequencies and distributions of SG-corrected urinary analyte concentrations among the 201 ECHO children are presented in Table 2 and Table S4 , and distributions of raw concentrations prior to SG correction are provided in Table S5 . Ninety-six of the 111 analytes (89 individual compounds and seven composites) were detected in at least five children ( Table 2 ). Half of them (45 individual compounds and three composites) were detected in over 50% of children, including one bactericide, three benzophenones, one bisphenol, four parabens, eight pesticide biomarkers (three fungicides/herbicides and five insecticides), three OPE biomarkers, 23 metabolites of phthalates and phthalate alternatives, and five PAH metabolites. Among the 34 analytes detected in over 90% of children, nine had not previously been included in NHANES: benzophenone-1, TEP, a DINCH metabolite (cyclohexane-1,2-dicarboxylic acid-mono (oxoisononyl) ester), a DEHP metabolite (mono-2-(carboxymethyl) hexyl phthalate), two DnOP/DiNP metabolites (mono (7-carboxyheptyl) phthalate and monohydroxy-iso-nonyl phthalate), two DPhP metabolites (mono-2-(propyl-6-carboxy-hexyl) phthalate and mono-2-(propyl-6-oxoheptyl) phthalate), and MiPP. The other 25 analytes with greater than 90% detection in our sample have been previously measured in NHANES: triclosan, benzophenone-3 (BP-3), bisphenol S (BPS), methyl-, ethyl-, and propyl paraben, two fungicide/herbicide biomarkers (pentachlorophenol [PCP] and 2,4-dichlorophenoxyacetic acid [2,4-D]), three insecticide biomarkers (N-desmethyl-acetamiprid [NDMA], 3,5,6-trichloro-2-pyridinol [TCP], and 3-phenoxybenzoic acid [3-PBA]), two OPE biomarkers (bis(1,3-dichloro-2-propyl phosphate [BDCIPP], DPHP), two DINCH metabolites (MCOCH and MHNCH), three DEHP metabolites (MEHHP, MEOHP, and MECPP), MCiOP, monoethyl phthalate (MEP), monomethyl phthalate (MMP), a composite of mono- n -butyl phthalate and monoisobutyl phthalate (MnBP/MiBP), and mono (3-carboxypropyl) phthalate (MCPP). Table 2. Distributions of Specific Gravity (SG)-corrected Urinary Analyte Concentrations among the 201 ECHO Children, Categorized by Previous Inclusion in the National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey (NHANES) Biomonitoring Program a , b not previously included in NHANES previously included in NHANES analyte class/name LOD N (%) > LOD GM P25 P75 analyte class/name LOD N (%) > LOD GM P25 P75 bactericides bactericides triclocarban (TCC) 0.01 77 (38) 0.02 <LOD 0.02 triclosan (TCS) 0.01 199 (99) 1.96 0.45 5.92 benzophenones benzophenones benzophenone-1 (BP1) 0.02 200 (99.5) 8.06 2.35 20.0 benzophenone-3 (BP3) 0.01 200 (99.5) 28.3 8.09 75.0 benzophenone-2 (BP2) 0.05 28 (14) <LOD <LOD <LOD benzophenone-6 (BP6) 0.03 28 (14) 0.06 <LOD <LOD benzophenone-8 (BP8) 0.13 126 (63) 0.30 <LOD 0.58 4-hydroxybenzophenone (OH4BP) 0.21 80 (40) 0.27 <LOD 0.43 bisphenols bisphenols bisphenol AP (BPAP) 0.05 36 (18) 0.06 <LOD <LOD bisphenol A (BPA)