esented as five molar sums (except for ∑DINCH) were detected in greater than 70% of both children’s and their mothers’ prenatal urine samples ( Table 2 and Table S6 ). Urinary concentrations of BPS, two pyrethroid biomarkers ( trans -DCCA and 3-PBA), an organophosphate insecticide biomarker (TCP), an OPE biomarker (BDCIPP), and two phthalate biomarkers (∑DPhP and MCiNP) were significantly higher in children’s samples than prenatal maternal samples ( Figure 1 ). The median ratios for analyte concentrations between child and prenatal maternal samples ranged from 1.37 (3-PBA) to 4.42 (BDCPP) ( Table S7 ). Conversely, urinary concentrations of triclosan and a phthalate (MEP) were higher in prenatal maternal samples than in children’s samples (ratio of child/mother = 0.41 and 0.69, respectively). Figure 1. Open in a new tab Comparison of specific gravity (SG)-corrected urinary analyte concentrations between children’s and prenatal maternal samples ( n = 180). Abbreviations: BDCIPP, bis(1,3-dichloro-2-propyl) phosphate; BP, benzophenone; BPS, bisphenol S; DEHP, di-2-ethylhexyl phthalate; DINCH, di-iso-nonyl-cyclohexane-1,2-dicarboxylic acid; DnOP/DiNP, di- n -octyl phthalate/di-iso-nonyl phthalate; DPhP, di(2-propylheptyl) phthalate; DPHP, diphenyl phosphate; MBzP; monobenzyl phthalate; MCiNP, monocarboxy isononyl phthalate; MEP, monoethyl phthalate; MHPP, mono-2-heptyl phthalate; MnBP/MiBP, composite of mono- n -butyl phthalate and monoisobutyl phthalate; PB, paraben; 3-PBA, 3-phenoxybenzoic acid; PYR1, 1-hydroxyoyrene; TCP, 3,5,6-trichloro-2-pyridinol; TCS, triclosan; trans -DCCA, trans -3-(2,2-dichlorovinyl)-2,2-dimethyl-cyclopropane-1-carboxylic acid. Note: Analytes detected in >70% of both child and prenatal maternal samples are presented. P -values were derived from the Wilcoxon rank-sum test. Concentration units are ng/mL, except for polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (ng/L) and molar sums (nmol/mL). Predictors of Analyte Concentrations. Year of specimen collection was a strong predictor of analyte concentrations in children’s urine samples ( Figure 2 and Table S8 ). Concentrations exhibited decreasing trends over calendar years for two environmental phenols (triclosan and ΣPB), two OPE biomarkers (BDCIPP and DPHP), all phthalate biomarkers, and PAHs (PYR1 and FLUOs). Compared to those collected in earlier years (2010–2013), samples collected in later years (2014–2021) had 68–93% lower concentrations of triclosan and parabens, 21–78% lower concentrations of phthalate biomarkers, and 25–56% lower concentrations of PAHs. In contrast, later years (2018–2021) showed increasing biomarker concentrations for several herbicides and insecticides (2,4-D, NDMA, trans -DCCA, and 3-PBA; 42–172% higher), an OPE biomarker (TEP; 256% higher), and a molar sum of metabolites for a phthalate alternative (ΣDINCH; 108% higher). Sampling season was not a strong predictor; however, ∑BP, an organophosphate insecticide biomarker (TCP), an OPE biomarker (TEP), and several phthalate biomarkers (∑DEHP, ∑DnOP/DiNP, MEP, and MiPP) showed higher concentrations in samples collected during spring than in those collected during winter. Figure 2. Open in a new tab Bivariate associations between potential predictors and specific gravity (SG)-corrected, natural log-transformed urinary analyte concentrations in 201 ECHO children. Abbreviations: BDCIPP, bis(1,3-dichloro-2-propyl) phosphate; BP, benzophenone; BPS, bisphenol S; 2,4-D, 2,4-dichlorophenoxyacetic acid; DEHP, di-2-ethylhexyl phthalate; DINCH, di-iso-nonyl-cyclohexane-1,2-dicarboxylic acid; DnOP/DiNP, di- n -octyl phthalate/di-iso-nonyl phthalate; DPhP, di(2-propylheptyl) phthalate; DPHP, diphenyl phosphate; ECHO, Environmental influences on Child Health Outcomes; FLUOs, composite of 2-, 3-, 9-hydroxyfluorene; MBzP; monobenzyl phthalate; MCiNP, monocarboxy isononyl phthalate; MEP, monoethyl phthalate; MHPP, mono-2-heptyl phthalate; MiPP, monoisopropyl phthalate; MMP, monomethyl phthalate; MnBP/MiBP, composite of mono- n -butyl phthalate and monoisobutyl phthalate; NDMA, N-desmethyl-acetamiprid; PB, paraben; NH, non-Hispanic; NHW, non-Hispanic White; OP, organophosphate; OPE, organophosphate ester; PAH, polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbon; 3-PBA, 3-phenoxybenzoic acid; PCP, pentachlorophenol; PYR1, 1-hydroxyoyrene; TCP, 3,5,6-trichloro-2-pyridinol; TCS, triclosan; TEP, triethyl phosphate; trans -DCCA, trans -3-(2,2-dichlorovinyl)-2,2-dimethyl-cyclopropane-1-carboxylic acid. Note: Numeric data regarding beta coefficients and 95% confidence intervals are presented in Table S8 . Child age at specimen collection was another strong predictor and was consistently associated with higher concentrations of almost all analytes in 2-year-old children than in 3- and/or 4-year-old children: 36–84% higher for environmental phenols, 18–40% higher for pesticide biomarkers, 30–48% for OPE biomarkers, 24–73% higher for phthalate biomarkers ( Figure 2 and Table S8 ). Similarly, children born to younger mothers (<30 years) showed 18–65% higher concentrations of BPS, ∑PB, trans -DCCA, most phthalate biomarkers, and all PAH metabolites than those born to older mothers (30–35 and/or ≥ 35 years). Firstborn children exhibited lower concentrations of triclosan (348–685%), BPS (68%), ΣPB (256–385%), all phthalate biomarkers except MCiNP (22–331%), and all PAH metabolites (68–101%) than later-born children. Non-Hispanic White children had lower concentrations of triclosan, ΣPB, MEP, and all PAH metabolites than Hispanic and non-Hispanic non-White children ( Figure 2 and Table S8 ). Conversely, non-Hispanic White children had higher concentrations of ∑BP, one herbicide biomarker (2,4-D), one organophosphate insecticide biomarker (TCP), two OPE biomarkers (TEP and DPHP), and MCiNP than Hispanic children. Children born to mothers with lower education and from low-opportunity neighborhoods showed higher concentrations of BPS, ΣPB, all pyrethroid biomarkers, several phthalate biomarkers, and all PAH metabolites, but lower ΣBP concentrations. Multivariate models adjusting for all potential predictors except for child opportunity index showed similar but attenuated associations ( Figure S3 and Table S9 ). While year of specimen collection, child age at specimen collection, child race/ethnicity, and parity were strong predictors, several associations for maternal age at delivery and maternal education became weaker ( P -values > 0.20). DISCUSSION This study quantified over 111 contemporary and emerging chemicals in the urine of 201 children aged 2–4 years from the geographically and sociodemographically diverse ECHO study population. Among 96 analytes detected in at least five children, half were observed in greater than 50% of children, including nine environmental phenols, eight pesticide biomarkers, three OPE biomarkers, 23 metabolites of phthalates and phthalate alternatives, and five PAH metabolites. Thirty-four analytes were ubiquitously detected in over 90% of children, nine of which were not previously measured in NHANES: seven environmental phenols, five pesticide biomarkers, three OPE biomarkers, 18 metabolites of phthalates and phthalate alternatives, and one PAH metabolite. Concentrations tended to be higher in child samples compared to prenatal maternal samples for several compounds, including BPS, BDCIPP, three pesticide biomarkers, and two phthalate biomarkers, but lower for two others, namely, triclosan and MEP. In both bivariate and multivariate models, calendar year and child age at specimen collection, parity, and maternal age at delivery were identified as predictors for several analytes, with most analyte concentrations being higher in firstborn children, younger children, and children born to younger mothers. Concentrations of triclosan, parabens, most phthalate biomarkers, and PAH metabolites showed decreasing trends over the specimen collection years, while those of a phthalate alternative biomarker and four pesticide biomarkers showed increasing trends. During the 2015–2016 and 2017–2018 (the most recent) NHANES survey cycles, biomarkers of nonpersistent chemicals were measured in the urine of children aged 3–5 years, including 39 of the 111 analytes quantified in this study ( Table S10 ). 10 Although the present study collected urine samples from 2010 to 2021, with many analyte concentrations appearing to decline over calendar years, the NHANES children still exhibited much higher median concentrations of 25 analytes than the children in our study. However, environmental phenol concentrations were comparable between the two study samples, except for higher triclosan in our study sample and higher bisphenol A (BPA) in the NHANES sample. On the other hand, compared to the current study, two previous studies measuring a similar analyte panel in 122 NHANES children recruited in 2013 and 571 Canadian Health Measures Survey (CHMS) children recruited from 2009 to 2011 of similar ages exhibited comparable or lower biomarker concentrations of most environmental phenols, pesticides, phthalates and phthalate alternatives, and PAHs. 9 , 26 Overall, these differences may be attributable not only to the year of specimen collection, geographical variations, and the differences in sample size but also to differences in demographic characteristics such as socioeconomic status, race and ethnicity, and parity among the study samples. While the temporal trends in this study were estimated based on single time-point chemical concentrations per child and may have been influenced by varying sample collection years for different cohorts, biomarker concentrations of triclosan, parabens, OPEs, phthalates, and PAHs exhibited decreasing trends over time. These trends align with declines in median biomarker concentrations of OPEs and phthalates observed in NHANES data for children aged 3–5 years between the 2015–2016 and 2017–2018 cycles ( Table S10 ). 10 Additionally, reductions in several chemicals likely reflect regulatory actions, such as the U.S. Food and Drug Administration’s 2016 ban on triclosan in hand and body soaps 27 and the Consumer Product Safety Commission’s restriction on specific phthalates in children’s products. 28 Furthermore, the data suggest that contemporary chemicals may have been gradually replaced by alternative compounds. For instance, in children’s samples, concentrations of many phthalate biomarkers tended to be lower in later years, while the molar sum of metabolites for the plasticizer alternative, DINCH, showed an increasing trend over time. In addition, BPA was detected in less than 50% of children, whereas its alternative, BPS, was detected in all children, although the higher LOD for BPA than for BPS may have influenced the lower detection frequency of BPA. Similarly, in prenatal maternal urine samples, the BPA alternatives BPS and bisphenol F were detected in a greater number of samples than BPA itself. Despite the short elimination half-lives of these alternatives (DINCH and BPS), which are comparable to their contemporary counterparts (high-molecular-weight phthalates and BPA), 29 , 30 their ubiquitous detection in over 97% of the children’s samples in our study warrants further investigation into the potential health impacts of the alternatives in this vulnerable population. Biomarker concentrations of one herbicide (2,4-D) and one neonicotinoid (NDMA; a metabolite of acetamiprid) and two pyrethroid insecticides ( trans -DCCA and 3-PBA) in the children’s samples tended to be higher in later years. Our findings generally align with previous biomonitoring studies conducted in North America, which have reported increasing temporal trends for these pesticide biomarkers from the 2000s to the 2010s. 31 – 34 Specifically, increasing concentrations were observed for 2,4-D 31 and 3-PBA 33 in NHANES children aged 6–19 years and for 3-PBA and cis - and trans -DCCA in the CHMS population. 34 Furthermore, U.S. surveillance data on fruits and vegetables revealed an increasing trend in acetamiprid residues, likely due to the increased use of acetamiprid as a replacement for imidacloprid. 32 Young children are more likely to consume more food and water per unit of body weight than adults. 35 Their diets often include fresh fruits and processed foods that may contain concentrated pesticide residues and tend to be less varied. 36 , 37 Given the diverse health impacts associated with pesticide exposure, further biomonitoring studies in larger samples of young children are needed to characterize recent exposure patterns and identify emerging pesticides of concern. This study identified demographic differences in analyte concentrations consistent with the previous study examining predictors of the same chemical panel in ECHO pregnant women. 19 In both pregnant mothers and children in the ECHO Cohort, concentrations of parabens and PAH metabolites were higher in Hispanic participants and those with lower educational attainment. Conversely, benzophenone concentrations were lower in these groups, likely reflecting less frequent use of sunscreens and other ultraviolet protection products. 38 We also observed similar exposure profiles among children from high-opportunity neighborhoods. In line with our findings, parabens and MEP were lower in non-Hispanic White children than Hispanic and non-Hispanic non-White children in pilot samples of NHANES preschool children, 9 and several phthalate biomarkers were higher in preschool children born to mothers with lower educational attainment in Ohio. 39 These consistent patterns suggest that race and ethnicity, individual- and neighborhood-level socioeconomic status, and living environment may influence children’s chemical exposure, primarily through differences in consumer product choices, dietary habits, home furnishings, and lifestyle practices. Notably, in our study, second- or later-born and younger children exhibited higher concentrations of most phenols, phthalate biomarkers, and PAH metabolites than first-borns and older children, respectively. These associations remained consistent after mutually adjusting for all potential predictors. A previous study reported that younger children had higher concentrations of organophosphate pesticide biomarkers than their older siblings. 40 While behaviors such as more frequent hand-to-mouth activity in younger children may explain the age-related differences, birth order-related differences may stem from more relaxed parenting behaviors and influences from older siblings. Although the sibling effect has not been widely studied in the context of chemical exposure, a recent large-scale study found that early life exposure to siblings and diverse micro-organisms can impact the postnatal environment and decrease the likelihood of developing atopic diseases. 41 Similarly, younger siblings may also have higher chemical exposure. However, we cannot exclude the possibility of confounding by unmeasured factors or chance due to the characteristics of the selected samples. Therefore, these findings should be confirmed in future studies. To our knowledge, there is a limited number of studies that have directly compared nonpersistent chemical concentrations between matched pregnant mothers and preschool-aged children. 13 – 15 In a French mother–infant cohort, pregnant mothers had higher median concentrations of triclosan, BP-3, MEP, MnBP, MiBP, MEOHP, and two DiNP metabolites but lower concentrations of BPA, BPS, parabens, two DEHP metabolites, and two DINCH metabolites compared to a subset of their 12-month-old children. 14 , 15 A Taiwanese study reported generally lower median concentrations of phthalate biomarkers in pregnant mothers than in their 2- and 5-year-old children. 13 Consistent with the findings, in our study sample, child urine samples had higher concentrations of BPS and several pesticide and phthalate biomarkers, but lower concentrations of triclosan and MEP compared to their matched prenatal maternal samples. These differences can be largely attributed to varying exposure patterns between mothers and young children. Diethyl phthalate, the parent compound of MEP, is commonly found in personal care products such as cosmetics and shampoos, 42 and triclosan is used in antibacterial products such as dish soap. 43 These products are likely used more frequently by pregnant women than young children. Conversely, higher concentrations of certain analytes in children may be explained by unique behaviors, such as hand-to-mouth activities, and higher rates of dietary intake, dust ingestion, and inhalation relative to body weight. 1 , 5 – 7 , 44 , 45 Furthermore, toxicokinetics may differ between children and adults due to physiological differences and children’s immature enzyme systems and developing clearance mechanisms. 46 – 48 Ginsberg et al. observed that clearance rates for certain chemical structures in young children increase progressively from birth to 2 years, followed by higher rates up to age 12. 46 This age-dependent variation in clearance rates likely influences differential exposure levels across children’s age groups. Finally, the time elapsed between pregnancy and childhood may introduce variability due to changes in industrial chemical use and regulatory interventions, potentially altering exposure levels over time. A major strength of this study is the simultaneous quantification in the same laboratory of an extensive chemical panel of 111 analytes from multiple chemical classes in urine samples from children aged 2–4 years across six U.S. cohorts. The chemical panel included multiple chemicals of concern, both those previously measured in NHANES and emerging chemicals of concern based on a prior ECHO review. 18 , 19 Additionally, this study incorporated matched prenatal maternal samples, enabling us to compare spot analyte concentrations between child and prenatal maternal samples. The geographically and sociodemographically diverse study population provided the opportunity to examine various potential predictors of the analyte concentrations. In particular, the long specimen collection period (2010–2021) allowed us to investigate temporal trends of both contemporary and emerging chemicals. However, several limitations should be noted. This study used a moderate sample size, which may have constrained the statistical power of the predictor analyses, suggesting that larger studies are needed to validate our findings. The small sample size within each cohort ( n = 14–74) also limited our ability to examine differences in exposure levels across geographical locations. Additionally, the selection of children’s urine specimens from six cohorts was based on convenience sampling, which introduced potential selection bias. As a result, the study children may not be broadly representative of each cohort or the general U.S. child population. For example, compared to all ECHO participants, 21 the children in our study sample were more likely to be Hispanic (41 vs 28% in the total ECHO Cohort), and mothers were more likely to have at least a bachelor’s degree (70 vs 49% in the total ECHO Cohort). These differences may limit the generalizability of our findings on child exposure levels and related predictors. Furthermore, most of the analytes quantified in this study are nonpersistent and rapidly eliminated in urine, with half-lives of hours to a few days, and thus have low to moderate reproducibility. 49 , 50 Therefore, chemical concentrations measured in a single spot urine sample represent only a snapshot of exposure and may not accurately capture a participant’s typical or long-term exposure. However, for some nonpersistent chemicals commonly found in personal care products or the home environment, a single urine sample may still provide a reasonable measure of short-term exposure. 49 , 50 Given these limitations, future larger-scale studies with repeated exposure measurements are needed to confirm our findings. Despite our comprehensive chemical panel, analytical challenges arose due to the difficulty of optimizing measurements across numerous compounds. While the CVs for the QC pools indicated good to moderate accuracy for almost all analytes, several analytes—mostly those with concentrations predominantly near the LOD—exhibited relatively higher CVs in matrix spike samples or larger RPDs in duplicate aliquots. Among the 39 analytes included in statistical analyses, only DPHP and MiPP had poor precision or reproducibility, 22 and their results should be interpreted cautiously. Additionally, for several pesticides and OPEs, parent compounds were measured instead of their metabolites due to unavailability of metabolite standards. This approach may lead to underestimation of exposure, as the parent compounds are rapidly metabolized; urinary metabolites would have provided a better estimate. Consequently, low detection or nondetection of parent compounds in urine does not necessarily imply an absence of exposure or low exposure levels. Lastly, several important potential lifestyle predictors, such as diet, consumer product use, time spent at home, and exposure to secondhand smoke, were collected for only a portion of our participants (35%) and, therefore, were not considered in this study. These lifestyle predictors are particularly useful for identifying exposure sources and pathways specific to the early life stage. These data have been collected in the ECHO Cohort; 21 thus, future ECHO studies could quantify the chemical panel in a larger number of childhood urine specimens to address the research questions. This pilot study offers a comprehensive overview of early life exposure to a wide range of environmental chemicals in U.S. preschool children. The frequent detection of emerging chemicals and replacements underscores the need for continued biomonitoring efforts in this vulnerable population. Children’s chemical exposure was often higher than their pregnant mother’s, and children had different patterns of exposure, emphasizing the importance of focusing on early childhood exposures as distinct from prenatal exposures. Given the known toxicity of many detected chemicals (e.g., BPA, several phthalates, and pesticides) and emerging evidence suggesting that some replacements may be equally or more toxic, 51 , 52 future research is warranted to explore early life exposures and their associations with health outcomes in later life. Ultimately, efforts to prioritize and reduce exposures that contribute unequally and uniquely to children’s exposures should be identified. Supplementary Material Supplementary Material NIHMS2108777-supplement-Supplementary_Material.pdf (1.6MB, pdf) Supporting Information The Supporting Information is available free of charge at https://pubs.acs.org/doi/10.1021/acs.est.4c13605 . Additional analytical results (PDF) ACKNOWLEDGMENTS The authors thank our ECHO colleagues; the medical, nursing, and program staff; and the children and families participating in the ECHO cohort. The authors thank Diana Steele Jones of the Duke Clinical Research Institute for editing assistance. The TOC Art was created with BioRender.com . The ECHO Consortium is the group of investigators working on the ECHO Cohort, an innovative and collaborative research initiative established by NIH in 2016. The overarching scientific goal of ECHO is to advance understanding of the effects of a broad array of early environmental exposures on children’s development and health outcomes with high public impact. Investigators at the ECHO Cohort Study Sites have collected information from participants to support multiple synergistic, longitudinal investigations. Members of the consortium contributed to the design of the ECHO study, and the Consortium Publication Committee provided critical review of the paper. The following are members of the ECHO Consortium: P Brian Smith (Duke Clinical Research Institute, Duke University School of Medicine), L Kristin Newby (Duke Clinical