20, 144, No. 106052. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] (48). van Donge T; Evers K; Koch G; van den Anker J; Pfister M Clinical pharmacology and pharmacometrics to better understand physiological changes during pregnancy and neonatal life. Handb Ep Pharmacol 2019, 261, 325–337. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] (49). Casas M; Basagaña X; Sakhi AK; Haug LS; Philippat C; Granum B; Manzano-Salgado CB; Brochot C; Zeman F; de Bont J Variability of urinary concentrations of non-persistent chemicals in pregnant women and school-aged children. Environ. Int 2018, 121, 561–573. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] (50). Roggeman M; Gys C; Klimowska A; Bastiaensen M; Wielgomas B; Bamai YA; Covaci A Reviewing the variability in urinary concentrations of non-persistent organic chemicals: evaluation across classes, sampling strategies and dilution corrections. Environ. Res 2022, 215, No. 114332. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] (51). Kumar R; Oke A; Rockmill B; de Cruz M; Verduzco R; Shodhan A; Woodruff-Madeira X; Abrahamsson DP; Varshavsky J; Lam J Rapid identification of reproductive toxicants among environmental chemicals using an in vivo evaluation of gametogenesis in budding yeast Saccharomyces cerevisiae. Reprod. Toxicol 2024, 128, No. 108630. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] (52). Ulaganathan G; Jiang H; Canio N; Oke A; Armstrong SS; Abrahamsson D; Varshavsky JR; Lam J; Cooper C; Robinson JF Screening and characterization of 133 physiologically-relevant Fenvironmental chemicals for reproductive toxicity. Reprod. Toxicol 2024, 126, No. 108602. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials Supplementary Material NIHMS2108777-supplement-Supplementary_Material.pdf (1.6MB, pdf) Data Availability Statement Select deidentified data from the ECHO Program are available